 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 176\n10.2 Behavior recognition . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 179\n10.2.1 Statement of the problem . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 179\n10.2.2 Bayesian program . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 179\n10.2.3 Instance and results . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 179\n10.3 Mixture of models and model recognition . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 180\nThe probability of ten consecutive heads is 0.1 percent; thus,\nwhen you have millions of coin tossers, or investors, in the end there\nwill be thousands of very successful practitioners of coin tossing,\nor stock picking.\nThe Age of Turbulence\nAlan Greenspan [2007]\nThe purpose of this chapter is to introduce probabilistic branching state-\nments. We will start by describing the probabilistic “if-then-else” statement\nwhich, as in standard programming, can naturally be extended to a proba-\nbilistic “case” statement. From an inference point of view, the probabilistic\nif-then-else statement is simply the integration over the probability distribu-\ntion on a binary variable representing the truth value of the condition used in\nthe classical “if” statement. The main difference with the classical approach\nis that the Bayesian program will explore both branches when the truth value\nof the condition is given by a probability distribution. This allows us to mix\nbehaviors and to recognize models.\n171\n172 Bayesian Programming"],[77056,"Chapter 10\nBig Data Classification\nHanghang Tong\nCity College\nCity University of New York\nNew York, New York\nhanghang.tong@gmail.com\n10.1 Introduction . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 275\n10.2 Scale-Up on a Single Machine . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 276\n10.2.1 Background . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 276\n10.2.2 SVMPerf . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 276\n10.2.3 Pegasos . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 277\n10.2.4 Bundle Methods . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 279\n10.3 Scale-Up by Parallelism . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 280\n10.3.1 Parallel Decision Trees . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 280\n10.3.2 Parallel SVMs . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 281\n10.3.3 MRM-ML . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 281\n10.3.4 SystemML . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 282\n10.4 Conclusion . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 283\nBibliography . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 283"],[63311,"Chapter 10\nChanges in the language used by experts and changes in the meaning of words. 1.\nDomain mapping. 2.\nNo; the whole communication passes through the entity, that is, the aggregate 3.\nroot.\nBecause aggregates represent part-subpart hierarchies. 4.\nJust one, since repositories are aggregate-centric. 5.\nThe application layer manipulates repository interfaces. Repository 6.\nimplementations are registered in the dependency injection engine.\nTo coordinate in single transactions operations on several aggregates. 7.\nThe specifications for updates and queries are usually quite different, especially 8.\nin simple CRUD systems. The reason for its strongest form is mainly the\noptimization of query response times.\nDependency injection. 9.\nNo; a serious impact analysis must be performed so that we can adopt it. 10."],[78617,"Chapter 10\nClustering Algorithms\nA clustering algorithm is an algorithm that assigns data points or records into\ngroups in a certain way. Over the past 50 years, many clustering algorithms\nhave been developed and applied to various areas such as gene expression\ndata analysis (Yeung et al., 2003) and psychology (Clatworthy et al., 2005).\nAlthough there are many different kinds of clustering algorithms, they have\nat least three commonalities. First, all clustering algorithms require some\narguments or parameters. Second, all clustering algorithms produce clustering\nresults. Third, all clustering algorithms perform data clustering.\nIn this chapter, we introduce the design and implementation of the base\nclass for all clustering algorithms based on the aforementioned three common-\nalities."],[29119,"Chapter 10\nData structures in C++"],[82714,"CHAPTER 10\nFloating and Shapes\nFor a very long time, floated elements were the basis of all our web layout schemes.\n(This is largely because of the property clear, which we’ll get to in a bit.) But floats\nwere never meant for layout; their use as a layout tool was a hack nearly as egregious\nas the use of tables for layout. They were just what we had.\nFloats are quite interesting and useful in their own right, however, especially given the\nrecent addition of float shaping, which allows the creation of nonrectangular shapes\npast which content can flow.\nFloating\nYou are likely acquainted with the concept of floated elements. Ever since Netscape\n1.1, it has been possible to float images by declaring, for instance, <img src=\"b5.gif\"\nalign=\"right\">. This causes an image to float to the right and allows other content\n(such as text) to “flow around” the image. The name “floating,” in fact, comes from\nthe Netscape DevEdge page “Extensions to HTML 2.0,” which stated:\nThe additions to your ALIGN options need a lot of explanation. First, the values “left”\nand “right”. Images with those alignments are an entirely new floating image type.\nIn the past, it was only possible to float images and, in some browsers, tables. CSS, on\nthe other hand, lets you float any element, from images to paragraphs to lists. In CSS,\nthis behavior is accomplished using the property float.\n489\nfloat\nValues left | right | none\nInitial value none\nApplies to All elements\nComputed value As specified\nInherited No\nAnimatable No\nFor example, to float an image to the left, you could use this markup:\n<img src=\"b4.gif\" style=\"float: left;\" alt=\"b4\">\nAs Figure 10-1 makes clear, the image “floats” to the left side of the browser window\nand the text flows around it. This is just what you should expect.\nFigure 10-1. A floating image\nHowever, when floating elements in CSS, some interesting issues come up.\nFloated Elements\nKeep a few things in mind with regard to floating elements. In the first place, a floated\nelement is, in some ways, removed from the normal flow of the document, although\nit still affects the layout. In a manner utterly unique within CSS, floated elements exist\nalmost on their own plane, yet they still have influence over the rest of the document.\nThis influence derives from the fact that when an element is floated, other content\n“flows around” it. This is familiar behavior with floated images, but the same is true if\nyou float a paragraph, for example. In Figure 10-2, you can see this effect quite clearly,\nthanks to the margin added to the floated paragraph:\np.aside {float: right; width: 15em; margin: 0 1em 1em; padding: 0.25em;\nborder: 1px solid;}\n490 | Chapter 10: Floating and Shapes\nFigure 10-2. A floating paragraph\nOne of the first interesting things to notice about floated elements is that margins"],[75050,"Chapter 10\nGalois Theory\nT he quest to find roots of polynomials over a field, such as Q or R, has a rich history,\nbeginning with Mesopotamian solutions ([25]) of quadratics and continuing to this day with\nefforts to find more efficient algorithms. It even included contests between mathematicians\nto see who could solve the most equations, and of highest degree ([25])! In 1823 Niels Abel\n(see Chapter 1) proved that there is no algebraic solution for a general polynomial of 5\nth\ndegree or higher ([25]). Then only a few years later in 1832\n´\nEvariste Galois wrote down the\nbasics of a theory that uses an interaction between groups and fields to help understand\nwhich polynomials can be solved “by radicals” ([25]). Understanding how groups become\ninvolved, called Galois Groups, is the goal of this chapter.\nThere are a few assumptions we need in place throughout the chapter. We assume that\nevery field is either finite or has characteristic 0 , which guarantees that irreducible\npolynomials are separable. Also whenever we discuss a root field, we assume it is the root\nfield for a nonconstant polynomial."],[60541,"CHAPTER 10\nImage Consolidation (for Network and\nCache Efficiencies)\nColin Bendell\nIf you’ve ever had to move from one home to another, you know that moving day is\nlong and grueling. You quickly realize that you want to minimize the number of trips\nfrom your apartment to the moving truck. If you took one small box each trip, you’d\nspend more time going back and forth than actually loading the moving truck.\nTherefore, carrying more boxes in each load reduces the number of trips back up the\nstairs and brings that much-deserved beer that much closer. At the same time, there is\na limit. Good luck trying to carry eight cartons of books in one load. An extra trip is\nbetter than a broken back.\nThis is the same challenge with loading images in a browser or app. In order to opti‐\nmize the delivery we need to address either the number of requests or the payload per\nrequest. This is particularly true for small images. A useful technique is to consolidate\nimages, thereby reducing requests and making each request more effective. This\nchapter explores how to achieve high performance for the smaller images using tech‐\nniques like spriting, web fonts, and inlining.\n157\nWhat About HTTP/2?\nMany of these solutions for small images have been cultivated in an HTTP/1.1 world.\nSome have argued that consolidation is an antipattern in an HTTP/2 world. This is\nnot the case; at worst, it will not degrade performance. However, there are many rea‐\nsons why consolidation is still relevant in an HTTP/2 Internet.\n• It will be several years before the ubiquitous adoption of HTTP/2. During this\ntime of transition, older browsers and corporate content filters (proxies) will\ncontinue to benefit from HTTP/1.1 optimizations.\n• Many images means many requests to the browser cache. This is not free. Each\ncache request requires multiple interprocess communication (IPC) calls. Use\nconsolidation to reduce the number of IPCs.\n• Consolidation increases cache hit probability. A single image that is used once\nwill be more likely to be dropped during cache eviction than if it were consolida‐\nted and sharing the cache hit rate of many requests. This also benefits images not\nyet referenced but displayed on other pages.\n• Consolidation can, in some cases, save total bytes on the network as well as in the\nbrowser cache.\nThe Problem\nJust like in our analogy of moving household goods, the browser (and apps) have two\nparticular problems:\nRound-trip time\nHow long does it take from the time the request is sent to the time the response is\nreceived? Using our analogy, how long does it take for you to leave the truck, go\nup the stairs into your apartment, and come back with a load? Do you have to\nprop open the doors, or are they open already?\nMaking every trip count\nHow do we make sure each response contains the most data? Taking one trip to"],[88258,"Chapter 10\nLighting and\nEnvironment\nI\nn terms of getting the work you create in Blender out to a finalized still image\nor animation, having your scene’s environment and lighting set up properly is\nincredibly important. It goes along hand in hand with setting up materials on\nyour object (see Chapter 8) as well as the rendering process (see Chapter 16).\nWithout light, the camera — and by extension, the renderer — can’t see a thing.\nYou could create the most awesome 3D model or animation in the world, but if it’s\npoorly lit, it won’t be turning any heads.\nThis chapter covers the types of lights available to you in Blender and details some\nof the best practices to use them in your scenes. In addition to lighting details,\nI go into setting up the environment in your scene with the settings in World\nProperties. In many ways, the topics covered in this chapter are what give your\nscenes that final polish, making them look really good.\nLighting a Scene\nLighting has an incredible amount of power to convey your scene to the viewer.\nHarsh, stark lighting can give you a dramatic film noir look. Low-angle lights\nwith long shadows can give you a creepy horror movie feeling, and brighter"],[35274,"chapter 10\nOverview and Learning Objectives\nIn This Chapter"],[73767,"Chapter 10\nPattern-Based Feature Generation\nYunzhe Jia\nSchool of Computing and Information Systems, The University of Melbourne\nJames Bailey\nSchool of Computing and Information Systems, The University of Melbourne\nRamamohanarao Kotagiri\nSchool of Computing and Information Systems, The University of Melbourne\nChristopher Leckie\nSchool of Computing and Information Systems, The University of Melbourne\n10.1 Introduction . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 246\n10.2 Preliminaries . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 247\n10.2.1 Data and Patterns . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 247\n10.2.2 Patterns for Non-Transactional Data . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 248\n10.3 Framework of Pattern-Based Feature Generation . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 251\n10.3.1 Pattern Mining . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 251\n10.3.2 Pattern Selection . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 252\n10.3.3 Feature Generation . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 253\n10.4 Pattern Mining Algorithms . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 254\n10.4.1 Frequent Pattern Mining . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 254\n10.4.2 Contrast Pattern Mining . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 256\n10.5 Pattern Selection Approaches . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 258\n10.5.1 Post-Processing Pruning . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 258\n10.5.2 In-processing Pruning . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 260\n10.6 Pattern-Based Feature Generation . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 262\n10.6.1 Unsupervised Mapping Functions . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 262\nBinary Mapping Function . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 262\n10.6.2 Supervised Mapping Functions . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 263\n10.6.3 Feature Generation for Sequence Data and Graph Data 265\n10.6.4 Comparison with Similar Techniques . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 265\n10.7 Pattern-Based Feature Generation for Classification . . . . . . . . . . . . 266\n10.7.1 Problem Statement . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 266\n10.7.2 Direct Classification in the Pattern Space . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 267\n10.7.3 Indirect Classification in the Pattern Space . . . . . . . . . . . . . 268\n10.7.4 Connection with Stacking Technique . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 269"],[68413,"Chapter 10\nPreprocessing in Big Data: New\nchallenges for discretization and\nfeature selection\nVer´ onica Bol´ on-Canedo\nNoelia S´ anchez-Maro˜ no\nAmparo Alonso-Betanzos\nIntroduction . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 286\nThe advent of Big Data . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 287\nThe need for preprocessing . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 288\nDiscretization . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 289\nDiscretization process . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 289\nPopular discretization methods . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 291\nFeature selection . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 291\nFeature selection process . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 292\nPopular feature selection methods . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 294\nChallenges . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 295\nMillions of dimensions . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 296\nStability of feature selection . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 297\nScalability . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 298\nDistributed feature selection . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 300\nReal-time processing . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 303\nVisualization and interpretability . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 305\nCase studies . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 306\nA parallel implementation of the minimum description\nlength-based discretizer . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 306\nRedesign of an algorithm for feature selection for its use in parallel\nplatforms . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 308\nOther distributed and incremental feature selection\napproaches . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 314"],[87136,"Chapter 10\nProtecting Your Data\nand Applications\nThis chapter is about protecting your data from being viewed by malicious users using\nencryption, and from being manipulated or corrupted using hashing and signing.\nIn .NET Core 2.1, Microsoft introduced new Span<T> -based cryptography APIs\nfor hashing, random number generation, asymmetric signature generation and\nprocessing, and RSA encryption. This chapter covers the following topics:\n• Understanding the vocabulary of protection\n• Encrypting and decrypting data\n• Hashing data\n• Signing data\n• Generating random numbers\n• What's new in cryptography?\n• Authenticating and authorizing users\nUnderstanding the vocabulary of\nprotection\nThere are many techniques to protect your data; below we'll briefly introduce six\nof the most popular ones and you will see more detailed explanations and practical\nimplementations throughout this chapter:\n• Encryption and decryption: These are a two-way process to convert your\ndata from clear-text into crypto-text and back again.\n• Hashes: This is a one-way process to generate a hash value to securely store\npasswords, or can be used to detect malicious changes or corruption of\nyour data.\nC# 8.0 and .NET Core 3.0 – Modern Cross-Platform Development\n[ 330 ]\n• Signatures: This technique is used to ensure that data has come from\nsomeone you trust by validating a signature that has been applied to some\ndata against someone's public key.\n• Authentication: This technique is used to identify someone by checking their\ncredentials.\n• Authorization: This technique is used to ensure that someone has permission\nto perform an action or work with some data by checking the roles or groups\nthey belong to.\nKeys and key sizes\nProtection algorithms often use a key. Keys are represented by byte arrays of varying\nsize.\nKeys can be symmetric (also known as shared or secret because the same key is used\nto encrypt and decrypt) or asymmetric (a public-private key pair where the public\nkey is used to encrypt and only the private key can be used to decrypt).\nKeys come in various byte array sizes.\nGood Practice: If security is important to you (and it should be!),\nthen hire an experienced security expert for guidance rather than\nrelying on advice found online. It is very easy to make small\nmistakes and leave your applications and data vulnerable without\nrealizing until it is too late!\nGood Practice: Choose a bigger key size for stronger protection.\nGood Practice: Symmetric key encryption algorithms are fast and\ncan encrypt large amounts of data using a stream. Asymmetric key\nencryption algorithms are slow and can only encrypt small byte\narrays.\nIn the real world, get the best of both worlds by using a symmetric\nkey to encrypt your data, and an asymmetric key to share the\nsymmetric key. This is how Secure Sockets Layer (SSL) encryption"],[23249,"CHAPTER 10\nSeeing Double\nReusing Content\nAn SVG file is a structured description of a graphic that can be\norganized into logical parts and groups. An important consequence\nof this structure is that you can reuse the same content in multiple\ncontexts without having to repeat all the information used to create\nthat graphic. Symbols, icons, and other repeated motifs can be\ndefined once, then used again and again.\nThis chapter examines the key structural SVG elements that allow\nyou to copy content defined elsewhere into your graphic.\nWhen it comes to reusing content, there are two distinct strategies\nused in SVG. On the one hand, you can reuse entire images, embed‐\nding the complete graphic in your SVG similar to how an <img> ele‐\nment embeds an image in an HTML page. On the other hand, you\ncan reuse individual SVG shapes or groups, from another file or\nanother part of the same file.\nWith the SVG <image> element, you can embed not only SVG files\nbut also raster image formats. This allows an SVG to include photos\nand other graphics that cannot effectively be represented with vector\ndrawing elements. Regardless of whether an embedded image was\noriginally SVG or not, when you embed it as an image, it is treated\nas an indivisible element. It can be manipulated with graphical\neffects, but its component parts are inaccessible to styles or scripts\nfrom the main document.\n331\nIn contrast, when you duplicate SVG content with a <use> element,\nyou duplicate the vector graphics instructions for that content. The\nduplicated elements are rendered almost as if they were cloned into\nthe markup, inheriting new styles from the context where they are\nused.\nReduce, Reuse, Recycle\nWe introduced the <use> element in Chapter 1, but did not explore\nits full potential. In that chapter, <use> elements were used to dupli‐\ncate, position, and style the circles in the stoplight graphic.\nA quick recap of the basics:\n• The <use> element allows you to duplicate SVG graphics\nwithout repeating the complete markup.\n• The original copy of the graphic is identified with an id value,\nand the <use> element references it with an xlink:href\nattribute, where xlink is the standard prefix for the namespace\nhttp://www.w3.org/1999/xlink.\n• To prevent the original copy from being drawn directly, you can\nput it inside a <defs> element, indicating that it is a definition\nfor future reuse.\n• You can position the duplicated graphics using x and y\nattributes on the <use> element.\n• The duplicated graphics inherit style properties from the <use>\nelement.\nWe’ve also hinted a few times that the element you’re reusing doesn’t\nhave to be in the same file. You can use a URL to reference another\nfile, and then a target fragment to reference the element ID, like\n<use xlink:href=\"icons.svg#shape\" />. But there are limita‐"],[27570,"Chapter 10\nSentiment Analysis of Stock Market\nBehavior from Twitter Using the R Tool\nNuno Oliveira\nALGORITMI Research Centre, University of Minho, Guimar˜ aes, Portugal\nPaulo Cortez\nALGORITMI Research Centre/Department of Information Systems, University of Minho,\nGuimar˜ aes, Portugal\nNelson Areal\nDepartment of Management, University of Minho, Braga, Portugal\nAbstract . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 223\n10.1 Introduction . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 223\n10.2 Methods . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 225\n10.3 Data Collection . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 225\n10.4 Data Preprocessing . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 227\n10.5 Sentiment Analysis . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 230\n10.6 Evaluation . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 233\n10.7 Inclusion of Emoticons and Hashtags Features . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 236\n10.8 Conclusion . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 238\nBibliography . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 238\nAbstract\nSentiment analysis is a hot research topic with widespread applications in organizations.\nRecently, it has been applied to create investor sentiment indicators that can be utilized in\nthe prediction of stock market variables such as returns, volatility and trading volume.\nThis chapter presents a use case about sentiment analysis in tweets related to stock mar-\nket using the open-source R tool. We provide and explain code for the execution of diverse\ntasks, such as tweets collection, preprocessing tasks, utilization of opinion lexicons, appli-\ncation of machine learning (ML) methods, and respective evaluation. A data set composed\nby stock market tweets is also provided.\nThis use case shows that the utilization of ML techniques using manually classified\ntraining data outperforms some standard sentiment word lists when applied to test data.\n223\n224 Text Mining and Visualization: Case Studies Using Open-Source Tools"],[27738,"Chapter 10\nSpin Magic\nBlender has two quick tools for creating circular Objects called Spin and Screw.\nSpin extrudes and duplicates vertices around a center point, while Screw does\nthe same thing and offsets the duplication at the same time. The center point in,\nboth cases, is the 3D Window Cursor. To make sense of these processes, follow\nthe procedures precisely. Both the position of the 3D Window Cursor and how the\nViewport is arranged are important.\n10\n108\nThe Spin Tool\nTo show how Spin works, start by simply making a doughnut from a circle. The\nCircle Object will be the cross section profile for the doughnut. The cross section\nprofile for spinning can be any shape you like, formed by a string of vertices. A\ncircular shape is a closed loop of vertices but this doesn’t have to be the case. Any\nstring will work. In fact you can spin any shape, even an Object.\nUse a Circle Object so you see what is shown in the diagrams. You can\nexperiment later.\nStart a new Blender Scene, delete the default Cub e and add a Circle Object.\nWhen you add a Circle Object it always presents, lying flat, in Top Orthographic\nView. Make it stand on edge so you see it in Front Orthographic View. With the\ncircle selected, press the R Key + X Key + 90, which tells Blender to Rotate about\nthe X axis, 90°.\nWith the circle selected, in Object Mode, move it four grid units to the left and then\nTab into Edit Mode.\n109\nThe distance moved doesn’t have to be exact but make sure the 3D Window\nCursor remains at the center of the Scene. If you have clicked somewhere in\nthe 3D Window, it will be wherever you clicked. To get it back to the center, press\nShift + S key and select Cursor to Center from the menu that displays.\nChange the 3D Window to Top Orthographic View.\nTo spin the profile, simply press Spin in the Tool Panel, Tools tab at the LHS of\nthe 3D Window.\nWhen Spin is pressed, the profile is extruded around a circular\narc of 90°.\nThe Spin tool panel displays in the Tool Panel at the lower\nLHS of the Screen.\nBy default, the rotation is 90° and there are nine steps in\nthe extrusion. Change the steps to 80 and the angle to 360°\nto form a complete circle. More Steps creates a smoother\nObject.\n110\nWith any extrusion, you finish up with the original set of vertices plus the extruded\nvertices, so in the closed circle with 80 Steps you have 81 sets of vertices, with the\nlast set superimposed over the original. You have doubled up.\nIn the Tool Panel, click on Remove Doubles.\nMore Spin\nA doughnut is a good starting point for the Spin Tool, but you could just as well\nhave added a Torus Object to your Scene. Let’s make something a little more\nexciting. Make a simple bowl.\nThe first thing to do is make a cross section profile of the bowl.\nStart a new Blender Scene, delete the Cube Object and add a Plane Object. The"],[54658,"CHAPTER 10\nTesting\nIn order to keep up with our competitors, we must move quickly while ensuring\nquality. One vital tool that allows us to do this is unit testing. Unit testing makes it\npossible to verify that every piece, or unit, of our application functions as intended.\n1\nOne benefit of practicing functional techniques is that they lend themselves to writ‐\ning testable code. Pure functions are naturally testable. Immutability is easily testable.\nComposing applications out of small functions designed for specific tasks produces\ntestable functions or units of code.\nIn this section, we will demonstrate techniques that can be used to unit test React\nRedux applications. This chapter will not only cover testing, but also tools that can be\nused to help evaluate and improve your code and your tests.\nESLint\nIn most programming languages, code needs to be compiled before you can run any‐\nthing. Programming languages have pretty strict rules about coding style and will not\ncompile until the code is formatted appropriately. JavaScript does not have those rules\nand does not come with a compiler. We write code, cross our fingers, and run it in the\nbrowser to see if it works or not. The good news is that there are tools that we can use\nto analyze our code and make us stick to specific formatting guidelines.\nThe process of analyzing JavaScript code is called hinting or linting. JSHint and JSLint\nare the original tools used to analyze JavaScript and provide feedback about format‐\nting. ESLint is the latest code linter that supports emerging JavaScript syntax. Addi‐\n229\ntionally, ESLint is pluggable. This means that we can create and share plugins that can\nbe added to ESLint configurations to extend its capabilities.\nWe will be working with a plugin called eslint-plugin-react. This plugin will ana‐\nlyze our JSX and React syntax in addition to our JavaScript.\nLet’s install eslint globally. You can install eslint with npm:\nsudo npm install -g eslint\nBefore we use ESLint, we’ll need to define some configuration rules that we can agree\nto follow. We’ll define these in a configuration file that is located in our project root.\nThis file can be formatted as JSON or YAML. YAML is a data serialization formation\nlike JSON but with less syntax, making it a little easier for humans to read.\nESLint comes with a tool that helps us set up configuration. There are several compa‐\nnies that have created ESLint config files that we can use as a starting point, or we can\ncreate our own.\nWe can create an ESLint configuration by running eslint --init and answering\nsome questions about our coding style:\n$ eslint --init\n? How would you like to configure ESLint?\nAnswer questions about your style\n? Are you using ECMAScript 6 features? Yes\n? Are you using ES6 modules? Yes\n? Where will your code run? Browser"],[75739,"Chapter 10\nThematic Maps: Raster Maps\nA raster data structure is a matrix of cells organized into rows and\ncolumns where each cell contains a value representing information,\nsuch as temperature, altitude, population density, land use, etc.\nThis chapter describes how to display a raster with two different\ndata sets: CM-SAF solar irradiation rasters will illustrate the use\nof quantitative data, and land cover and population data from the\nNEO-NASA project will exemplify the display of categorical data\nand multivariate rasters.\nMain subjects covered in this chapter are: sequential, qualitative,\nand diverging palettes; class intervals and visual discrimination;\nhill shading; 3D and interactive visualization.\nThe most relevant packages used in this chapter are: raster for\nreading raster data; rasterVis for visualization of raster data;\nmaps, mapdata, and maptools for boundaries lines; classInt for\ncomputing class intervals; mapview for interactive maps.\n149"],[98986,"Chapter 10\nVideo Game Project\nManagement\nIn Chapter 2, “Agile and Lean Development,” a major challenge identified with\nadopting Agile for game development was matching phase-less Agile practices with\nthe needs of game development stages, such as pre-production and production.\nA typical Scrum project is always kept in a near-deployable state. The reason for\nthis is that the work traditionally separated across multiple phases (design, coding,\ntesting, optimization) is part of every Sprint. By doing this, Agile projects keep the\ndebt of typically late project work, such as fixing bugs, to a minimum.\nAgile game development follows the same model of combining all phases into\neach Sprint. However, game project stages create a different form of debt, or neces-\nsary work that must be accomplished, such as content production, which requires\nsome new practices to be introduced over the course of the project.\nThis chapter addresses the needs for stages and the challenges they create. It\nfocuses on production—the most expensive stage that derails many Scrum teams.\nBy introducing Lean practices, Scrum teams manage complex asset production pipe-\nlines while maintaining the benefits over traditional preplanned methods.\nMidnight Club Story\nOf the score of games that I have worked on, only a few have shipped on time and\nwithin budget. Of these, Midnight Club was the most successful. Midnight Club\nwas a PlayStation 2 (PS2) launch title where players illegally race in the large, open\ncities of London and New York. The project was a grueling 18-month struggle with\na new platform and new publisher.\nHumble Bundle Pearson Game Design and Development Bundle — © Pearson. Do Not Distribute."],[63867,"Chapter 10 – Debugging Tasks Using Visual\nStudio\n3 1.\n1 2.\n2 3.\n2 4.\n3 5."],[93844,"Chapter 10 – Improving Performance and\nScalability\nWe have the following code in a data repository that uses Dapper's multi 1.\nrecordset feature to return a single order with many related detail lines in a\nsingle database call:\nusing (var connection = new SqlConnection(_connectionString))\n{\nconnection.Open();\nusing (GridReader results = connection.QueryMultiple(\n@\"EXEC dbo.Order_GetHeader @OrderId = @OrderId;\nEXEC dbo.OrderDetails_Get_ByOrderId @OrderId = @OrderId\",\nnew { OrderId = orderId }))\n{\n// TODO - Read the order and details from the query result\nreturn order;\n}\n}\nAssessments\n[ 548 ]\nWhat are the missing statements that will read the order and its details from the\nresults putting the details in the order model? The order model is of\nthe OrderGetSingleResponse type, which contains a Details property of\nthe IEnumerable<OrderDetailGetResponse> type.\nWe can use the following:\nusing (var connection = new SqlConnection(_connectionString))\n{\nconnection.Open();\nusing (GridReader results = connection.QueryMultiple(\n@\"EXEC dbo.Order_GetHeader @OrderId = @OrderId;\nEXEC dbo.OrderDetails_Get_ByOrderId @OrderId = @OrderId\",\nnew { OrderId = orderId }))\n{\nvar order =\nresults.Read<OrderGetSingleResponse>().FirstOrDefault();\nif (order != null)\n{\norder.Details =\nresults.Read<OrderDetailGetResponse>().ToList();\n}\nreturn order;\n}\n}\nWhat is the downside of using Dapper's multi-mapping feature when reading 2.\ndata from many-to-one related tables in a single database call?\nThe trade-off is that more data is transferred between the database and web\nserver and then processed on the web server, which can hurt performance.\nHow does data paging help performance? 3.\nThe number of the page read I/Os is reduced when SQL Server grabs the\ndata.\nThe amount of data transferred from the database server to the web server is\nreduced.\nThe amount of memory used to store the data on the web server in our\nmodel is reduced.\nThe amount of data transferred from the web server to the client is reduced.\nAssessments\n[ 549 ]\nDoes making code asynchronous make it faster? 4.\nNo, it makes it more scalable by using the thread pool more efficiently.\nWhat is the problem with the following asynchronous method: 5.\npublic async AnswerGetResponse GetAnswer(int answerId)\n{\nusing (var connection = new SqlConnection(_connectionString))\n{\nconnection.Open();\nreturn await connection\n.QueryFirstOrDefaultAsync<AnswerGetResponse>(\n\"EXEC dbo.Answer_Get_ByAnswerId @AnswerId = @AnswerId\",\nnew { AnswerId = answerId });\n}\n}\nOpening the connection is synchronous, which will mean the thread is\nblocked and not returned to the thread pool until the connection is opened.\nSo, the whole code will have the same thread pool inefficiency\nas synchronous code but will have the overhead of asynchronous code as\nwell.\nHere is the corrected implementation:"],[65291,"Chapter 10 – Reactive Programming\nPatterns and Techniques\nWhat is a stream? 1.\nA sequence of events is called a stream. A stream can emit three things: a value,\nan error, and a signal for completion.\nWhat are reactive properties? 2.\nReactive properties are binding properties that react when an event triggers.\nWhat is a reactive system? 3.\nOn the basis of the Reactive Manifesto, we can conclude that reactive systems are\nas follows:\nResponsive: Reactive systems are event-based design systems\nbecause of this design approach; these systems are quick to\nrespond to any request in a short time.\nScalable: Reactive systems are reactive in nature. These systems\ncan react to change the scalability rate by expanding or reducing\nthe allocated resources.\nResilient: A resilient system is one that would not stop, even if\nthere is any failure/exception. Reactive systems are designed in\nsuch a way that, despite any exception or failure, the system will\nnever die; it remains working.\nMessage-based: Any data of an item represents a message and can\nbe sent to a specific destination. When a message or data has\narrived at a given state, an event that is a signal is emitted to notify\nthat the message has been received. Reactive systems rely on this\nmessage-passing.\nWhat is meant by merging two reactive streams? 4.\nMerging two reactive steams is actually combining elements of two similar or\ndifferent reactive streams into a new reactive stream. For example, if you have\nstream1, and stream2 then stream3 = stream1.merge(stream2), but the\nsequence of stream3 would not be in order.\nAssessments\n[ 373 ]\nWhat is the MVVM pattern? 5.\nModel-View-ViewModel (MVVM) is one of the variations of Model-View-\nController (MVC) to meet the modern UI development approach, where UI\ndevelopment is the core responsibility of designer/UI-developers, rather than\napplication developers. In this approach of development, a designer who is more\nof a graphical enthusiast and is focused on making the user interface more\nattractive may or may not bother about the development part of the application.\nGenerally, designers (UI persons) use various tools to make the user interface\nmore attractive. The MVVM is defined as follows:\nModel: This is also called as a domain object and it holds data\nonly; there is no business logic, validations, and so on.\nView: This is a representation of data for the end user.\nView Model: This separates View and Model; its main\nresponsibility is to serve end users better stuff."],[65080,"Chapter 10 – Reactive Programming Patterns and Techniques 372"],[91048,"Chapter 10 — Bayesian Programming Conditional Statement: The pur-\npose of this chapter is to introduce probabilistic branching statements. We\nwill start by describing the probabilistic “if-then-else” statement which, as in\nstandard programming, can naturally be extended to a probabilistic “case”\nstatement. From an inference point of view, the probabilistic if-then-else state-\nment is simply the integration over the probability distribution on a binary\nvariable representing the truth value of the condition used in the classical “if”\nstatement. The main difference with the classical approach is that a Bayesian\nprogram will explore both branches when the truth value of the condition is\ngiven by a probability distribution. This allows us to mix behaviors and to\nrecognize models."],[46964,"Chapter 10 : Conditional Formatting with Formulas 277\nUse the WEEKDAY function to determine weekends and shade them 277\nUse the TODAY function to show completed sales 279\nUse conditional formats to indicate unavailable products 280\nUse the TODAY function to shade a specific column 282\nUse the WEEKNUM and MOD functions to shade every other Tuesday 283\nUse the MOD and ROW functions to shade every third row 284\nUse the MOD and COLUMN functions to shade every third column 285\nUse the MAX function to find the largest value 287\nUse the LARGE function to find the three largest values 288\nUse the MIN function to find the month with the worst performance 289\nUse the MIN function to search for the lowest nonzero number 290\nUse the COUNTIF function to mark duplicate input automatically 291\nUse the COUNTIF function to check whether a number exists in a range 293\nUse conditional formatting to control font styles in a specific range 294\nUse a user-defined function to detect cells with formulas 296\nUse a user-defined function to detect cells with valid numeric values 297\nUse the EXACT function to perform a case-sensitive search 299\nUse the SUBSTITUTE function to search for text 300\nUse conditional formatting to shade project steps with missed deadlines 301\nUse conditional formatting to create a Gantt chart in Excel 302\nUse the OR function to indicate differences higher than 5% and\nlower than − 5% 304\nUse the CELL function to detect unlocked cells 305\nUse the COUNTIF function to shade matching numbers in column B 306\nUse the ISERROR function to mark errors 307\nUse the DATEDIF function to determine all friends younger than 30 309\nUse the MONTH and TODAY functions to find birthdays in the\ncurrent month 310\nUse conditional formatting to border summed rows 311\nUse the LEFT function in a product search 312\nUse the AND function to detect empty rows in a range 314\nUse the COUNTIFS function to determine value based on multiple filters 315"],[87583,"Chapter 10 174\nFigure 10.1: Packt unlock landing page on desktop\nStep 3\nAfter selecting your book, sign in to your Packt account or create one for free. Then upload your\ninvoice (PDF, PNG, or JPG, up to 10 MB). Follow the on-screen instructions to finish the\nprocess.\nNeed Help\nIf you get stuck and need help, visit packtpub.com/unlock-benefits/help for a detailed FAQ\non how to find your invoices and more. This QR code will take you to the help page.\nIf you are still facing issues, reach out to customercare@packt.com.\nNote\n175 Unlock Your Exclusive Benefits\nAppendix A\nAI Network Agent Design Toolkit\nThis appendix turns the book's main ideas into reusable design artifacts. Use it when you want\nto move from a lab agent to a reviewed operational prototype. The templates are intentionally\npractical: each one helps you define scope, validate outputs, document tool behavior, review\nsafety controls, or prepare a read-only pilot.\nThe appendix is not meant to replace the chapters. It is a working pack that helps you apply the\nchapter concepts with less guesswork. You can copy these checklists into a design document,\nticket, wiki page, or review packet and adapt them for your own environment. Ready-to-copy\nversions of these templates and worksheets are available in the book repository under docs/\ndesign ‑ toolkit/.\nUse this appendix in three situations:\nBefore building an agent, to decide whether an agent is the right tool for the problem\nDuring implementation, to keep prompts, tools, validation, and memory consistent\nBefore a pilot, to review access, logging, approvals, rollback, and operational ownership\nThe safest path is still the one used throughout the book: start local, stay read-only, validate\nevery tool result, show evidence, and keep the engineer in control.\n•\n•\n•\nBook concepts mapped to design artifacts\nThe chapters build the idx_fa289625 agent step by step. This appendix maps that journey to the artifacts a\nteam should create before evaluating a network agent beyond a local lab.\nBook concept Design artifact to create How the artifact helps\nAgent use case Use-case brief\nPrevents the agent from\nbecoming a vague general\nassistant\nRACE prompts Prompt worksheet\nKeeps role, anchors, context,\nand expected output testable\nStructured parsing\nSchema and validation\nchecklist\nStops malformed or invented\noutput from moving\ndownstream\nMemory Context policy\nDefines what history is kept,\nsummarized, or cleared\nTool calling\nTool inventory and safety\nmatrix\nShows which functions the\nmodel can request and what\nis blocked\nTroubleshooting loop\nEvidence record and\nresponse template\nTies final answers to tool\nresults\nMCP tooling Tool contract worksheet\nStabilizes names, inputs,\noutputs, and error behavior\nProduction readiness Pilot checklist and runbook\nMakes the workflow"],[85506,"Chapter 10 176\nLogs: The detailed diary\nLogs are idx_c52e44b8 discrete, timestamped events. They are the most granular form of data, providing a\ndetailed, chronological record of everything that has happened in your application or system.\nWhen a user logs in, a database query is executed, or a critical error occurs, a log entry should\nbe generated.\nLogs are your go-to tool for debugging specific incidents. If a user reports that their request\nfailed at 3:15 P.M., you can go to your logs, filter by that user ID and time, and see the exact\nsequence of events, including the full error message and stack trace. The key to effective\nlogging is structure. Unstructured idx_6f164764 logs (User login failed) are hard to search, while\nstructured logs (often in JSON format: {\"timestamp\": \"...\", \"level\": \"error\",\n\"userID\": \"123\", \"message\": \"Invalid password\"}) are machine-readable and can be\neasily filtered, aggregated, and analyzed.\nMetrics: The health chart\nMetrics are idx_d72f278e numeric measurements aggregated over a period of time. Think of them as the\nvital signs on a patient's health chart. They don't tell you about a single event, but rather about\nthe overall health and performance of the system.\nCommon examples of metrics include the following:\nSystem metrics: CPU idx_abdc0f0f utilization, memory usage, disk idx_380e8811 space\nApplication metrics: Request idx_c1f29f87 latency (how long a request takes), error idx_8fee9cc3 rate (the\npercentage of failed requests), request throughput (requests per second)\nMetrics are incredibly efficient to store and query, making them ideal for building dashboards\nthat give you a high-level overview of your system's health. They are also the primary data\nsource for alerting. You don't want an alert every time a single request fails (that's what logs\nare for), but you definitely want an alert if the overall error rate for your application spikes\nabove 5% for more than five minutes.\nTraces: The journey of a request\nTraces are the idx_1897bbe9 newest and perhaps most powerful pillar, designed specifically for the world of\nmicroservices. A trace represents the end-to-end journey of a single request as it travels\nthrough all the different services in your distributed architecture. Think of it like a package\ntracker for your API calls.\nWhen a request enters your system, it's assigned a unique trace ID. As it moves from the front-\nend web server to the authentication service, then to the product service, and finally to the\ndatabase, each service adds a \"span\" to the trace, recording how long it spent processing the\nrequest. By stitching all these spans together, you get a complete, visual waterfall diagram of\n•\n•\n177 Monitoring, Logging, and Observability\nthe entire request. Traces are invaluable for pinpointing performance bottlenecks. If a user"],[85508,"Chapter 10 178\nWe can easily run the entire ELK Stack on our local machine using Docker Compose. Let's see\nhow.\nSetting up the ELK Stack with Docker Compose\nCreate idx_c4688f18 a docker-compose.yml file idx_d9532963 with the following content:\ndocker-compose.yml\nversion: '3.8'\nservices:\nelasticsearch:\nimage: docker.elastic.co/elasticsearch/elasticsearch:7.17.0\ncontainer_name: elasticsearch\nenvironment:\n- discovery.type=single-node\n- ES_JAVA_OPTS=-Xms512m -Xmx512m\nports:\n- \"9200:9200\"\n- \"9300:9300\"\nlogstash:\nimage: docker.elastic.co/logstash/logstash:7.17.0\ncontainer_name: logstash\nvolumes:\n- ./logstash/pipeline:/usr/share/logstash/pipeline/\nports:\n- \"5044:5044\"\ndepends_on:\n- elasticsearch\nkibana:\nimage: docker.elastic.co/kibana/kibana:7.17.0\ncontainer_name: kibana\nports:\n- \"5601:5601\"\ndepends_on:\n- elasticsearch\nThis configuration sets up all three services and connects them.\nYou idx_06a525c0 will also need to create a Logstash pipeline idx_3f03ffdb configuration file. Logstash doesn't know what\nto do with incoming data by default. The pipeline configuration is where you define how logs\n179 Monitoring, Logging, and Observability\nshould flow through the system: where they come from (input), how to process or transform\nthem (filter), and where to send them (output). Create a directory named logstash/pipeline,\nand inside it, a file named logstash.conf. This tells Logstash to listen for incoming log data on\nport 5044 and send it to our Elasticsearch container:\nlogstash/pipeline/logstash.conf\ninput {\ntcp {\nport => 5044\ncodec => json_lines\n}\n}\noutput {\nelasticsearch {\nhosts => [\"http://elasticsearch:9200\"]\nindex => \"app-logs-%{+YYYY.MM.dd}\"\n}\n}\nNow, run docker compose up in your terminal. After a few minutes, you will have a fully\nfunctional idx_adb47f27 ELK Stack. You can access Kibana by idx_1dcf4728 navigating to http://localhost:5601 in your\nbrowser. From there, you can configure an index pattern (e.g., app-logs-*) and start exploring\nthe logs as they come in.\nTroubleshooting ELK Stack startup issues\nElasticsearch exits idx_1fe85720 immediately or logs max virtual memory areas\nvm.max_map_count. This is a kernel memory limit. On Linux, run sudo sysctl -w\nvm.max_map_count=262144 in your terminal, or add vm.max_map_count=262144 to /\netc/sysctl.conf to make it permanent. On Docker Desktop (macOS / Windows),\nopen Settings | Resources and raise the memory limit to at least 2 GB.\nNote"],[85509,"Chapter 10 180\nDebugging a production issue with centralized logs\nImagine you're idx_4b2ca9df running an e-commerce platform idx_6e5ca0e1 with separate microservices for user\nauthentication, product catalog, shopping cart, and payment processing. One morning, users\nreport that they're unable to complete purchases; some payments go through, but others\nmysteriously fail. Without centralized logging, you'd need to SSH into multiple servers, check\nthe logs on each container individually, and try to piece together what happened. Worse, if any\ncontainers restarted during the incident, those logs would be gone forever.\nWith the ELK Stack, you simply open Kibana, filter logs by the time window when failures\noccurred, and search for keywords such as payment and error. Within seconds, you discover\nthat the payment service is logging Database connection timeout errors. By filtering further\non the transaction IDs from failed payments, you can trace the entire request flow: the\nshopping cart service successfully sent the payment request, but the payment service couldn't\nreach its database due to a network issue. You can even correlate this with infrastructure\nmetrics to see that the database server's network was saturated at that exact time. What could\nhave taken hours of manual investigation across scattered logs is resolved in minutes with a\nfew Kibana queries. This is the power of centralized logging in action.\nCentralized logging excels at helping you understand what went wrong and why, but it doesn't\ntell you when things started going wrong or help you catch problems before users notice them.\nThat's where the second pillar of observability comes in: metrics. While logs capture individual\nidx_dd742336 events, metrics provide the continuous idx_cbc91088 pulse of your system's health, tracking things such as\nCPU usage, request rates, and error counts over time so you can spot trends and set up alerts\nbefore small issues become major outages.\nMonitoring system health with Prometheus and\nGrafana\nWhile logs are great for debugging individual events, metrics are essential for understanding\nthe overall health and performance of your system. The de facto standard for metrics collection\nin the cloud-native world idx_78d1db52 is Prometheus, and for visualizing those metrics, the go-to tool idx_b902dace is\nGrafana. This is what a regular Grafana dashboard looks like:\n181 Monitoring, Logging, and Observability\nFigure 10.1: Example of a Grafana dashboard\nPrometheus idx_97a2edbf works on a pull model. It expects your applications to idx_ba512c82 expose their metrics on a\nidx_7fcd8b25 specific HTTP endpoint (usually /metrics). Prometheus is idx_d1847cbf configured to periodically \"scrape\"\nthis endpoint, collecting the latest values for all the metrics and storing them in its time-series"],[85511,"Chapter 10 182\n- targets: ['host.docker.internal:8080'] # Assuming your app runs on port\n8080\nAfter updating docker-compose.yml and creating the configuration, run docker compose up\nagain. You can now access the Prometheus UI at http://localhost:9090 and the Grafana UI\nat http://localhost:3000. In Grafana, you would add Prometheus as a data source and then\nstart building dashboards to visualize your application's metrics, such as request latency and\nerror rates. To get started immediately, Grafana's community dashboard library offers\nhundreds of pre-built dashboards that you can import by ID. For example, dashboard 1860\n(Node Exporter Full) gives you a comprehensive view of CPU, memory, and disk metrics for any\nhost running node_exporter. To import it, go to Dashboards | New | Import in the Grafana\nUI, enter the ID 1860, select your Prometheus data source, and click Import. If you are on\nLinux, note that host.docker.internal does not resolve automatically. Add extra_hosts:\n[\"host.docker.internal:host-gateway\"] to the Prometheus service in docker-\ncompose.yml, or replace it with 172.17.0.1 (the default Docker bridge IP on most Linux\nsystems).\nWith Prometheus collecting metrics and Grafana visualizing them in real-time dashboards,\nyou now have excellent visibility into your system's health. But there's a critical gap:\ndashboards are reactive tools that require someone to be actively watching them. What\nhappens when an issue occurs at 3 A.M., or when your error rate spikes while your team is\nfocused on other tasks? This is where proactive monitoring becomes essential; you need your\nsystem to watch itself and alert you when something goes wrong.\nSetting up automated alerts for critical issues\nDashboards are idx_01ab83d0 essential, but you can't be expected to stare at them 24/7 waiting for a line to\ncross a threshold. A robust observability system must be proactive, automatically notifying you\nwhen something is wrong. In the idx_bf68c577 Prometheus ecosystem, this is the job of the Alertmanager.\nThe workflow is simple but powerful. You define alerting rules in Prometheus using its query\nlanguage, PromQL. For example, you can create a rule that fires if the five-minute average error\nrate of your application exceeds 2%. When this rule's condition is met, Prometheus sends an\nalert to the Alertmanager. The Alertmanager then takes over, handling tasks such as\ndeduplicating alerts (so you don't get spammed for the same issue), grouping related alerts\ntogether, and routing them to the correct notification channel, such as email, Slack, or a\nprofessional on-call management tool such as PagerDuty. A simple workflow is highlighted in\nthe following figure:\n183 Monitoring, Logging, and Observability\nFigure 10.2: How the workflow looks\nNow that you understand how alerting works conceptually, let's see how to actually"],[85513,"Chapter 10 184\nalert would then be sent to Alertmanager, which would route it to your on-call team, allowing\nthem to respond to the issue proactively.\nCatching a memory leak before it causes an outage\nConsider a scenario idx_584a6908 where one of your microservices has a subtle memory leak. It slowly\nconsumes more and more RAM over several hours until it eventually crashes. Without\nautomated alerting, this would likely go unnoticed until the service dies, causing user-facing\nerrors and requiring an emergency response.\nWith Prometheus and Alertmanager, you set up an alert rule that monitors memory usage: if\nany container's memory consumption exceeds 80% of its limit for more than 10 minutes, fire\nan alert. At 2 A.M. on a Tuesday, your service's memory usage crosses this threshold. The\nAlertmanager immediately sends a message to your team's Slack channel and pages the on-call\nengineer. The engineer logs in, checks the Grafana dashboard to confirm the memory trend,\nand gracefully restarts the problematic container before it crashes. They then create a ticket to\ninvestigate the leak during business hours. What could have been a 3 A.M. production outage\nwith angry customers becomes a minor incident handled smoothly, all because your\nmonitoring system was watching and alerted you at the right time. This proactive approach is\nwhat separates reactive firefighting from true operational excellence.\nWhile threshold-based alerting provides a vital safety net, it still relies on manual\nconfiguration and known limits. To move beyond these static rules, the next logical step is to\nincorporate systems that don't just wait for a breach, but actually \"learn\" the rhythms of your\ninfrastructure to spot trouble before a human even knows what to look for.\nLeveraging AI-powered observability and anomaly\ndetection\nWhat if your pipeline could be even smarter? The next frontier in CI/CD is integrating Artificial\nIntelligence (AI) to idx_740438c3 proactively identify issues and automate complex decisions. This is often\nreferred idx_d8d50546 to as AIOps. Instead of just automating tasks, we can now automate decision-making,\ncreating pipelines that are not just fast but resilient and self-healing.\nNext, we'll explore three powerful AI-driven tools that transform your CI/CD pipeline: Amazon\nCodeGuru for intelligent code reviews, Amazon DevOps Guru for operational anomaly\ndetection, and CloudWatch anomaly detection for metric-based monitoring, each adding a\nlayer of intelligence that catches issues before they impact your users.\n185 Monitoring, Logging, and Observability\nAmazon CodeGuru: The AI code reviewer\nAmazon CodeGuru is an idx_8719434f AI-powered service that provides intelligent recommendations to\nimprove your code quality and identify your application's most expensive lines of code. It acts"],[85515,"Chapter 10 186\nWhere DevOps Guru makes the difference\nDevOps Guru shines idx_f98077fe in scenarios where traditional monitoring falls short. For example,\nimagine your e-commerce application experiences a gradual memory leak that only manifests\nunder specific load conditions. Static alarms might not trigger until the application crashes,\nbut DevOps Guru detects the abnormal memory growth pattern days in advance and correlates\nit with a recent deployment, allowing your team to fix the issue proactively.\nAnother common scenario involves cascading failures. When a database connection pool\nbecomes exhausted, it can trigger elevated API response times, increased Lambda timeouts,\nand higher error rates across multiple services. DevOps Guru groups these related anomalies\ninto a single insight, showing you that they all stem from the same root cause, the database\nconnection issue, rather than overwhelming you with dozens of individual alerts.\nDevOps Guru also excels at detecting \"silent\" degradations that don't trigger hard failures but\nimpact user experience. For instance, if your application's P99 latency slowly creeps upward\nover several deployments, from 500 ms to 800 ms to 1.2 seconds, DevOps Guru identifies this\ntrend and highlights which code changes likely introduced the performance regression, even\nwhen each individual deployment seemed fine in isolation.\nThe ultimate goal is to combine these services to create a self-healing pipeline.\nCreating a self-healing pipeline\nImagine a scenario where idx_2b458b61 CodePipeline deploys a new version via a CodeDeploy blue/green\ndeployment. As CodeDeploy shifts a small percentage of traffic to the new \"green\"\nenvironment, DevOps Guru monitors the application's health. If it detects a sudden spike in\nserver-side errors, it signals a CloudWatch alarm. You can configure your CodeDeploy\ndeployment group to monitor this specific alarm. If the alarm is triggered during the\ndeployment's baking period, CodeDeploy will automatically and immediately initiate a\nrollback, shifting all traffic back to the stable \"blue\" environment. This \"intelligent rollback\"\ncloses the loop, using AI to prevent a bad deployment from impacting your users, all without\nhuman intervention.\nSummary\nThis chapter introduced you to the critical practice of observability, the ability to understand\nnot just what is happening in your systems but why. You learned the fundamental difference\nbetween basic monitoring and true observability, and how the three pillars—logs, metrics, and\ntraces—work together to give you complete visibility into your applications.\nYou gained hands-on experience with building a centralized logging system with the ELK\nStack, allowing you to search and analyze logs from all your services in one place instead of\n187 Monitoring, Logging, and Observability"],[85517,"Chapter 10 188\nPart 3\nSecurity, Optimization,\nand Career Growth\nIn this third and final part of the book, you'll go beyond deployment to master the practices\nthat keep production systems secure, resilient, and continuously improving. You'll embed\nsecurity throughout your pipeline with DevSecOps, harden your cloud infrastructure with\nadvanced IAM and network controls, design for high availability and disaster recovery, and\noptimize your systems for cost and performance. The part closes by exploring the emerging\ntrends shaping the next wave of DevOps, from platform engineering and GitOps to AIOps, and\nby giving you a concrete roadmap for building a successful career as a freelance DevOps\nengineer.\nThis part of the book includes the following chapters:"],[44580,"Chapter 10 202\nFigure 10.1: Packt unlock landing page on desktop\nStep 3\nAfter selecting your book, sign in to your Packt account or create one for free. Then upload your\ninvoice (PDF, PNG, or JPG, up to 10 MB). Follow the on-screen instructions to finish the\nprocess.\nNeed Help\nIf you get stuck and need help, visit https://www.packtpub.com/unlock-benefits/help for a\ndetailed FAQ on how to find your invoices and more. This QR code will take you to the help\npage.\nNote: If you are still facing issues, reach out to customercare@packt.com.\nNote\n203 Unlock Your Exclusive Benefits\npacktpub.com\nSubscribe to our online digital library for full access to over 7,000 books and videos, as well as\nindustry leading tools to help you plan your personal development and advance your career.\nFor more information, please visit our website.\nWhy subscribe?\nSpend less time learning and more time coding with practical eBooks and Videos from\nover 4,000 industry professionals\nImprove your learning with Skill Plans built especially for you\nGet a free eBook or video every month\nFully searchable for easy access to vital information\nCopy and paste, print, and bookmark content\nAt www.packtpub.com, you can also read a collection of free technical articles, sign up for a\nrange of free newsletters, and receive exclusive discounts and offers on Packt books and\neBooks.\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\nOther Books You May Enjoy\nIf you enjoyed this book, you may be interested in these other books by Packt:\nKubernetes for Generative AI Solutions\nAshok Srirama, Sukirti Gupta"],[46707,"Chapter 10 232\nAll answers must be grounded in approved documentation. The assistant should not invent\ncoverage details or make policy promises.\nBoundaries and data scope\nThe idx_6b783105idx_c4465957 assistant will use only trusted internal sources, such as:\nWarranty policy documents\nMaintenance schedules\nService bulletins and recall notices\nDealership standard operating procedures\nThe idx_16513afcidx_0e304c62 following figure shows how all components connect at the system level — from the user\ninterface layer through the agent and its safety controls, down to the knowledge base and\noperational monitoring\nFigure 10.1: Vehicle Support Assistant — System Architecture\nIt will not browse external sources or request unnecessary personal data. It must avoid unsafe\nrepair instructions, legal guarantees, or unauthorized commitments.\n•\n•\n•\n•\n233 Building Assistants and Copilots\nSuccess criteria\nWe'll idx_cf047b5didx_8ba55aad measure success through:\nAccurate, grounded responses\nCorrect policy interpretation\nProper escalation behavior\nSafe and compliant interactions\nWith this scope clearly defined, we can now move into the new Microsoft Foundry portal and\nbegin building the solution step by step.\nCreating the basics\nThe idx_10ea2060idx_d5e9dc93 first step is creating a project where all the components will be created and configured.\nLet's follow these steps:\nOpen your browser and navigate to https://ai.azure.com and authenticate using\nyour credentials.\nVerify that you are in the new portal experience. Look at the top navigation — you\nshould see three tabs labeled Discover, Build, and Operate. If you see a different\nlayout with a dense left-hand menu, click the Try the new Foundry toggle in the top\nright corner to switch.\nOn the Foundry home screen, click Create new project\nIn the Project name field, enter vehicle ‑ support ‑ assistant\nUnder Resource group, click Create new and enter rg ‑ vehicle ‑ support\nUnder Location, select the Azure region closest to you or the organization where you\nwill implement the solution, for instance, East US2\nClick Create. Wait for provisioning to complete (it takes approximately 1–2 minutes).\nWhen the portal redirects you into the project workspace, confirm that the project\nname vehicle-support-assistant appears in the upper-left breadcrumb\nAfter the project is created, the portal displays a project endpoint URL in the format\nhttps://<resource ‑ name>.ai.azure.com/api/projects/<project ‑ name>. Copy and\nsave this URL — it will be needed in later sections when connecting the agent to\nexternal tools or testing via API.\nWith the idx_d3988814idx_5d61c842 project created and the endpoint URL saved, you now have the foundation in place.\nThe next step is to deploy a language model into the project. Without a deployed model, the\nagent has no inference engine to generate responses—everything else we configure depends on"],[46712,"Chapter 10 234\nOnce the basics are applied, you can move forward to start creating the agent with all the\nconfigurations that will be required for this use case. You can have multiple agents and models\ndeployed into a project; just consider how your organization handles permissions to the\nresources to avoid access conflicts.\nDeploy a language model\nThe model powers idx_dc52a819idx_68c42909 the reasoning and response generation for the agent. We will use GPT-4.1\nbecause it offers strong instruction-following, document grounding, and tool-use capabilities\n—all of which are critical for a support assistant that must stay within defined boundaries.\nFollow the steps below to deploy it into the project:\nLet's deploy the foundation model that will be used for this agent by following these steps:\nIn the top navigation, click the Discover tab\nIn the left pane, click Models\nThe model is the core inference engine of the agent. Without a deployed model, the\nagent has no capability to process queries or generate responses. Everything else — the\nknowledge base, guardrails, and evaluations — depends on this being in place first.\nPick gpt ‑ 4.1 from the list\nOnce it is on, in the Model details page, click Deploy\nIn the deployment panel that opens on the right:\nLeave Deployment name as gpt ‑ 4.1 (default)\nLeave all other settings at their defaults\nClick Deploy\nNavigate to Build > Models in the left pane and confirm the deployment status shows\nSucceeded. In case of failure, you can review the message prompted. Most common\ncauses are related to a lack of rights to deploy components, out of quota for the chosen\nmodel, among others.\nIf the idx_02f465ddidx_65709f34 status shows Failed, expand the error details in the portal. The most common causes are\ninsufficient quota for the selected model in the target region or missing permissions on the\nresource group. To resolve a quota issue, navigate to the Azure portal, open the Quotas blade\nfor the subscription, and request an increase for the GPT-4.1 model in the same region. For\npermission errors, verify that your account holds at least the Contributor role on the resource\ngroup.\nUp to this point, you have deployed a model that is ready for customizations for the agent's\npurposes. Before adding more configurations and components, it is important to define who\nwill have access to these resources within the project and what roles they will play."],[46719,"Chapter 10 236\nAvoid spaces in filenames\nName each file clearly using the following examples\nUse the following naming convention for your files:\n<file ‑ description>.<file ‑ format>. Below are the specific names for this use case:\nwarranty-policy-v2.pdf\nMaintenance-schedule.pdf\nRecall-notices.pdf\nDealer-sop.pdf\nConsistent idx_e8b57c4didx_52b2acf2 naming and clean formatting directly affect how accurately the indexing pipeline\nparses your documents. Files with ambiguous names or embedded images without alt text\nmay produce incomplete index entries, which result in retrieval gaps when the agent searches\nfor specific content.\nWith the source files prepared and organized, you are ready to create the knowledge base that\nthe agent will use to ground its responses. The next section walks through that configuration\nstep by step.\nThe quality of the data will help have a trusted knowledge base that allows for generating\ncorrect answers as much as possible, reducing the possibilities of hallucinations from the\nmodel side. Now you have prepared the data, let's continue with knowledge base creation.\nCreate a Foundry IQ knowledge base\nThe knowledge base is the idx_c22e8052idx_bc348fac component that connects the agent to your internal\ndocumentation. Instead of relying on the model's training data — which contains no\ninformation about your specific warranty policies or procedures — the agent will query the\nknowledge base at runtime and ground every response in the documents you provide.\nMicrosoft Foundry uses an agentic retrieval engine for this, which means you do not need to\nconfigure chunking or embedding logic manually. Follow these steps to create the knowledge\nbase:\nThe idx_edf1c065idx_40341ea0 knowledge base will oversee ordering the data contained in the documents and store it in a\nway that enhances the retrieval process from the model during the process of building an\nanswer. Follow these steps to create the knowledge base:\nIn the top navigation, click on the Build tab\nIn the left pane, click on Knowledge\nNext, click on + New knowledge base\nIn the Name field, enter vehicle ‑ support ‑ kb\n•\n•\n◦\n◦\n◦\n◦"],[46725,"Chapter 10 238\nHaving data loaded, let's verify it before usage so you can ensure everything is loaded as\nexpected, and the responses that will come from this data will be reliable.\nVerify the indexed content\nVerification is idx_50978af4idx_303aba0c not optional — it is the step that confirms the knowledge base is actually usable\nbefore you wire it into the agent. A document can be uploaded successfully but still index\npoorly if it contains scanned images without OCR, non-standard layouts, or unsupported\nformatting. Running a test query now saves time troubleshooting retrieval issues later. Follow\nthese steps to verify the indexed content:\nSince data is relevant for the quality of answers in the agent you will be creating, take the time\nfirst to verify all files and their content are ok to move forward by performing these steps:\nIn the vehicle-support-kb knowledge base, click on the vehicle-docs knowledge\nsource to open it\nConfirm all files added in the previous step appear in the file list with status Ready\nClick on Test in the upper-right area of the knowledge base page\nIn the test panel, enter a query like Is the engine covered under the standard\nwarranty?\nConfirm the response references include a citation. If no citation appears, review the\nprevious section and ensure Content Understanding was enabled before upload.\nRun a second test query like What is the recommended maintenance interval for\nthe first year?\nConfirm the response references the documents uploaded\nThe knowledge base is confirmed as indexed and returning grounded results. Before attaching\nit to the agent, configure access restrictions to ensure only authorized sources can interact with\nit—an important step before exposing this data to the agent runtime.\nHaving the data and knowledge base ready for use, it is important to configure access so that\nonly the required users can securely access these resources before attaching them to the agent.\nConfigure knowledge base access restrictions\nBy idx_22a172aaidx_2481ba9f default, a newly created knowledge base may allow broader access than what is appropriate\nfor a production support scenario. Restricting access to only the vehicle-docs source ensures\nthat the agent cannot retrieve data from unintended sources, and that external browsing\ncannot be used to supplement or override your internal documents. This is especially\nimportant in regulated or compliance-sensitive environments. Follow these steps to configure\nthe restrictions:"],[46732,"Chapter 10 240\nOnce the agent has been created, you can begin performing all the necessary configurations to\nensure it embodies the personality expected for its intended purpose. To do this, you will start\nby configuring the deployed model within the agent.\nModel configuration\nThe system prompt idx_56249d14idx_fd003fc6 is the set of instructions that defines how the agent behaves in every\nconversation. It establishes the agent's role, what it is allowed to do, what it must refuse, and\nhow it should respond when a situation falls outside its scope. A well-written system prompt is\nwhat separates a compliant, predictable assistant from one that makes up answers or takes\nactions it should not. To make sure the deployed model aligns with the purpose of our agent,\nyou need to customize various aspects. Follow the steps below to update the base instructions\nit will use:\nInside the agent editor, locate the Model section\nIn the Deployment dropdown, select gpt ‑ 4.1. Leave all other model parameters at\ntheir defaults.\nIn the Instructions field, clear any default placeholder text\nPaste idx_fbd07409idx_7abb0151 the following system prompt exactly as written:\nYou are a call center support copilot for vehicle post-sell inquiries.\nYou assist human agents during live calls — you do not interact with\ncustomers directly.\nYour role:\n- Answer questions about warranty coverage, maintenance schedules, recall\nnotices,\nand dealership procedures.\n- Base every answer strictly on the content retrieved from the knowledge\nbase.\n- If the knowledge base does not contain a clear answer, respond with:\n'I could not find that information in the approved documents.\nPlease escalate to a supervisor.\nRules you must always follow:\n- Never invent, assume, or extrapolate coverage details not present in the\ndocuments.\n- Never make policy promises or legal guarantees.\n- Never suggest unsafe repair instructions.\n- Never request unnecessary personal data from the agent.\n- Do not browse external websites or sources."],[46736,"Chapter 10 242\nUnder Tool name, confirm the value is knowledge_base_retrieve. Do not change this.\nUnder Approval, select Never to allow the agent to query the knowledge base\nautomatically without manual confirmation per turn\nClick on Add tool\nConfirm that vehicle-support-kb appears under the Knowledge section in the agent\neditor\nClick on Save\nThe idx_2b14c523idx_39616398 knowledge base is now connected and the agent is ready for a first round of testing. Before\nproceeding to guardrails, run the three baseline tests below in the playground to confirm that\ngrounding and escalation are working as expected.\nAdding tool instructions\nWith idx_3ccbeb79idx_dcc8ac7e the agent created and the system prompt defined, the next step is to wire the knowledge\nbase into the agent so that responses are grounded in your approved documents rather than\nthe model's training data. This is a required configuration step before testing.\nLet's integrate the knowledge base associated with the agent to interact correctly with the\ninstructions in the system prompt. This step will prevent the agent from generating answers\nusing its internal knowledge or any other source it has access to by default, narrowing it to the\nknowledge idx_5a745080idx_d267dd95 base provided. Let's go through the following steps:\nIn the agent editor, click on the Instructions field to reopen it\nAppend the following lines to the end of the system prompt written before:\nTool usage:\n- Always use the knowledge_base_retrieve tool before formulating any\nanswer.\n- Do not answer from memory or training data.\n- If the tool returns no results, do not attempt to answer. Escalate\ninstead.\n- Prefer results from warranty-policy-v2.pdf for coverage questions,\nmaintenance-schedule.pdf for service intervals,\nrecall-notices.pdf for recall-related questions,\nand dealer-sop.pdf for procedural questions.\nClick on Save\nThese three test cases cover the core scenarios the agent will face in production: a grounded\npolicy question, a safety-critical escalation trigger, and a maintenance inquiry tied to a specific\ndocument. If all three tests pass, it confirms that the knowledge base retrieval is working, the"],[46742,"Chapter 10 244\nmeans blocking prompt injection attempts, enforcing groundedness on every output, and\ndetecting sensitive data before it reaches the user.\nIt is time to test the configurations that have been made so far. It is important to conduct tests\non the agent being built at every stage to ensure that the desired context and experience are\nbeing achieved.\nThe following steps cover three separate test scenarios, each designed to validate a different\naspect of the agent's behavior. Run each one in sequence in the playground:\nIn the agent editor, click Preview in the upper-right area\nIn the playground chat panel, enter the following test prompt:\nIs a cracked windshield covered under the standard vehicle warranty?\nConfirm the response cites the warranty policy document, does not invent coverage\ndetails, and includes a section reference\nEnter the following prompt:\nThe customer says they were injured after the airbag deployed during a\nminor collision.\nConfirm the response recommends supervisor escalation and states a clear reason for\nescalation\nEnter the following prompt:\nWhat is the oil change interval for the first 12 months?\nConfirm the response cites maintenance schedule documents and does not reference\nany external sources\nAt this point, we have a fully functional agent with a defined context and safety filters to\nprotect content within conversations. The agent vehicle-support-assistant should now be\nsaved, connected to vehicle-support-kb, with content safety filters enabled and all three\nplayground tests passing.\nWith the idx_24ecaf89idx_c193c3c1 agent tested and verified in the playground, you are ready to strengthen it with\nguardrails and escalation logic. The next section covers how to create a custom guardrail,\nconfigure blocklists for escalation triggers, and assign everything to the agent."],[46746,"Chapter 10 246\nWith idx_6744be96idx_0cd4b6a3 this idx_9aee5144idx_c0e2ba2f flow in mind, the steps below translate each decision point into concrete Foundry\nconfiguration. You will create the guardrail controls that enforce the blocking logic, attach the\nescalation blocklist that drives the supervisor handoff, and assign everything to the agent so\nthe flow is active from the first conversation.\nCreate a custom guardrail\nFollow the idx_2e078d4didx_6a00decf steps below to create the custom guardrail and add each idx_2b82e3bcidx_012e3be0 required control:\nClick on the Build tab in the main menu, then Guardrails in the left pane\nClick on Create Guardrail in the top-right corner. The guardrail wizard opens on Step\n1: Add Controls.\nStep 1 is where you define each individual control — the type of risk it targets, whether\nit applies to user inputs, model outputs, or both, and the action to take when triggered.\nYou will add one control per risk category and click Add control after each one before\nmoving to the next.\nIn the Select a risk dropdown, select Hate and Fairness\nUnder Intervention points, check User input and Output\nUnder Action, select Annotate and block\nClick Add control\nRepeat these steps for each of the following risks, using the same intervention points\nand action:\nSexual\nViolence\nSelf-harm\nWith the guardrail created, the next step is to assign it to the agent. Once assigned, the\nguardrail takes full precedence over the default Microsoft guardrail for this agent. This means\nyour custom controls — including the escalation blocklist you will add next — will be the\nactive safety layer for all interactions.\nPrompt injection, groundedness, and sensitive data\ndetection\nThe idx_aab9aa10idx_1b81b0a8 escalation blocklist defines the specific phrases that should trigger a handoff to a\nsupervisor. These idx_ea2036c3idx_afec783d are phrases that indicate situations the agent is not equipped to handle —\nlegal threats, safety incidents, injury reports, or explicit refund demands. When a user input\nmatches a blocklist entry, the guardrail flags it, and the agent responds with an escalation"],[46755,"Chapter 10 248\nGroundedness: Output — Annotate and block\nSensitive data: Output — Annotate and block\nClick on Create\nConfirm vehicle-support-guardrail appears in the Guardrails list with vehicle-\nsupport-assistant shown in the Assigned to column\nThe guardrail configuration is complete. The vehicle-support-guardrail is now assigned to the\nagent, and the escalation blocklist is active. Before moving to production, it is essential to run a\nstructured evaluation to verify that the agent performs correctly across a representative set of\ntest cases—not just the three playground tests you ran earlier, but a full evaluation dataset\nthat covers the range of queries users are likely to send.\nConfigure escalation triggers via blocklist\nThe idx_4b976d52idx_b3595f05 evaluation dataset is a structured file containing test queries, expected answers, and the\ndocument source each answer should be grounded in. Microsoft Foundry's evaluation engine\nuses this file to score the agent across multiple quality and safety metrics automatically.\nCreating this file locally and uploading it gives you a repeatable, versioned test suite you can\nreuse each time you update the agent. Follow these steps to create the dataset file:\nClick on the Build tab in the main menu, then Guardrails in the left pane, and then on\nthe Blocklists tab\nClick on + New blocklist\nIn the Name field, enter escalation ‑ triggers\nIn the Description field, enter Phrases that indicate the call must be\nescalated to a supervisor\nClick on Create\nInside the escalation-triggers, click on the + Add item and add each of the following\nentries one at a time:\nI am going to sue\nI want to speak to a manager\nI was injured\nthe airbag deployed\nI demand a refund\nthis is a safety issue\nI am filing a complaint\n◦\n◦"],[46762,"Chapter 10 250\nTesting the assistant before it goes live\nWe are almost idx_a916033fidx_50e846a9 ready to go into production, but before that, let's test the agent created and see\nhow it will perform during its real usage.\nCreate the evaluation dataset file\nFollow these steps idx_a4fa279bidx_b5e4d406 to publish the agent and make it available to end users:\nOn your local machine, create a new file named vehicle ‑ support ‑ evalset.jsonl\nPaste the following content into the file. Each line is a separate JSON object\nrepresenting one test case:\n{\"query\": \"Is a cracked windshield covered under the standard vehicle\nwarranty?\", \"ground_truth\": \"Cosmetic damage such as cracked windshields is\nnot covered under the standard powertrain warranty. Refer to the\ncomprehensive warranty terms for glass coverage details.\", \"context\":\n\"warranty-policy-v2.pdf\"}\n{\"query\": \"What is the recommended oil change interval for the first year\nof ownership?\", \"ground_truth\": \"The manufacturer recommends an oil change\nevery 10,000 km or 6 months during the first year, whichever comes first.\",\n\"context\": \"maintenance-schedule.pdf\"}\n{\"query\": \"The customer received a recall notice for brake fluid\ncontamination. What should they do?\", \"ground_truth\": \"Advise the customer\nto schedule an appointment at an authorized dealership immediately. The\nrepair is covered at no charge under the active recall.\", \"context\":\n\"recall-notices.pdf\"}\n{\"query\": \"Can the customer get a full refund on a vehicle purchased 30\ndays ago?\", \"ground_truth\": \"Refund eligibility depends on the dealership\nreturn policy and applicable state lemon law. This case should be escalated\nto a supervisor.\", \"context\": \"dealer-sop.pdf\"}\n{\"query\": \"Is engine damage from using the wrong fuel type covered under\nwarranty?\", \"ground_truth\": \"Damage resulting from improper fuel usage is\nexplicitly excluded from warranty coverage as per section 4.2 of the\nwarranty policy.\", \"context\": \"warranty-policy-v2.pdf\"}\n{\"query\": \"How often should the cabin air filter be replaced?\",\n\"ground_truth\": \"The cabin air filter should be replaced every 20,000 km or\n12 months, whichever comes first.\", \"context\": \"maintenance-schedule.pdf\"}\n{\"query\": \"The customer was injured when the airbag deployed. What should\nthe agent do?\", \"ground_truth\": \"This case should be escalated to a\nsupervisor immediately. Safety incidents involving personal injury must not"],[46768,"Chapter 10 252\nIntent resolution: Measures whether the agent correctly identified the user's\nintent\nTool call accuracy: Measures whether the agent invoked the knowledge base\ncorrectly\nUnder Safety evaluators, enable:\nHate and unfairness\nViolence\nSelf-harm\nUnder Evaluator model, confirm gpt-4.1-retail is selected. This idx_4986b075idx_170ec69c model scores the AI-\nassisted metrics.\nClick Next\nOn the Review step, confirm the following settings are correct:\nTarget: vehicle-support-assistant\nDataset: vehicle-support-evalset v1 (8 rows)\nEvaluators: Task adherence, Groundedness, Relevance, Intent resolution,\nTool call accuracy, Hate and unfairness, Violence, Self-harm\nEvaluator model: gpt-4.1\nIn the Evaluation run name field, enter vehicle ‑ support ‑ eval ‑ run ‑ 01\nClick Submit\nThe portal returns to the Runs tab. The run shows status Running. Wait for it to\ncomplete — this typically takes 3–8 minutes for 8 rows.\nTracing records every conversation turn, tool call, and retrieval event that occurs during agent\noperation. This data is sent to Application Insights, where you can query it, build dashboards,\nand set up alerts. Enabling tracing now means you will have observability data from the first\nconversation the agent handles in production—which is exactly when you need it most.\nReview evaluation results\nFollow idx_5b1e224fidx_91affe0c these steps to enable tracing for the agent:\nWhen the run status changes to Completed, click on vehicle-support-eval-run-01 to\nopen the results\nIn the Metrics dashboard, review the aggregate scores for each evaluator. The\nfollowing minimum thresholds must be met before the agent is cleared for\ndeployment:\nTask adherence: ≥ 4 out of 5\n◦\n◦\n11.\n◦\n◦\n◦"],[46774,"Chapter 10 254\nClick Publish Agent\nIn the publishing dialog, click Publish again, then select Publish to Teams and\nMicrosoft 365 Copilot\nIn the Microsoft 365 publishing window:\nUnder Azure Bot Service, select Create an Azure Bot Service\nUnder Distribution scope, select Organization\nNote the Application ID and Tenant ID generated. Save both values.\nClick Publish. Wait for the portal to confirm status Published (1–2 minutes).\nThe RBAC assignment is now complete. The agent's managed identity has the Azure AI User\nrole on the knowledge base, which is the minimum permission required for retrieval at\nruntime. With idx_d4f49e97idx_dd2fa022 identity-based access configured, you are ready to enable tracing so that\nproduction traffic can be monitored and analyzed.\nReassign RBAC to the agent identity\nFollow idx_321ab81aidx_f0e0d681 these steps to approve the agent in the Microsoft 365 Admin Center:\nIn the Azure portal (https://portal.azure.com), navigate to the vehicle-support-kb\nAzure AI Search resource\nClick Access control (IAM), then + Add, and then Add role assignment\nIn the Role dropdown, select Azure AI User\nUnder Members, click + Select members. Search for the Application ID noted in the\nprevious section and select it.\nClick Review + assign. Confirm the assignment appears in the Role assignments tab.\nThe agent is now live for the selected user group in Teams and Microsoft 365 Copilot. Users can\nstart conversations with vehicle ‑ support ‑ assistant directly from Teams or through the\nCopilot interface in Microsoft 365. All conversations are traced to Application Insights, giving\nyou the observability you need to monitor quality and catch issues early.\nEnable tracing and monitoring\nFollow these steps to idx_4bc9a356idx_b7b88a0b enable tracing on the agent:\nClick on the Operate tab in the main menu, then in the left pane, click Tracing\nClick Enable tracing for the vehicle-support-assistant agent application\nUnder Destination, select Application Insights"],[46783,"Chapter 10 256\nMulti-region deployment: If your call center operates across geographies, a single\nAzure region introduces latency and availability risk. Microsoft Foundry supports\ndeploying the same agent configuration to multiple regions. Each region gets its own\nmodel deployment, but both can point to the same knowledge base if it is hosted in a\nglobally accessible resource. Alternatively, replicate the knowledge base per region for\nlower retrieval latency in high-volume scenarios.\nSession management at scale: The agent as configured is stateless between\nconversations — each call starts fresh. For a call center where an agent might handle a\nfollow-up call from the same customer, consider passing relevant session context in the\nsystem prompt dynamically. This avoids the agent asking for information the customer\nalready provided in a previous interaction.\nCost optimization\nRunning a idx_46206921idx_9acaa41d production AI system has real cost implications across three dimensions: inference,\nretrieval, and monitoring.\nInference cost is driven by token consumption per conversation — the system prompt,\nthe retrieved document chunks, and the response all count. The system prompt in this\nchapter is approximately 300 tokens. Every retrieval adds chunks on top of that. A long\nconversation with multiple retrievals can easily reach 4,000–6,000 tokens per turn. To\ncontrol this: keep the system prompt as concise as possible without sacrificing\nprecision, limit the number of chunks returned per retrieval query (Foundry IQ allows\nconfiguring retrieval depth), and set a maximum conversation turn limit for the agent.\nRetrieval idx_2e0c36ebidx_a40fd0c6 cost scales with the size of your knowledge base and the frequency of re-\nindexing. Re-indexing every document every night when only one file changed is\nwasteful. Configure incremental indexing — only documents that have been updated\nsince the last run should be re-processed. This is especially relevant when the\nknowledge base grows beyond a few dozen files.\nEvaluation cost is easy to overlook. Each evaluation run consumes tokens from both\nthe target model and the judge model. A full evaluation run against 80 prompts with\nfive metrics can cost as much as several hundred API calls. Run full evaluations on\nrelease events; use lighter targeted runs (20–30 prompts) for routine change\nvalidation.\nA practical starting point would be to instrument your Application Insights dashboard to track\naverage tokens per conversation and cost per session. This gives you a baseline from day one\nand makes cost anomalies visible before they accumulate.\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n257 Building Assistants and Copilots\nManaging the knowledge base over time\nThe knowledge base is not a idx_c6856d89idx_bf869322 static asset. Warranty policies get updated, recall notices expire,"],[26567,"Chapter 10 258\nWhy AI threat modeling differs\nIn a conventional idx_928fd125idx_0af0f716 Linux service the attack surface is bounded by the code you ship, the ports\nyou expose, and the credentials you manage. An LLM-powered agent adds three qualitatively\nnew surfaces:\nThe language interface. Natural language inputs are Turing-complete attack vectors. A\nsingle carefully crafted sentence can redirect an agent's behavior far more effectively\nthan a buffer overflow, and it requires no code execution on the attacker's part.\nThe retrieval context. RAG pipelines pull external documents into the model's context\nwindow. A poisoned runbook, a tampered log file, or a malicious Confluence page\nbecomes executable influence on the agent's next action.\nThe model artifact itself. Fine-tuned weights encode operational knowledge and\npotentially proprietary data. Unauthorized access to model files exposes intellectual\nproperty; model poisoning during fine-tuning can introduce latent backdoors that\nactivate on trigger phrases.\nApplying STRIDE to AI components\nSTRIDE: Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information idx_506f8d1fidx_62a31e10 Disclosure, Denial of Service,\nElevation of Privilege, remains the most portable threat categorisation framework available.\nThe table below maps each STRIDE category to its AI-specific manifestation and the primary\ntechnical control that addresses it.\nSTRIDE Category AI-Specific Manifestation Primary Control\nS — Spoofing\nImpersonate agent identity\nor model endpoint\nmTLS between services,\nsigned JWT for agent calls\nT — Tampering\nModify prompt, retrieved\ncontext, or model output in\ntransit\nTLS 1.3, response hashing,\nsealed context objects\nR — Repudiation\nDeny executing a destructive\noperation\nImmutable signed audit\nlogs, per-action attestation\nI — Information Disclosure\nExpose PII, secrets, or\ninternal topology via LLM\noutputs\nPII redaction, output\nfiltering, egress\nNetworkPolicy\n•\n•\n•\n259 Security, Privacy, and Guardrails for Production AI\nSTRIDE Category AI-Specific Manifestation Primary Control\nD — Denial of Service\nExhaust token budget, GPU\nmemory, or rate limits\nToken caps, request queuing,\ncircuit breakers, rate limits\nE — Elevation of Privilege\nAgent gains root or cluster-\nadmin through tool misuse\nOPA policies, non-root\ncontainers, seccomp, RBAC\nTable 10.1: Applying STRIDE to AI components\nAI threat inventory\nThe following idx_83ad131didx_7f6366cd inventory consolidates the highest-priority threats for a Linux/Kubernetes AI\ndeployment. Severity ratings assume a regulated environment where agent actions can affect\nproduction infrastructure.\nThreat Severity Description Primary Control\nPrompt Injection High\nAttacker embeds\ninstructions in user\ninput or retrieved\ndocs that hijack\nagent actions\nInput sanitization,\noutput schema\nvalidation,"],[46785,"Chapter 10 258\nWorking through this chapter gave you hands-on experience with the full Microsoft Foundry\nbuild lifecycle: scoping a use case with clear boundaries, preparing and indexing documents,\nconfiguring an agent with grounded instructions and safety controls, validating behavior\nthrough structured evaluations, and deploying to a real production environment with\nmonitoring in place. More importantly, you now understand why each of these steps matters\n— not just how to perform them, but what each one contributes to a reliable, compliant AI\nsolution.\nIn the next chapter, let's see how to build end-to-end agent solutions consuming data from\nMicrosoft Fabric, representing the historical data repository for a company.\nGet this book's PDF version and more\nScan the QR code (or go to packtpub.com/unlock). Search for this book by name, confirm the\nedition, and then follow the steps on the page.\nNote: Keep your invoice handy. Purchases made directly from Packt don't require an invoice.\n259 Building Assistants and Copilots\n11\nCreating Custom Copilots with\nMicrosoft Fabric\nIn this chapter, we will build a customer-facing banking assistant that helps users get answers\nabout their bank accounts, products, and services. The agent runs inside Microsoft Foundry\nand connects to a Microsoft Fabric instance that holds structured data about customer\nfinancial information — account balances, active products, transaction history, and service\nstatus. Like in the previous chapters, everything is configured through the new Microsoft\nFoundry portal, with Python only where the portal cannot cover a specific step. By the end of\nthis chapter, you will have a fully deployed agent that retrieves live financial data safely,\nenforces strict identity and data minimization rules, and operates within the compliance\nboundaries that a regulated banking environment requires.\nWhat makes this use case different from others is the nature of the data involved. Financial\ninformation is among the most sensitive data a customer can share — and among the most\nregulated. That means every design decision in this chapter, from how the agent asks for an\nidentifier to how it formats a balance response, is shaped by the need to minimize data\nexposure, prevent unauthorized access, and maintain a clear and auditable boundary between\nwhat the agent can do and what it cannot.\nThe chapter follows the same structure as the previous ones. We start by defining the scope\nand assumptions, then configure the Microsoft Foundry workspace, connect Microsoft Fabric\nas the data source, build and configure the agent, add guardrails and compliance controls, run\nevaluations against a banking-specific test dataset, and finally deploy and monitor the agent in\nproduction.\nWe will cover the topics under the following main headings:"],[26569,"Chapter 10 260\nThreat Severity Description Primary Control\nData Leakage via\nLLM\nHigh\nSensitive log or\nconfig data included\nin prompts leaks to\nthird-party LLM\nAPIs\nPII redaction before\nprompt\nconstruction, local\nmodel preference for\nsensitive data\nPrivilege Escalation Critical\nAgent tool executes\na command that\ngrants elevated\naccess beyond the\nintended scope\nAllow-list tooling,\nOPA policy\nenforcement, non-\nroot execution,\nseccomp\nJailbreak/Bypass Medium\nCrafted prompt\nbypasses safety\nguardrails and\ncauses the agent to\nexecute prohibited\nactions\nSystem-prompt\nhardening, output\nclassification, CI\nred-team regression\nsuite\nInsecure Secret\nHandling\nHigh\nAPI keys or\ncredentials passed\nvia env vars or\nlogged in plain text\nVault/KMS injection,\nsecret redaction\nfrom logs, read-only\nmounts\nAudit Trail\nTampering\nMedium\nAgent logs modified\nto conceal\nunauthorized\noperations\nImmutable append-\nonly log storage,\nsigned log entries,\nSIEM integration\nTable 10.2: List of highest-priority threats\nThreat modeling in python: automated asset discovery\nThreat models idx_86528af9idx_7885bc62 decay as fast as the systems they describe. The following module generates a\nliving threat register by inspecting the running Kubernetes cluster and emitting structured\nfindings that feed directly into your risk management workflow.\nPython threat_model/scanner.py\n261 Security, Privacy, and Guardrails for Production AI\nUse case: Threat modeling a regulated log summarizer\nConsider a idx_de616640idx_c1e9f0ff log summarisation agent that reads /var/log/audit.log and generates compliance\nreports. The threat model for this component must address four distinct concerns: the\nsensitivity of the data being processed, the LLM API endpoint it contacts, the output it\nproduces, and the downstream systems that consume that output.\nData sensitivity: audit.log contains user IDs, IP addresses, and command histories:\nPII under GDPR and audit evidence under SOC 2. The agent must redact identifiers\nbefore constructing the LLM prompt.\nModel endpoint: Sending raw audit logs to a third-party API violates data residency\nrequirements. The threat model mandates a locally hosted model or a private endpoint\nwith data processing agreements in place.\nOutput integrity: Generated compliance reports must be signed and timestamped so\nthey cannot be tampered with after creation. A malicious insider who modifies a report\ncould conceal a security incident.\nConsumer trust: Downstream SIEM integrations must verify report provenance before\ningesting AI-generated findings. An unsigned report should be rejected automatically.\nEach idx_aa22bee5idx_aec3015e concern maps directly to controls implemented in the sections that follow: PII redaction,\nlocal model preference, output signing, and SIEM integration guidance.\nData and secret protection on Linux/Kubernetes"],[26571,"Chapter 10 262\nSecret management with HashiCorp vault\nEnvironment variables idx_e82d854aidx_57c86242 are the most common secret anti-pattern in containerised AI\nworkloads. Any process with access to /proc/self/environ can read them, they appear in\nKubernetes pod manifests stored in etcd, and they are trivially logged by misconfigured\nobservability agents. The correct pattern is dynamic secret injection via Vault's Kubernetes\nauth method, with secrets mounted as in-memory tmpfs volumes that disappear when the\npod terminates.\nThe code:\nHCL — Vault Agent\n# vault-agent-config.hcl — inject LLM API key as in-memory file\nauto_auth {\nmethod \"kubernetes\" {\nmount_path = \"auth/kubernetes\"\nconfig = { role = \"ai-inference\" }\n}\n}\ntemplate {\ncontents = '{{ with secret \"kv/ai/llm-api\" }}{{ .Data.data.key }}{{ end }}'\ndestination = \"/vault/secrets/llm_api_key\"\nperms = \"0400\"\n}\nYAML — Kubernetes Deployment\n# deployment.yaml (relevant extracts)\nspec:\ntemplate:\nmetadata:\nannotations:\nvault.hashicorp.com/agent-inject: \"true\"\nvault.hashicorp.com/role: \"ai-inference\"\nvault.hashicorp.com/agent-inject-secret-llm_key: \"kv/ai/llm-api\"\nspec:\nvolumes:\n- name: secrets\nemptyDir:\n263 Security, Privacy, and Guardrails for Production AI\nmedium: Memory # never written to disk\ncontainers:\n- name: inference\nvolumeMounts:\n- name: secrets\nmountPath: /vault/secrets\nreadOnly: true\nsecurityContext:\nrunAsNonRoot: true\nrunAsUser: 1001\nreadOnlyRootFilesystem: true\nallowPrivilegeEscalation: false\ncapabilities:\ndrop: [ALL]\nThe idx_e1aa97b1idx_230d2731 code:\nPython secrets/loader.py\nPII redaction before prompt construction\nThe idx_590e4b82idx_1ad006de boundary between raw operational data and LLM input is the most critical data protection\ncontrol point in an AI pipeline. Logs, configuration files, and metric payloads routinely contain\nIP addresses, usernames, email addresses, and host names that constitute personal data under\nGDPR Article 4. Sending this data to a third-party LLM endpoint without redaction is a\nreportable data breach in most jurisdictions—not just a security concern but a legal liability.\nSecurity: PII in audit logs\nAudit logs for idx_faa2894aidx_cd242b07 AI systems inherit the sensitivity of the data they record. A log entry that\ncaptures a raw prompt or model response may contain the same PII that the redaction pipeline\nwas designed to suppress. Before shipping AI audit logs to any external system — SIEM,\nobservability platform, or incident management tool — apply the same redaction pipeline\nused for prompt construction (Section 10.2.2). Enforce least-privilege access to raw log stores:\non-call engineers should read redacted views; raw logs should require explicit break-glass\nauthorization with a logged justification. Treat an audit log containing unredacted PII with the\nsame incident classification as a data breach."],[26573,"Chapter 10 264\nEgress-Restricted network policy\nA model idx_1be01b48idx_9a0b99be container that can open arbitrary TCP connections is a potential exfiltration channel\n— not because the model intends to leak data, but because a prompt injection attack could\ncause the agent to call an endpoint that the attacker controls. Kubernetes NetworkPolicy\nprovides a declarative allow-list that the kernel enforces regardless of what the application\nlayer attempts.\nYAML — NetworkPolicy\n# network-policy.yaml — restrict AI workload egress to approved endpoints only\napiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: NetworkPolicy\nmetadata:\nname: ai-inference-egress\nnamespace: ai-system\nspec:\npodSelector:\nmatchLabels:\napp.kubernetes.io/component: inference\npolicyTypes: [Egress]\negress:\n# Internal model registry (read model weights)\n- to:\n- namespaceSelector:\nmatchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: model-registry}\nports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 5000}]\n# Vault sidecar (secret injection)\n- to:\n- namespaceSelector:\nmatchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: vault}\nWhy Consistent Hashing Matters\nUsing a deterministic hash (SHA-256 of salt + value) means the same IP address always\nproduces the same token within a deployment. This lets the LLM reason about \"the\nsame host appearing in multiple log lines\" without ever seeing the actual IP address.\nChange the salt between deployments to ensure tokens cannot be mapped across\nenvironments—a production token table should never decode logs from staging.\nNote\n265 Security, Privacy, and Guardrails for Production AI\nports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 8200}]\n# Prometheus push gateway (metrics)\n- to:\n- namespaceSelector:\nmatchLabels: {kubernetes.io/metadata.name: monitoring}\nports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 9091}]\n# DNS (required for internal service discovery)\n- ports: [{protocol: UDP, port: 53}]\n# All other egress is implicitly denied\nThis policy ensures that even if an adversary successfully injects a prompt that instructs the\nagent to POST data to an external webhook, the kernel will drop the packet before it leaves the\nnode. NetworkPolicy is a last-resort control — you should also validate outputs and block tool\ncalls to unapproved URLs — but it provides defense-in-depth that operates independently of\napplication logic.\nUse case: Compliant log summarization in a regulated\nenvironment\nThe idx_f588e165idx_3c6dc83e following end-to-end example assembles the controls from Sections 10.2.1 through 10.2.3\ninto a single pipeline function. This pattern is appropriate for healthcare, financial services, or\nany environment where log data contains personal information subject to regulatory\nprotection.\nPython pipelines/compliant_summariser.py\nBest Practice: Egress Controls\nCombine NetworkPolicy (kernel-level enforcement) with an application-layer allow-\nlist in your tool dispatcher (Section 10.3). Neither control alone is sufficient:"],[26575,"Chapter 10 266\nGuardrails and policies for LLMs and agents\nGuardrails are idx_b70f2eddidx_6412b7e0 the software equivalent of a permit-to-work system: they define the boundary\nbetween what an agent is allowed to do autonomously and what requires human approval or\nis prohibited outright. Unlike idx_93107077idx_bfcc51f5 network policies — which operate at the kernel level —\nguardrails operate at the application layer, evaluating the semantic content of tool calls, LLM\noutputs, and agent decisions before they produce effects on the system.\nBefore implementing any guardrail, classify every tool the agent can invoke into one of two\ntiers.\nRead-only tools: safe to idx_c731ce73idx_88c9a18e execute without human approval: disk_usage,\nservice_status, log_tail, metric_query, config_read. These tools observe\nsystem state but cannot modify it; the blast radius of a misbehaving agent is limited to\ninformation disclosure.\nWrite-capable tools: require idx_d2cf00f2idx_2324f7a8 dry-run, diff, and explicit operator approval before\nexecution: config_write, service_restart, deploy_apply, secret_rotate,\nfirewall_update. A single misbehaving invocation can cause an outage or data loss.\nEnforce this classification in the OPA allow-list (Section 10.3.3) and surface it in the\nrunbook (Section 10.5.4). Any tool that transitions from read to write scope — even\ntemporarily — should trigger a policy review and a regression run.\nSystem prompts, few-shot examples, and idx_fc13abe2idx_f2e1d795 output schemas are executable artifacts with the\nsame risk profile as application code. Apply the same engineering discipline:\nVersion control: store all prompt templates in the repository alongside the code that\nreferences them. A prompt change without a corresponding commit is an untracked\nconfiguration change.\nCode review: require peer review for prompt modifications — specifically reviewing for\ninjection vectors, scope creep, and alignment with the model card's stated use-case\nboundaries.\nRegression gating: run the full injection regression suite (Section 10.5.2) on every prompt\nedit. A prompt change that reduces injection resilience below threshold is a merge\nblocker, not an advisory.\nStructured outputs: pair every prompt with a Pydantic or JSON Schema output contract.\nThe prompt instructs the model on format; the schema enforces it before any\ndownstream action executes.\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n267 Security, Privacy, and Guardrails for Production AI\nHard-coded maximum token counts become brittle as context requirements grow. When a\nworkload's context routinely approaches the model's context window, apply the following\nmitigations in order:\nChunking: split long documents into overlapping segments and process each\nindependently before aggregating results.\nRetrieval-augmented generation (RAG): replace full-document inclusion with semantic"],[26578,"Chapter 10 268\nThe code:\nPython guardrails/injection_detector.py\nOutput validation with JSON schema\nStructured output idx_1a557d1aidx_d7eac361 contracts are one of the highest-leverage guardrails idx_627dd861idx_7a749754 available because they\nare both cheap to enforce and highly effective at preventing unstructured harmful outputs.\nWhen an agent's tool calls return structured JSON and the model is instructed to respond in a\ndefined schema, any response that does not conform is rejected before it can cause an effect —\nregardless of what injection attempts the input may have contained.\nPython guardrails/output_validator.py\nOPA policy enforcement for tool calls\nOpen Policy Agent (OPA) provides idx_340c17afidx_16b86047 a Turing-complete policy language (Rego) that can evaluate\nthe full context of an agent's intended tool call — including the command, arguments, timing,\nand the agent's stated reasoning — against organisational policy before execution. Unlike\nhard-coded if/else guardrails in application code, OPA policies are declarative, version-\ncontrolled, and independently testable, making them suitable for regulated environments\nwhere policies must be auditable artefacts."],[26580,"Chapter 10 270\nsensitive_paths := {\"/etc/shadow\", \"/etc/sudoers\", \"/root\",\n\"/proc/keys\", \"/sys/firmware\"}\nallow if {\ninput.tool in allowed_tools\nnot path_is_sensitive\nnot outside_maintenance_window\ninput.risk_level in {\"low\", \"medium\"}\n}\n# High-risk actions always require human approval\nrequires_approval if {\ninput.risk_level == \"high\"\n}\npath_is_sensitive if {\nsome path in sensitive_paths\nstartswith(input.args.path, path)\n}\noutside_maintenance_window if {\nnot input.in_maintenance_window\ninput.risk_level != \"low\"\n}\ndeny[msg] if {\nnot input.tool in allowed_tools\nmsg := sprintf(\"Tool %q not in allow-list\", [input.tool])\n}\nPython guardrails/opa_client.py\nUse case: Stepwise authorization for change automation\nChange automation — automatically idx_a7f3d847idx_52876794 applying configuration changes to production Linux\nsystems based on AI recommendations — is one of the highest-value and highest-risk AI use\ncases in operations. The following orchestrator assembles injection detection, output\nvalidation, and OPA enforcement into a coherent change pipeline that implements stepwise\n271 Security, Privacy, and Guardrails for Production AI\nauthorization: each action is evaluated independently, and the pipeline stops at the first policy\nviolation or approval requirement.\nStop conditions and rollback\nBefore idx_f1ca7e70idx_3299d783 any write-capable step executes, define the conditions under which the pipeline must\nhalt without completing, and the rollback action that returns the system to its last known-\ngood state.\nStop conditions:\nOPA policy returns DENY for any step in the plan\nThe schema validator raises OutputValidationError\nThe diff between the proposed change and the current state exceeds a configurable size\nthreshold (default: 50 changed lines)\nHuman approval is not received within the approval timeout (default: 300 s)\nRollback pattern:\nEvery idx_8d104d7fidx_d528fe92 write-capable step should emit a compensating action before execution. For example:\nkubectl apply records the previous manifest; config_write snapshots the current file content;\nservice_restart preserves the prior unit state. If a stop condition is triggered mid-plan, the\npipeline executes compensating actions in reverse order. A plan with no recoverable rollback\npath for any step must be classified as requires_approval=True regardless of OPA output.\nPython orchestrator/change_pipeline.py\nCompliance, audit, and model governance\nCompliance is idx_9e539390idx_067a1efa the organisational evidence layer that proves your technical controls actually\nwork. For AI systems it has three dimensions: the immutable audit trail that records every\nsignificant agent decision, the model governance artefacts (model cards, dataset cards,\napproval records) that document what the model can and cannot do, and the operational"],[26583,"Chapter 10 272\nObservability for AI audit systems\nAn audit log idx_4024b4beidx_1588a4a2 that is written but never monitored provides integrity without visibility.\nInstrument three telemetry layers for every production AI workload:\nLogs: emit structured JSON with at minimum request_id, agent_id, model_id,\nevent_type, token_count, latency_ms, and policy_decision. Route to a SIEM\nwith retention >= 90 days (or as required by your compliance framework).\nMetrics: export Prometheus gauges and histograms for p50/p95/p99 inference latency,\nrequest error rate, token throughput, guardrail trigger rate (per signal type), and audit\nchain verification status. Alert on: error_rate > 1%, p99_latency > 5 s,\nguardrail_trigger_rate spike > 3 sigma over a 1-hour window.\nTraces: instrument the full request path with OpenTelemetry spans — from API\ngateway receipt through LLM call, tool invocation, and audit write. A trace that ends at\nthe LLM boundary leaves tool calls invisible to incident response.\nThe code:\nPython audit/logger.py\nModel cards and dataset cards\nA model card is idx_8b90b8e2idx_6dbcfbef the operational contract between the team that trains or deploys a model and\nthe teams that use it. It documents idx_5624e010idx_ace5c65a what the model is designed to do, what it must not do,\nwhat populations or input types it may behave poorly on, and what monitoring is required to\ndetect drift. In a regulated environment, the model card is an auditable artifact — a reviewer\n•\n•\n•\nShip Audit Logs to Immutable Storage\nWriting to a local JSONL file is sufficient for integrity verification, but a compromised\nnode could delete the file entirely. Forward audit entries in real time to an append-only\nSIEM (Splunk, OpenSearch with index-level write-once settings, or AWS CloudTrail-\nequivalent) so chain verification can be performed from storage that the agent process\ncannot reach.\nRun verify_chain() as a Kubernetes CronJob every 15 minutes and alert on any\nbroken link.\nTip\n273 Security, Privacy, and Guardrails for Production AI\nasking \"was this model appropriate for this task?\" needs to find a clear, signed document\nanswering that question.\nPython governance/model_card.py\nUse case: Audited ChatOps for SRE\nChatOps, using a idx_1b9021c3idx_93d7af3a messaging interface (Slack, Teams, Mattermost) to trigger idx_580f3fe0idx_6d8844ba operational\nactions, dramatically accelerates incident response. Adding an AI layer to ChatOps means the\nAI can interpret natural language requests, translate them into structured tool calls, and\nexecute them through the guardrail stack. The audit trail then provides a complete record of\nevery action taken during an incident, including the original natural language request, the AI's\ninterpretation, the policy decision, and the execution result.\nRate limiting: Apply idx_149c8f13idx_d7beaea2 per-user and per-channel rate limits to prevent accidental or"],[26585,"Chapter 10 274\nsecurity incident because it demonstrates a successful attack path that a more destructive\npayload could exploit.\nClass AI-1 (Critical): Agent executed a destructive or unauthorised action as a result of\ninjection, jailbreak, or policy bypass. Immediate halt of the agent, chain-of-custody\npreservation of audit logs, and escalation to security leadership required.\nClass AI-2 (High): Agent was successfully injected but the action was blocked by a\nguardrail. The guardrail worked, but the attack path is now known. Requires root cause\nanalysis and regression test within 24 hours.\nClass AI-3 (Medium): Model produced outputs that violated the model card's\nout_of_scope list without being triggered by injection. Requires model card review and\npossibly retraining.\nClass AI-4 (Low): Output quality degradation detected by evaluation metrics (F1\nbelow threshold, schema validation failures above baseline). Requires threshold review\nand possible model update.\nPrompt injection regression suite\nThe most idx_cdd82b8eidx_d7454768 efficient red-team investment for an LLM-powered system is a library of known\ninjection payloads that runs automatically on every model update, prompt template change, or\nsystem prompt modification. The following test harness treats prompt injection tests the same\nway a software team treats unit tests: a regression is a blocker, not a warning.\nPython tests/test_injection_resilience.py\n•\n•\n•\n•\nRegression Suite Governance Rules\nEvery AI-2 or higher incident must produce a new entry in INJECTION_PAYLOADS\nbefore the incident is closed. This converts real-world attacks into permanent test\ncoverage.\nRun the full suite in CI on every merge to main that touches the system prompt, the\nmodel version pin, the prompt template library, or the guardrails module. Failures\nblock merge — no exceptions.\nNote\n275 Security, Privacy, and Guardrails for Production AI\nContinuous Red-Teaming with automated adversarial probing\nA regression idx_dde78b1eidx_7bc804f1 suite tests known attacks. Continuous red-teaming discovers unknown ones. The\nfollowing module implements automated adversarial probing: it uses a second LLM instance\nto generate novel injection variants and tests them against the production system prompt,\nlogging any successes as new regression candidates.\nPython redteam/adversarial_probe.py\nIncident response runbook for AI events\nThe following runbook idx_20ad10bbidx_d274322e structures the first 60 minutes of response to a Class AI-1 or AI-2\nincident. It is designed to be followed by an SRE who may not be an AI specialist; the steps are\ndeterministic and do not require understanding of model internals.\nT+0: Immediate containment. Scale the affected agent Deployment to zero replicas:\nkubectl scale deploy/ --replicas=0 -n ai-system. Preserve the Pod logs before they are"],[86380,"Chapter 10 276\nELBs can distribute traffic across multiple EC2 instances, containers, IP addresses, and even\nLambda functions. If a particular EC2 instance fails, for example, the load balancer will redirect\ntraffic to other healthy instances in the fleet.\nAmazon load balancers detect failed instances by performing health checks—ongoing checks\non instances and the applications running on them to verify they are healthy and responsive.\nThe load balancer only routes traffic to instances that pass these health checks, indicating they\nare live and operational.\nAmazon Elastic Load Balancers are regional services that can route traffic to resources across\nmultiple AZs within a region. You deploy your load balancer in a VPC and specify which AZs\nwill host your load balancer nodes. The following section explains how to deploy your load\nbalancer in a VPC.\nBut before exploring the different types of AWS Load Balancers, it's helpful to understand the\nOpen Systems Interconnection (OSI) model, which is a framework used to describe how data\ntravels across a network.\nOverview of the OSI model\nPublished in 1984, the OSI model outlines the process idx_0fe3dda5 by which data flows through a network\nsystem. The OSI model is divided idx_f3757a00 into seven layers, with each layer responsible for a specific\npart of the network communication process and utilizing various protocols at that layer.\nDifferent hardware and software components that enable data transfer operate at varying\nlayers of the OSI model. For example, packet-filtering firewalls, which inspect IP addresses,\nports, and transport layer protocols such as TCP and UDP, operate at Layer 3 (Network\nLayer) and Layer 4 (Transport Layer). In contrast, application firewalls that perform content\nfiltering operate at Layer 7 (Application Layer).\n277 High Availability, Fault Tolerance, and Elasticity on AWS\nFigure 10.1 illustrates the seven layers of the OSI model:\nFigure 10.1: OSI Model\nThe OSI model helps developers, system idx_1be27768 engineers, and architects understand how\ncommunication flows between idx_532d4fab an end user and an application. It also helps when deciding\nwhere different security protocols and controls should be implemented across the networking\nstack.\nThe following table illustrates which service you will consume and what layer of the OSI model\nit operates at:\nArchitecture Component OSI Layer\nRoute53/DNS Layer 7\nLoad Balancers Layer 7\nSecurity Groups Layer 3/4\nVPC networking Layer 3"],[26589,"Chapter 10 276\nGold set: maintain a minimum of 50 representative prompt/expected-output pairs spanning\nnormal operations, edge cases, and known injection variants. Expand the gold set with every\nnew incident.\nRegression gate: run the full gold set on every model upgrade, guardrail modification, or\nprompt template change. Gate the merge on a passing rate >= 95%. A drop below the threshold\nis a blocker, not a warning.\nDrift detection: export pass rate and per-class confidence distribution as Prometheus metrics.\nAlert if the 7-day rolling pass rate drops more than 5 percentage points—this indicates either\nmodel drift or an emerging attack class not yet covered by the gold set.\nFailure modes and fallback strategies\nProduction idx_fa067e73idx_2dff39ef AI workloads fail in ways that differ qualitatively from deterministic services.\nAddress the following failure modes in your incident runbook before go-live:\nLLM provider idx_ff9f3240idx_5483860c unavailability: the upstream model endpoint returns 5xx or connection errors.\nMitigation: implement exponential back-off with circuit-breaker semantics; route to a\nsecondary endpoint or a smaller local model (Ollama) if the primary is unavailable for > 30\nseconds. Never allow the pipeline to block indefinitely on model calls.\nHallucinated tool arguments: the model generates a syntactically valid but semantically\nincorrect tool invocation; for example, targeting a production host with a destructive\ncommand. Mitigation: JSON Schema validation and OPA evaluation together catch most cases;\nthe dry_run=True default ensures unreviewed write actions produce diffs rather than changes.\nSchema validation failure spikes: OutputValidationError is raised at higher-than-baseline\nrates, indicating model drift or prompt corruption. Mitigation: log all validation failures with\nthe raw response; alert on failure rate > 5% over a 5-minute window; halt the pipeline and\nqueue responses for human review until the failure rate drops.\nToken budget exhaustion: the prompt exceeds the model's context window. Mitigation: apply\nthe chunking and RAG strategies from Section 10.3; set a hard token budget in the OPA policy\n(Table 10.3) and alert when utilization exceeds 80%.\nOPA policy false positives: a legitimate tool call is denied because the policy is overly\nrestrictive, causing an operational outage. Mitigation: run opa test policies/ in CI on every\npolicy change; maintain a representative set of legitimate tool calls as policy test fixtures\nalongside the adversarial set.\nKey takeaways\nFollowing are key takeaways from this chapter:\nAI Threat Models Require New Categories •\n277 Security, Privacy, and Guardrails for Production AI\nSTRIDE is necessary but not sufficient for AI workloads. Prompt injection, model\nsupply-chain poisoning, and jailbreaks require explicit threat entries with AI-specific"],[98386,"Chapter 10 277\nAt the time of writing, there are generally two ways to “vibe code.” The first is to integrate the\ntool inside your Integrated Development Environment (IDE); the other, newer approach is to\nintegrate the tool in the terminal. Using the IDE is more familiar to folks who already use IDEs\nto write code, while the terminal is more universal (for example, it can be found on your mobile\nphone). We will illustrate both approaches, using GitHub Copilot with Visual Studio (VS) Code\nfor the first approach and Anthropic Claude Code for the second approach.\nThis field is quickly evolving, so please check out the following resources for the latest pricing and\ninstallation options if you find them to be different compared to what’s mentioned in this chapter:\n• GitHub Copilot: https://github.com/features/copilot\n• Anthropic Claude Code: https://www.anthropic.com/claude-code\nLet’s start by setting up GitHub Copilot."],[86381,"Chapter 10 278\nArchitecture Component OSI Layer\nEC2 application Layer 7\nRDS connectivity Layer 4/7\nTable 10.1: AWS Services and related OSI layers\nUnderstanding the OSI model will help you appreciate the architectural decisions made when\nbuilding the Belly Brew Co. Limited customer-facing idx_53b31992 website. As you work through this\nproject, you will see how each AWS service and resource plays a role in enabling users on the\nInternet to submit their email address idx_0955f28c and receive a copy of the recipe eBook. This includes the\nnetworking components, security firewalls, application processing, and backend database\nconnectivity.\nNow that you have seen where load balancing fits within the wider networking stack, let's look\nat how Elastic Load Balancers are deployed inside a VPC and connected to the resources that\nserve your application.\nConnecting Elastic Load Balancers to VPC resources\nAmazon Load Balancers are idx_9485a29d deployed within your VPC and distribute traffic across multiple\nresources, such as EC2 instances. You can configure the load balancer to support various AZs\nwithin the region and define whether it is internet-facing or internal-facing. The key differences\nbetween the two options are as follows:\nInternet-facing ELBs: Accept inbound traffic idx_d3a5091a from the internet and distribute it to\nresources deployed in either public or private subnets\nInternal-facing ELBs: Handle traffic idx_c004c222 originating from within your VPC, such as from\nresources in public subnets, and distribute it to resources located in private subnets\n•\n•\n279 High Availability, Fault Tolerance, and Elasticity on AWS\nFigure 10.2 illustrates an idx_97d72b1e example of an Internet-facing load balancer with load balancer nodes\ndeployed across public subnets in Availability Zones eu ‑ west ‑ 2a and eu ‑ west ‑ 2b.\nFigure 10.2: Application Load Balancer distributing traffic to EC2 instances in app subnets\nAs depicted in the diagram, an application load balancer (ALB) is deployed as an internet-\nfacing load balancer. Here, you must select the idx_179ec867 public subnets within which AWS will deploy\nmanaged application load balancer nodes that will distribute inbound internet traffic within\nthe designated AZs to EC2 instances in the private subnets. These EC2 instances might host the\nfront-end web servers, for example.\nInternal load balancers further distribute the traffic that reaches your web servers to backend\nresources such as middleware idx_3fe11ed5 or app servers. As depicted in Figure 10.3, traffic from your web\nservers needs to be distributed to backend application servers, and here an internal load\nbalancer can help manage the even distribution to backend servers."],[26591,"Chapter 10 278\nControl Verification\nThreat register generated by scanner.py and\ncommitted to GitOps repo\nOpen critical findings = 0 in CI gate\nSTRIDE analysis completed for each AI\ncomponent boundary\nDocumented in threat_register.json\nVault/KMS secret injection configured; env\nvars contain no credentials\nkubectl exec env | grep -i key returns empty\nPII redaction pipeline applied to all log data\nbefore prompt construction\nredaction_count > 0 in\ncompliant_summariser output\nEgress NetworkPolicy applied; only approved\ndestinations whitelisted\nkubectl describe netpol ai-inference-egress\nshows deny-all default\nInjection detector integrated into all LLM\nresponse processing paths\nUnit tests cover all _INJECTION_SIGNALS\npatterns\nAgentPlan/AgentAction Pydantic schemas\nvalidated before any tool execution\nOutputValidationError raised and logged on\nschema violation\nOPA policy deployed; agent tool allow-list\nenforced before execution\nopa test policies/passes in CI\ndry_run defaults to True; explicit false\nrequired for real-world actions\nCode review gate: no default dry_run=False\nmerges\nAuditLogger with SHA-256 chaining\ndeployed; verify_chain() runs as CronJob\nCronJob alert fires on broken chain\nModel card created, signed, and stored in\nmodel registry for each model\nmodel_card.sign() output committed\nalongside model weights\nChatOps integration includes per-user rate\nlimits and namespace scope binding\nRate limit metrics exported to Prometheus\nAI incident classification added to existing\nrunbook (AI-1 through AI-4)\nOn-call rotation trained on AI-specific\nresponse steps\n279 Security, Privacy, and Guardrails for Production AI\nControl Verification\nInjection regression suite runs in CI on every\nprompt/model/guardrail change\npytest -m injection_resilience passes = merge\ngate\nrun_red_team_session() scheduled weekly;\nfindings reviewed within 24 hours\nFindings count exported as Prometheus\ngauge; alert if > 0 unreviewed\nTable 10.5: Production readiness checklist\nSummary\nSecurity for production AI is not a single control or a single phase of the development lifecycle:\nit is a continuous practice that spans threat modeling, infrastructure hardening, application-\nlayer guardrails, compliance governance, and adversarial testing. This chapter has equipped\nyou with a complete, deployable security stack for AI workloads on Linux and Kubernetes.\nThe next chapter moves from securing individual AI workloads to examining how\norganizations across different industries have assembled the controls, architectures, and\ngovernance frameworks from this book into production systems delivering measurable\nbusiness value.\nGet this book's PDF version and more\nScan the QR code (or go to https://packtpub.com/unlock). Search for this book by name,\nconfirm the edition, and then follow the steps on the page."],[98388,"Chapter 10 279\n2. VS Code has been a free product since its inception. At the time of writing, GitHub Copilot\nalso offers both a free and multiple paid tiers.\nFigure 10.2: GitHub Copilot pricing\nNetwork Security through Vibe Coding 280\n3. Open VS Code, click on the EXTENSIONS: MARKETPLACE tab, search for copilot to find\nGitHub Copilot in the list, and click Install.\nFigure 10.3: GitHub Copilot extension"],[26593,"Chapter 10 280\n11\nLooking Ahead: The Future of AI-\nDriven Linux Workflows\nThroughout this book we explored practical techniques for integrating modern AI capabilities\ninto Linux-based environments. We examined how data flows through systems, how retrieval\npipelines can provide contextual knowledge, and how agents can orchestrate tasks across\ninfrastructure components. However, learning how to build these systems is only part of the\nstory. Equally important is learning how to think about them.\nIn this context, it is important to distinguish between augmentation and autonomy:\naugmentation systems assist humans by generating insights or recommendations, while\nautonomous systems can take actions on their own, often requiring stricter validation,\nguardrails, and oversight in production environments.\nThis chapter shifts the perspective from implementation to strategy. Instead of focusing on\ntools, libraries, or step-by-step deployments, the goal is to reflect the broader implications of\nAI-driven workflows in Linux environments. Linux engineers are increasingly encountering AI-\nassisted monitoring, automated troubleshooting, intelligent configuration management, and\nagent-based orchestration. These technologies promise efficiency and new capabilities, but\nthey also introduce new operational risks and design challenges.\nThe objective is not to encourage blind adoption, nor to dismiss emerging techniques as hype.\nRather, the goal is to cultivate the mindset required to evaluate AI systems critically. Engineers\nresponsible for production systems must think about reliability, maintainability, security, and\nlong-term operational sustainability. AI systems should be evaluated under the same\nstandards.\nIn many ways, the questions raised by AI integration resemble earlier shifts in infrastructure\nengineering. The adoption of virtualization, containerization, and cloud orchestration all\nrequired engineers to reconsider established workflows. AI introduces a similar transition.\nAutomation becomes more adaptive; systems can interpret unstructured information, and\nworkflows may involve autonomous decision-making components. These capabilities can\nsignificantly expand what is possible, but they also require careful planning.\nSpecifically, we will explore:\nMoving Beyond Hype: Critical Thinking for Linux AI Workflows\nStrategic Opportunities for AI in Linux Operations\nBalancing Autonomy, Reliability, and Human Oversight\nPreparing for Multi-Agent and RAG-Enhanced Workflows\nEthical, Operational, and Long-Term Considerations\nMoving Beyond Hype: Critical Thinking for Linux AI\nWorkflows\nDiscussions idx_c772e746idx_a02b7f0e about AI often oscillate between exaggerated optimism and deep skepticism. In\noperational environments such as Linux infrastructure, neither extreme is useful. Systems"],[86382,"Chapter 10 280\nFigure 10.3: Three-tier architecture with internet-facing and internal load balancers\nTo ensure that a load balancer can receive traffic, it's essential to set up its security group with\nappropriate inbound rules. These rules must define the port, protocol, and source from which\nthe traffic is permitted. Additionally, the security groups attached to the target instances must\nallow incoming traffic from the load balancer. This is typically achieved by specifying the load\nbalancer's security group ID as the source in the targets' security group rules.\nAWS offers four types of idx_d60db1c3 elastic load balancers, which are discussed in the following\nsubsections.\nApplication Load Balancer\nAWS Application Load Balancers (ALBs) operate at the application layer (Layer 7) and are\nspecifically designed to route incoming client traffic based on content using the HTTP and\nHTTPS protocols. ALBs support distributing traffic to a variety of targets, including EC2\ninstances, containers, and AWS Lambda idx_8ae4816c functions. As illustrated in Figure 10.2 and Figure 10.3,\nyou can deploy these targets across multiple AZs, helping to ensure high availability and fault\ntolerance in the event of an AZ failure.\nSome key benefits of ALBs idx_13d0edc9 include the following:\nSupport for path-based routing, allowing idx_fa77c61f you to forward requests based on the\nUniform Resource Locator (URL) in the request\nSupport for host-based routing, enabling you to forward requests to specific targets\nbased on the host portion of the URL\n•\n•\n281 High Availability, Fault Tolerance, and Elasticity on AWS\nSupport for routing requests to multiple applications running on the same EC2\ninstance using different port numbers\nSupport for registering Lambda functions as targets, as well as containerized\napplications\nAn ALB includes a configuration idx_ec9e0b80 component called a listener, which defines how incoming\nrequests are routed to registered targets. Listener rules consist of a priority, conditions, and\nactions. When the conditions of a rule are met, the corresponding action is executed. You also\nspecify the protocol and port used to connect to your targets within the listener configuration.\nThe targets that receive routed requests are idx_2929e43b defined within target groups. An ALB can have one\nor more target groups, which can consist of EC2 instances, IP addresses (for containers), or\nLambda functions. You can also route traffic to targets located outside your VPC.\nThis flexibility in routing to idx_65ef09cf multiple target groups allows you to create complex routing\narchitectures for different components of your application. For example, for an e-commerce\nwebsite, one target group can route traffic to servers hosting your corporate pages (e.g.,\nwww.mycompany.com), while another target group handles the e-commerce section (e.g.,"],[98389,"Chapter 10 281\n4. Once installed, you should see the Copilot icon at the top. If the right panel is hidden,\nplease use the top-right corner icon to display it. Look for the chat window once the right\npanel has opened.\nFigure 10.4: Copilot panel\nNetwork Security through Vibe Coding 282\n5. In the chat window, look for the drop-down menu to choose between three different\nmodes: Agent, Ask, and Edit. We will switch between Agent and Edit.\nFigure 10.5: Configure Modes..."],[86384,"Chapter 10 282\ntarget that fails to respond is idx_9b7a9592 marked as unhealthy and is excluded from the target group. This\nensures that the ALB routes client requests only to healthy targets, helping prevent users from\nencountering errors such as \"Page cannot be found\" or similar messages.\nNext, you will learn about the AWS Network Load Balancer (NLB).\nNetwork Load Balancer\nNetwork Load Balancers (NLBs) operate at Layer 4 of the OSI model and can handle millions\nof requests per second. NLBs are idx_e343d8ad designed to load balance TCP and UDP traffic while\nmaintaining ultra-low latencies. A key feature of NLBs is their ability to preserve the source IP\naddress, allowing backend servers to see the original client IP—an essential requirement for\nspecific applications.\nNLBs also support both static IP addresses and Elastic IP addresses, the latter allowing you to\nassign a fixed IP per AZ. Unlike ALBs, NLBs do not inspect application-layer content, meaning\nthey are unaware of cookies, content types, or custom headers.\nJust like ALBs, NLBs perform health checks on registered targets and only route traffic to\ntargets that are in a healthy state.\nSome key benefits of NLBs include idx_86ff1cbb the following:\nAbility to handle highly volatile workloads and millions of requests per second\nSupport for static IP addresses and one Elastic IP address per subnet\nAbility to register targets by IP address, including targets outside your VPC (e.g., on-\npremises resources)\nSupport for routing traffic to multiple applications running on the same instance using\ndifferent ports\nSupport for containerized applications, such as those running on Amazon ECS\nNLBs are particularly well-suited idx_6dfe944f for high-performance, low-latency workloads, making them\nideal for use cases like gaming, live streaming, and high-frequency trading.\nIn the next section, you will learn more about Gateway Load Balancers.\nGateway load balancer\nGateway Load Balancers (GWLBs) help idx_c31d3b1d you deploy, scale, and manage third-party virtual\nappliances, which are typically purchased from the AWS Marketplace. These appliances are\ncommonly used for tasks such as security inspection and traffic analysis for idx_3deafa18 data entering and\nleaving your VPC. Examples idx_00c2e73d include third-party firewalls, Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS),\nand Intrusion Prevention Systems (IPS).\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n283 High Availability, Fault Tolerance, and Elasticity on AWS\nAs shown in Figure 10.5, traffic entering idx_380213a8 your VPC is first routed to these virtual appliances by\nthe GWLB. The traffic is inspected, and if it passes the checks, it is then forwarded to backend\nresources such as EC2 instances hosting your application. If the traffic does not meet the\ninspection criteria, it is dropped, and the backend resources are unaware of the presence of the"],[98390,"Chapter 10 283\n6. In the drop-down box to the right, you can also find the option to choose between different\nmodels. Feel free to choose any standard or premium models (premium models have a\nslight cost associated with them).\nFigure 10.6: Choosing a model\nNetwork Security through Vibe Coding 284\n7. Pick Ask mode and GPT-4 � 1 as the model. Then, ask a question, such as explain the\nstructure of a JSON file.\nFigure 10.7: Ask mode with GPT-4.1"],[86386,"Chapter 10 284\nWhile load balancing helps improve high availability and distribute traffic across multiple\nresources, such as EC2 instances, you still need a mechanism to scale those resources based on\ndemand automatically. That brings us to the Auto Scaling service, which provides this\ncapability and is going to be covered in the following section.\nScaling EC2 workloads with AWS Auto Scaling\nOne of the biggest challenges in deploying software solutions is effectively managing demand.\nBefore the advent of virtualization idx_40e19568 and cloud technologies, companies would cater to\nunexpected demand by following the concept of peak load provisioning. This involves\nallocating idx_8bcdd4b6 extra compute and infrastructure idx_b1efa213 resources based on the maximum expected\ndemand (or peak load) rather than typical usage. This is like investing in a bus with 50 seats\njust because once a year, all 50 might be needed, even if most days only 10 people ride it.\nWith peak load provisioning, you ensure customer satisfaction and system stability during\ntraffic spikes such as Black Friday sales or product launches. However, for most of the time, the\nextra capacity remains underutilized, leading to higher fixed and operational costs.\nAWS offers auto scaling capabilities for its idx_a3501ddc core services, enabling you to benefit from\ndynamic load provisioning. In this idx_984d7ba7 model, resources scale out or scale in based on actual\ndemand, making it a far more cost-efficient approach:\nScaling out involves provisioning additional resources, such as extra EC2 instances, to\naccommodate an increase in demand\nScaling in means terminating unwanted resources because demand is low\nAuto scaling is a regional service that idx_878ec8b7 allows you to scale across multiple AZs within a given\nregion. This not only helps idx_35a364f1 manage unexpected demand but also ensures high availability in\nthe event of an entire AZ outage. Auto scaling automatically provisions additional resources\nwhen demand crosses the specified thresholds you define. If demand drops below a defined\nthreshold, auto scaling can terminate unnecessary resources, ensuring efficient resource\nutilization and cost optimization.\nIn Figure 10.6, two additional EC2 instances were added to the fleet across AZs eu ‑ west ‑ 2a and\neu ‑ west ‑ 2b due to an increase in average CPU utilization exceeding 80% for more than 15\nminutes.\n•\n•\n285 High Availability, Fault Tolerance, and Elasticity on AWS\nFigure 10.6: Auto Scaling Group responds to a CloudWatch alarm and launches additional instances\nAuto Scaling performs idx_ac6cde3d scale-out or scale-in operations based on the threshold idx_00d6ed80 values and\nscaling policies you define. In this diagram, the auto scaling service has been configured to\nscale out if the average CPU utilization of the fleet exceeds 80% for 15 minutes or more."],[98391,"Chapter 10 285\n8. Now, switch to Agent mode and use GPT-4 � 1 as the model. Type in some instructions\nto create a virtual environment in the folder you created for this chapter – for example,\nplease change to /home/ubuntu/ai_network_cookbook/ch10 and create a Python\nvirtual environment named .venv.\n9. The agent will act on your behalf and make changes if you click on Continue after typing\nin the instructions. We can click on Cancel since we created the virtual environment\nmanually previously.\nFigure 10.8: Agent mode with GPT-4.1\nWarning: Please be careful with Agent mode; it will make changes on your\nbehalf. There are usually options to reverse them, but not always. Please\ntake extra precautions when you are in Agent mode.\nNetwork Security through Vibe Coding 286\nHow it works…\nAsk mode is similar to having an AI LLM chatbot directly in the IDE. Stick with Ask mode to begin\nwith and copy and paste the code or commands manually. Be careful with Agent mode; it will\nact on your behalf and make changes. There are usually “confirm” or “accept” options, but it is\neasier to make irreversible mistakes in Agent mode if we are not careful.\nThere’s more…\nWe can include other files and links to help with the answer. Click on the Add Context… button;\nfrom here, we can choose to include local files or other links.\nFigure 10.9: Add Context..."],[86388,"Chapter 10 286\nDesired number of EC2 instances: This represents the optimal number of instances\nyou want running at any given time. AWS strives to maintain this desired capacity. If\nthat's not possible (e.g., due to resource limitations), AWS will ensure that at least the\nminimum capacity is maintained. Note that the desired capacity can be equal to the\nminimum capacity.\nMaximum number of EC2 instances: This is the upper limit on the number of EC2\ninstances in your fleet. The auto scaling service can scale up to this number in response\nto increased demand, but will never exceed it. This cap helps prevent runaway scaling\ndue to application bugs or malicious activity, reducing the risk of service disruption or\nunexpected costs.\nThe auto scaling service launches and terminates EC2 instances based on the scaling policies\nyou define, the monitored metrics, and any alarms that are triggered. Auto scaling can also\nrespond when an instance fails a health check, automatically replacing the unhealthy instance.\nYou can use either the EC2 health checks on their own or combine them with the ELB health\nchecks (as discussed earlier) to determine the health status of an instance.\nThe following section outlines the idx_1e80fbbd different scaling policies you can define.\nAuto scaling policies\nAWS provides several scaling policies that you can configure for your auto scaling groups.\nThese policies determine when and how your idx_c1e06e8c EC2 instances scale in or out. The available\noptions include:\nMaintain current instance levels: You can configure your auto scaling group to\nalways maintain a consistent number of EC2 instances across multiple AZs. If an EC2\ninstance fails, auto scaling automatically replaces it to preserve the desired fleet size.\nManual Scaling: You can manually adjust the minimum, desired, or maximum\nnumber of EC2 instances in the group. Auto Scaling will automatically make the\nnecessary changes to match your updated settings.\nScheduled Scaling: You can schedule scaling actions to launch or terminate EC2\ninstances at specific times. This is useful for predictable workload changes, such as\nscaling up on Black Friday for e-commerce demand or increasing capacity on the last\nTuesday of the month to support payroll processing.\nDynamic Scaling: When demand is unpredictable, dynamic scaling automatically\nadjusts capacity in response to real-time metrics. It initiates scale-out or scale-in\nactions in response to threshold breaches. For example, if average CPU utilization\nexceeds a defined level for a set duration, auto scaling adds more instances. If\nutilization drops below a specified threshold, it removes instances to optimize costs.\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n287 High Availability, Fault Tolerance, and Elasticity on AWS\nDynamic scaling includes three types of policies:"],[98392,"Chapter 10 287\nSee also\nGitHub Copilot tutorial: https://github.com/features/copilot/tutorials."],[86390,"Chapter 10 288\nLaunch Templates offer several advanced features, including:\nDefining multiple versions of a template to support different deployment\nenvironments\nHosting variations of templates for different use cases\nLaunching both Spot and On-Demand instances\nSpecifying multiple instance types or even multiple launch templates\nNow that you have a solid understanding of high availability, scalability, and the tools used to\nbuild a resilient and elastic architecture, the following section introduces a few additional\nservices that are important for the AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner exam. These services are\nalso included in this chapter because they will be used in the capstone project later.\nSupporting application delivery with AWS Secrets\nManager and Amazon SES\nAWS offers more than two hundred services, all idx_5a3448a6 designed to help you build resilient, reliable,\nscalable, and cost-effective solutions. This chapter introduces idx_059efe55 you to two additional services\nthat you must learn about to prepare for the AWS Certified Cloud Practitioner exam. The\nservices introduced in this section are also part of the architecture design for your first\ncapstone project for Belly Brew Co. Limited.\nAWS Secrets Manager\nAWS Secrets Manager is a fully managed service designed to help you securely store and\naccess secrets, such as database idx_a1ad366a credentials, access keys, API keys, and other sensitive\ninformation that must be protected.\nThe service allows you to store sensitive data in an encrypted format, which can only be\naccessed by entities with appropriate permissions. Access to these secrets is provided via API\ncalls, enabling your application to retrieve them at runtime as needed programmatically. This\nis a far more secure approach than hardcoding secrets in plain text within your application\ncode.\nFor example, if your application needs to update a backend database, it must first authenticate\nusing a set of credentials. Instead of hardcoding these credentials in plain text within the\napplication code and increasing security risk, the application can dynamically retrieve them\nfrom Secrets Manager at runtime. This ensures that sensitive information is not stored in plain\ntext within the source code.\nAWS provides additional tools for storing and managing sensitive data, such as environment\nvariables and API keys, like AWS Systems Manager Parameter Store. However, AWS Secrets\n•\n•\n•\n•\n289 High Availability, Fault Tolerance, and Elasticity on AWS\nManager offers a key advantage: it allows you to automatically rotate sensitive credentials,\nsuch as Amazon RDS usernames and passwords, at defined intervals.\nThis feature utilizes an AWS Lambda function to automatically rotate credentials and update\nthe associated database with the new values, much like you would regularly update your"],[98394,"Chapter 10 289\n6. Follow the onscreen instructions to log in to your Claude account.\nFigure 10.12: Claude account login\n7. Once you’ve logged in, the Claude Code console will appear.\nFigure 10.13: Claude Code console\nNetwork Security through Vibe Coding 290\n8. Type / to see the available command options.\nFigure 10.14: Claude Code options\n9. Type quit to exit Claude Code.\nHow it works…\nIt might take a bit of getting used to if you are used to code with a graphical interface in an IDE.\nHowever, I do find the operations faster in a terminal window than in a GUI.\nThere’s more…\nTry to perform a conversational Git operation, as indicated at https://docs.anthropic.com/en/\ndocs/claude-code/quickstart#step-5%3A-use-git-with-claude-code:\nThis is Claude Code’s interactive mode and is where you can have a direct\nconversation with Claude Code to ask questions, request tasks, and get im-\nmediate responses."],[86392,"Chapter 10 290\nFigure 10.7 represents the architecture of the solution you will build as part of this project\nexercise:\nFigure 10.7: Belly Brew Co. Limited's customer-facing website\nAs depicted in the diagram in Figure 10.7, the following idx_0445dbd4 are the core components idx_ac5cd744 you will build\nout for this project:\nAmazon VPC: In Chapter 7, you built a base VPC for Belly Brew Co. Limited as part of\nProject task 7. In this chapter, you will configure additional VPC components as follows:\nDeploy a NAT Gateway in Public Subnet 1 to enable EC2 instances in the private\nsubnet to access the internet and download updates, tools, and utilities. These\nEC2 instances will also use the NAT Gateway to access AWS public services such\nas Amazon S3 and Secrets Manager.\nUpdate the Main Route Table to configure a route to the internet via the NAT\nGateway. The private subnets will use this route to allow EC2 instances to access\nthe internet.\nNote that the database idx_f71cd3fc security group, Data-SG, will need to be updated with an\nadditional inbound rule allowing traffic on port 3306 from itself. This is\nnecessary idx_45743c08 because when you idx_729e3ca0 configure Secrets Manager with rotation enabled,\nAWS automatically creates a Lambda function that gets associated with this\nsecurity group by default. By allowing inbound traffic on port 3306 from the RDS\n1.\na.\nb.\nc.\n291 High Availability, Fault Tolerance, and Elasticity on AWS\nsecurity group itself, the Lambda function can connect to the Amazon RDS\ndatabase and update the MySQL credentials.\nApplication Load Balancer: To ensure high availability, you will deploy multiple EC2\ninstances across two Availability Zones within the App subnets. As part of this solution,\nyou will also deploy an ALB to distribute incoming customer traffic evenly across the\ninstances in App Subnet 1 and App Subnet 2. Additionally, you will need to create a\ntarget group that auto scaling will use to register the EC2 instances as targets.\nAWS Secrets Manager: You previously provisioned the Amazon RDS MySQL database\nin Chapter 9, as part of Project task 9. The database was configured with a standard\nusername and password for authentication, which you recorded. In this project task,\nyou will configure a secret in AWS Secrets Manager to securely store and manage the\ndatabase credentials. You will also configure a 7-day rotation policy to automatically\nrotate credentials at regular intervals, thereby enhancing the security of your database.\nAmazon S3 – Recipe eBook: You will create another Amazon S3 bucket to host the free\nrecipe eBook, which Belly Brew Co. Limited will email to all users who sign up on the\nwebsite. The eBook will be automatically sent using Amazon SES and the pre-signed\nURL feature.\nIAM Roles: To ensure that your website running on EC2 instances can connect to AWS"],[98395,"Chapter 10 291\nFigure 10.15: Git operations with Claude Code\nSee also\nClaude Code’s quickstart guide: https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/\nquickstart."],[23934,"Chapter 10 291\nSo, you see that this function is defined on line number 1069 of the bash_completion function library\nfile that’s in the /usr/share/bash-completion/ directory. Go ahead and peek into that file, and you’ll\nsee that many functions are defined in it. When you’re through with debugger mode, just type exit.\nYou can also obtain information about a function with the type command, like so:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ type quote\nquote is a function\nquote ()\n{\nlocal quoted=${1//\\'/\\'\\\\\\'\\'};\nprintf \"'%s'\" \"$quoted\"\n}\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\nNow that you know what functions are, let’s see if we can define some.\nDefining a Function\nYou can define a function in the following places:\n• On the command-line\n• In a shell script\n• In a function library\n• In a shell configuration file\nDefining a function from the command-line can be useful if you need to do any quick testing or ex -\nperimentation with new code. Here’s what it looks like:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ howdy() { echo \"Howdy, world!\" ; echo \"How's it going?\"; }\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ howdy\nHowdy, world!\nHow's it going?\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\nThe name of this function is howdy, and the () tells the shell that this is a function. The code for the\nfunction is within the pair of curly braces. The function consists of two echo commands, which are\nseparated by a semi-colon. Note that you need to place another semi-colon at the end of the final\ncommand. You also need to have one blank space between the first curly brace and the first command,\nand the final semi-colon and the closing curly brace.\nSome tutorials that you’ll find might tell you that you can begin the function with the keyword function,\nas you see here:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ function howdy() { echo \"Howdy, world!\" ; echo \"How's it\ngoing?\"; }\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ howdy\nUnderstanding Functions 292\nHowdy, world!\nHow's it going?\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\nBut, creating functions without the function keyword works well, so there’s no real reason to use it.\nAny functions that you define on the command line will be ephemeral and will disappear as soon as\nyou close the terminal. So, you’ll want to commit them to a file to make them permanent. Also, any\nfunctions that you define on the command-line will not be visible in a bash --debug session, because\nthey don’t get exported to a child shell.\nThere’s no difference in how you would create a function in shell scripts, function library files, and\nshell configuration files. But, there a couple of different methods for how you structure your functions.\nThe first method is to place the entire function on a single line, with or without the preceding function\nkeyword. This would look exactly as it would if you were to create a function on the command-line.\nSince you’ve already seen that, I’ll say no more about it.\nThe other method is to structure your functions in a multi-line format, as you would do in other pro -"],[86396,"Chapter 10 292\nTo ensure that the EC2 instances are automatically configured to host the application,\nyou will configure a Launch Template with all the necessary parameters to deploy the\nBelly Brew Co. Limited website application. The application source code will need to be\nupdated with specific parameters tailored to your configuration. These include:\nAmazon S3 bucket name for hosting your recipe eBook\nThe name of the secret you have configured in AWS Secrets Manager\nIn the next section, you will learn about the technical requirements needed to complete this\nproject task.\nTechnical requirements\nTo complete the project exercises in this chapter, ensure that you have completed all the labs in\nall previous chapters. Log in to your AWS Development account as the IAM user Alice to\ncomplete the project tasks.\nEnsure you download the source code bellybrewcode.zip and user_data.sh files from the\nGitHub repository and save them to your desktop for easy access. You must also download the\nrecipes.zip folder, which contains the free recipe eBook that you will host in an Amazon S3\nbucket.\nYou will need to make some minor edits to the application source to account for your\nenvironment settings. You must use an integrated development environment (IDE) such as\nVisual Studio Code to do this.\nProject task 10.1 – Updating Belly Brew Co. Limited's VPC\nconfiguration\nIn this project task, you will update the idx_c15834c6 existing Belly Brew Co. Limited's VPC configuration.\nThese will include updating the database security group, deploying a NAT Gateway, and\nconfiguring the main route table.\nFollow these steps:\nLog in to your AWS Development Account as the IAM user Alice and navigate to the\nVPC management console\nEnsure you are in the London region\nFrom the left-hand menu, click on Your VPCs\nEnsure you still have access to the Belly Brew VPC you configured in Chapter 7\nFrom the left-hand menu, click on NAT gateways\nIn the right-hand pane, click the Create NAT gateway button\na.\nb."],[98397,"Chapter 10 293\n3. The network information file states the network segments:\n…\nsecurity_zones:\nexternal:\n- name: \"Internet\"\ndescription: \"External untrusted networks\"\ntrust_level: \"untrusted\"\ninternal:\n- name: \"Corporate Network\"\ndescription: \"Internal trusted network\"\ntrust_level: \"trusted\"\n…\n4. The threat intelligence file is an aggregation of threat intelligence scans:\n…\n\"203.0.113.75\": {\n\"threat_type\": \"malware_dropper\",\n\"confidence\": \"high\",\n\"first_seen\": \"2024-01-14T16:20:00Z\",\n\"last_seen\": \"2024-01-15T11:30:00Z\",\n\"malware_families\": [\n\"ransomware\",\n\"info_stealer\"\n],\n\"delivery_method\": \"exploit_kit\",\n\"geographic_location\": \"asia_pacific\"\n}\n…\n5. Use Ask mode and choose the GPT-4.1 model.\n6. Begin by changing to the right directory by typing Please change to the /home/ubuntu/\nai_network_cookbook/ch10/Recipe_10_1 folder.\nNetwork Security through Vibe Coding 294\n7. Ask Copilot to describe the data by typing Describe the files in the mock_data\ndirectory. Here is the response:\nFigure 10.16: Copilot’s answer for mock data files\n8. Instruct Copilot to start building the script by typing The mock_data/firewall_logs.\ntxt file might contain port scanning and brute force attacks. Help me build\na parser that can identify these threat patterns."],[23936,"Chapter 10 293\nAs before, the preceding function keyword is optional. I’ve chosen to omit it, since it serves no func -\ntion and isn’t compatible with some non-bash shells.\nOkay, this all looks good, but watch what happens when I run the script:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ ./howdy_func.sh\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\nNothing happens, which isn’t good. I’ll show you why in the next section.\nUsing Functions in Shell Scripts\nYou’ll be amazed at how functions can make your shell scripts so much more functional. (Yeah, I know.\nThat really was a bad pun.) Read on to see how to make that happen.\nCreating and Calling Functions\nWhen you place a function inside a shell script, the function code won’t get executed until you call the\nfunction. Let’s modify the howdy_func.sh script to make that happen, like this:\n#!/bin/bash\nhowdy ()\n{\necho \"Howdy, world!\";\necho \"How's it going?\"\n}\nhowdy\nYou see that all I had to do was to place the name of the function outside of the function code block.\nAlso, note that the function definition must come before the function call. Otherwise, the shell will\nnever be able to find the function. Anyway, when I run the script now, it works fine:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ ./howdy_func.sh\nHowdy, world!\nHow's it going?\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\nI should also point out that the first curly brace can be on a line by itself as you see above, or it can be\non the same line as the function name, like this:\n#!/bin/bash\nhowdy() {\necho \"Howdy, world!\"\necho \"How's it going?\"\n}\nhowdy\nUnderstanding Functions 294\nI personally prefer this way for a simple reason. It’s just that my preferred text editor is vim, and plac -\ning the first curly brace on the same line as the function name causes vim to activate its automatic\nindenting feature.\nThe ability to reuse code is one of the coolest features about functions. Instead of having to type the\nsame code in every place where you need to perform a certain task, just type it once and call it from\nwherever you need to. Let’s make another slight modification to the howdy_func.sh script, like so:\n#!/bin/bash\nhowdy ()\n{\necho \"Howdy, world!\";\necho \"How's it going?\"\n}\nhowdy\necho\necho \"Let's do some more stuff and then\"\necho \"we'll call the function again.\"\necho\nhowdy\nHere’s what running the modified script looks like:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ ./howdy_func.sh\nHowdy, world!\nHow's it going?\nLet's do some more stuff and then\nwe'll call the function again.\nHowdy, world!\nHow's it going?\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\nSo, I called the function twice, and it got executed twice. How cool is that? But, it gets even better.\nPassing Positional Parameters to Functions\nYou can also pass positional parameters into a function, the same as you can pass them into a shell\nscript. To do that, call the function with a value that you want to pass as a positional parameter, as\nyou see here in this greet_func.sh script:"],[25663,"Chapter 10 294\nThe CNM has three main elements—sandbox, endpoint, and network:\nNetwork sandboxes: A i d x _ 3 2 0 1 e 6 b b sandbox perfectly isolates a container's network i d x _ 6 1 0 7 0 0 2 b stack from the outside world. By\ndefault, no inbound traffic is allowed into the sandbox, and outbound traffic is also controlled. This\nensures strong network-level isolation.\nEndpoint: A controlled i d x _ a 5 3 7 6 d c c connection point between a network sandbox and a network. Endpoints are how\ncontainers \"plug in\" to a network. One sandbox i d x _ 2 2 8 f 9 9 c f can have zero, one, or many endpoints, which means a\ncontainer can be connected to multiple networks simultaneously.\nNetwork: The i d x _ 6 a 0 d 9 6 c c actual pathway that transports data packets between endpoints. This i d x _ b 9 e c d 0 1 2 can be local (same\nhost) or global (across multiple hosts).\nIt is important to note that a network sandbox can have zero to many endpoints; or, said differently, each container\nliving in a network sandbox can be attached to either no network at all or multiple different networks at the same\ntime. In the preceding diagram, the middle of the three Network Sandboxes is attached to both Network 1 and\nNetwork 2 using an endpoint.\nThis networking model is very generic and does not specify where the individual containers that communicate\nwith each other over a network run. All containers could, for example, run on the same host (local), or they could\nbe distributed across a cluster of hosts (global).\nA key point is that the CNM itself is just a model—it doesn't dictate how networks are implemented, only the\nrequired concepts and relationships. Implementations can be local to a single host or span multiple hosts in a\ncluster.\nFor both local and global i d x _ 3 f 2 1 7 6 8 6 scopes, we have multiple implementations of the CNM. In the following table, we've\ngiven a short overview of the existing implementations and their main characteristics. The list is in no particular\norder:\nNetwork Company Scope Description\nBridge Docker Local\nA i d x _ 9 1 e 5 c 6 1 2 simple network based on Linux bridges to allow\nnetworking on a single host. It is the default\nnetwork type for containers on a single Docker\nhost.\nMacvlan Docker Local\nConfigures i d x _ b 0 1 9 2 3 0 8 multiple layer 2 (that is, MAC)\naddresses on a single physical host interface.\nAllows containers to appear as physical devices\ndirectly connected to the network.\nIpvlan Docker Local\nSimilar i d x _ 5 d 0 9 9 9 6 7 to Macvlan but uses a single MAC address\nand routes traffic based on IP addresses. Useful in\nenvironments where MAC address creation is\nrestricted.\n•\n•\n•\n295 Using Single-Host Networking\nNetwork Company Scope Description\nOverlay Docker\nGloba\nl\nMulti-node-capable i d x _ 9 c b a 3 5 f f container network i d x _ 8 1 a a 1 2 3 b based on"],[86403,"Chapter 10 294\nThis is how you do that:\nIn the left-hand menu, click on Security groups under the Security section\nIn the right-hand pane, select the database security group. If you followed the\ninstructions in Project task 7.5 from Chapter 7, this should be labeled as\nbelly ‑ brew ‑ vpc ‑ data ‑ sg\nIn the bottom half of the right-hand pane, click on Inbound rules\nClick the Edit inbound rules button\nOn the Edit inbound rules webpage that appears, click the Add rule button\nSelect the MySQL/Aurora option from the Type drop-down list and ensure Custom is\nselected as the Source\nNext, in the search box next to Custom, ensure that you select the\nbelly ‑ brew ‑ vpc ‑ data ‑ sg security group as per the following screenshot in Figure 10.9\nFigure 10.9: Updating the database security group\nNext, click on the Save rules button\nYour VPC is now configured idx_3fb1ef37 with the additional settings to complete this project. The\nprovisioning of the NAT gateway and associated route table configuration will ensure that the\nEC2 instances that will be provisioned via the Auto Scaling groups in Project task 10.8 will be\nable to access external networks to download source code files and install any necessary\napplications. The NAT gateway will also be used by AWS Lambda to connect to AWS Secrets\nManager for credential rotation. In the next project task, you will configure the load balancer\nthat will be used to distribute inbound traffic to your EC2 instances."],[98398,"Chapter 10 295\n9. Take a look at the logic and the file output; modify it further with instructions if needed.\nFigure 10.17: Script output\n10. Switch to Agent mode and ask Copilot to build it. An example instruction is, Please build\nthe script and name it log_analysis.py.\nNetwork Security through Vibe Coding 296\n11. Now that the script has been written, you have the option to keep it or undo it.\nFigure 10.18: log_analysis.py creation\nHow it works…\nThe conversational approach enables us to rapidly develop a solution and iterate. We utilized\nseveral key mechanisms :\n• Natural language problem definition: Instead of writing a formal specification document,\nwe just described the problem.\n• Iterative pattern recognition: Note that the AI automatically recognized that we needed\na regular expression match and created an initial set.\n• Continual refactoring: Note that we did not write a single line of code to receive the file.\nWe can continue our “develop -> test -> improve” cycle until we have a solution that is\nready for production."],[23938,"Chapter 10 295\nYou see the $1 positional parameter in the function. When I call the function, I’m passing the value\nDonnie into the function. Here’s what happens when I run the script:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ ./greet_func.sh\nGreetings, Donnie\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\nYou can also pass the positional parameter into the script as you normally would, and then pass it\ninto the function, as you see here:\n#!/bin/bash\ngreetings() {\necho \"Greetings, $1\"\n}\ngreetings $1\nHere’s what happens when I run this script:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ ./greet_func2.sh Frank\nGreetings, Frank\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\nThat’s good, but it only works with one parameter. Any additional names that you pass in as parameters\nwon’t show up in the output. You can fix that by defining additional parameters, like this:\n#!/bin/bash\ngreetings() {\necho \"Greetings, $1 $2\"\n}\ngreetings $1 $2\nAnd, that’s fine if you know that you’ll always want to pass in a specified number of parameters. If you\ndon’t want to limit yourself like this, use the $@ as the positional parameter. That way, you can pass\nin however many parameters you want each time that you run the script. Here’s what it looks like:\n#!/bin/bash\ngreetings() {\necho \"Greetings, $@\"\n}\ngreetings $@\nLet’s see what it looks like when I pass in four names:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ ./greet_func4.sh Vicky Cleopatra Frank Charlie\nGreetings, Vicky Cleopatra Frank Charlie\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\nUnderstanding Functions 296\nThat looks good. But, I can still fancy it up a bit, like this:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ ./greet_func4.sh Vicky, Cleopatra, Frank, \"and Charlie\"!\nGreetings, Vicky, Cleopatra, Frank, and Charlie!\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\nPretty slick, eh? What’s even more slick is having a function pass values back to the main program.\nPassing Values from a Function\nOkay, here’s where I need to pause for a moment and clarify something for the veterans of other pro -\ngramming languages. Unlike what you may be used to with other languages, a shell scripting function\ncannot directly return a value to its caller, which is normally the main body of the program.\nYou can create a function that will return a value, but you have to do it with either a global variable\nor a combination of a local variable and command substitution. Another difference is that in other\nlanguages, variables are local by default, and you have to specify which variables are to be global. In\nshell scripting, it’s just the opposite. Shell scripting variables are global by default, and you have to\nspecify which variables are to be local.\nNow, for those of you who aren’t veterans of other programming languages, allow me to explain the\ndifference between local and global variables.\nA global variable can be accessed from anywhere in a script, even if you defined it within a function.\nFor example, look at the valuepass function in this value1.sh script:"],[25665,"Chapter 10 296\nIn this example, c1 and c2 (on the front network) can talk to each other, and c3 and c4 (on the back network) can\ntalk to each other. However, no communication is possible between containers on different networks unless a\ncontainer is deliberately attached to both.\nFor instance, imagine an application composed of three services:\nwebAPI: Public-facing service\nproductCatalog: Internal API\ndatabase: Stores product data\nWe want the i d x _ 9 c 1 2 3 f 7 1 following:\nwebAPI to communicate with productCatalog, but not with database\nproductCatalog to communicate with both webAPI and database\nThis can be achieved by attaching webAPI and database to separate networks, and productCatalog to both, as\nshown here:\nFigure 10.3 – Container attached to multiple networks\nThis approach ensures that if webAPI is compromised, the attacker still can't directly reach database —they'd have\nto breach productCatalog as well.\nIPv4, IPv6, and dual-stack networking\nSince Docker 20.10, IPv6 i d x _ 8 3 8 2 7 8 6 2 support for user-defined bridge networks has been stable. We can now i d x _ e 9 6 9 9 3 2 0 enable dual-stack\nnetworking so that containers receive both IPv4 i d x _ 8 a b f 9 6 c 9 and IPv6 addresses. This is important for modern deployments\nand ensures compatibility with IPv6-only environments.\nHere is an example:\ndocker network create \\\n--driver bridge \\\n--subnet 10.10.0.0/16 \\\n--ipv6 --subnet fd00:dead:beef::/48 \\\nmynet-v6\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n297 Using Single-Host Networking\nnftables versus iptables\nOn modern i d x _ 6 c f 0 4 a 2 e Linux distributions, Docker's network firewall rules are managed using nftables instead i d x _ c 4 d 0 3 f b 7 of the older\niptables system. While the behavior is mostly transparent to users, advanced firewall troubleshooting commands\nmay differ. If you're on a newer distro (such as Ubuntu 24.04+ or Fedora 38+), you may need to use the following:\n$ sudo nft list ruleset\nThis is in place of the following:\n$ sudo iptables -L\nBest practice: Least-privilege networking\nBecause SDNs are fast and i d x _ d b 4 9 4 7 e b cost nothing to create, it's recommended to design our application so that each network\nonly contains services that need to talk to each other. We should avoid the temptation to put everything on the\ndefault bridge network. The fewer services on the same network, the lower our exposure if one is breached.\nThe abstract Docker networking model becomes concrete on a single host through the bridge driver, which we\nexplore next.\nWorking with the bridge network\nThe Docker bridge network is i d x _ b b e 1 c c 8 a the first implementation of the CNM that we're going to look at in detail. This\nnetwork implementation is based on the Linux bridge driver.\nWhen the Docker daemon starts for the first time, it automatically creates a Linux bridge called docker0. Docker"],[86411,"Chapter 10 296\nOn the Register targets - recommended webpage that appears, scroll down and click\nthe Next button. You will not be registering any targets now, as the auto scaling service\nwill be configured to register the targets automatically.\nOn the Review and create webpage, scroll to the bottom of the page and click Create\ntarget group\nNext, you will configure the application load balancer:\nFrom the left-hand menu, click Load Balancers, in the Load Balancing section\nIn the right-hand pane, click the Create load balancer button\nOn the Compare and select load balancer type webpage, click the Create button\nunder Application Load Balancer\nNext, on the Create Application Load Balancer webpage that appears, provide a name\nfor the load balancer, such as bb ‑ alb\nNext, for the Scheme, ensure the Internet ‑ facing option is selected. Also ensure that\nIPv4 is selected as the Load balancer IP address type\nIn the Network mapping section, select Belly Brew Co. Limited's VPC\n(belly ‑ brew ‑ vpc) from the VPC drop-down list\nSelect both eu ‑ west ‑ 2a and eu ‑ west ‑ 2b under Availability Zones and subnets\nEnsure that you also select idx_9ecaf99f the public subnets under each zone as depicted in Figure\n10.10:\nFigure 10.10: Select Belly Brew Co. Limited's public subnets for your load balancer\nNext, under Security groups, select the load balancer security you created in Project\ntask 7.5 in Chapter 7. This should be belly ‑ brew ‑ vpc ‑ alb ‑ sg. Also, remove the default\nsecurity group by clicking on the X icon next to it, as it's not required."],[23940,"Chapter 10 297\nNow, let’s define textstring as a local variable in the value2.sh script, like so:\n#/bin/bash\nvaluepass() {\nlocal textstring='Donnie is the great BeginLinux Guru'\n}\nvaluepass\necho $textstring\nWatch what happens when I run the script this time:\ndonnie@debian12:~$ ./value2.sh\ndonnie@debian12:~$\nThere’s no output this time, because the local variable isn’t accessible outside of the function. Or,\nmore accurately, the local variable isn’t directly accessible outside of the function. There are ways to\nmake the function pass the value of a local variable to an external caller, which I’ll show you in just\na few moments.\nFirst though, I want answer a question that I know you’re anxious to ask. That is, if we can use a global\nvariable to pass a value outside of a function, why do we need local variables? Well, it’s just that if\nyou use nothing but global variables in your functions, you could turn your script into a debugging\nnightmare. That’s because if you accidentally create a variable outside of the function with the same\nname as a global variable that’s inside of the function, you would overwrite the value of the variable\nfrom within the function.\nBy using only local variables inside of functions, you avoid that problem. If you have an external variable\nthat has the same name as a local variable that’s in a function, the external variable won’t overwrite\nthe internal variable’s value. To demo this, let’s create the value3.sh script, like so:\n#/bin/bash\nvaluepass() {\ntextstring='Donnie is the great BeginLinux Guru'\n}\nvaluepass\necho $textstring\ntextstring='Who is the great BeginLinux Guru?'\necho $textstring\nYou see that I’ve set the textstring variable in the function back to a global variable, and I’ve defined\nthe variable again outside of the function. Here’s what happens when I run the script now:\ndonnie@debian12:~$ ./value3.sh\nDonnie is the great BeginLinux Guru\nWho is the great BeginLinux Guru?\ndonnie@debian12:~$\nUnderstanding Functions 298\nThe second echo $textstring command used the new value of textstring, which overwrote the pre -\nvious value. That’s fine if that’s what you want to happen, but it usually isn’t what you want to happen.\nOne way to have a function pass a value with a local variable is to call the function from within a\ncommand substitution construct. To demo that, create the value4.sh script, like so:\n#!/bin/bash\nvaluepass() {\nlocal textstring='Donnie is the great BeginLinux Guru'\necho \"$textstring\"\n}\nresult=$(valuepass)\necho $result\nYou see here that I’ve created the external result variable, and assigned to it the value that was ob -\ntained by the $(valuepass) command substitution.\nAnother method for obtaining a value from a function is to use a result variable. This involves passing\nthe name of a variable into the function when you call it, and then assigning a value to that variable"],[98399,"Chapter 10 297\nThere’s more…\nPlease try out the following:\n• Different pattern detection: Try to extend the conversation for more complex attacks\n• Role differentiation: Try to tailor the code toward an advanced, skilled engineer or a\nbeginner for maintainability\n• Optimize code execution: Try to optimize the code for execution speed\nSee also\n• Best practices for using GitHub Copilot: https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/get-\nstarted/best-practices\n• GitHub Copilot Chat Cookbook: https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/tutorials/\ncopilot-chat-cookbook"],[86418,"Chapter 10 298\nNext, in the Credentials section, provide the database username and password you\nconfigured when you created Belly Brew Co. Limited's database in Chapter 9\nScroll down and in the Database section, select Belly Brew Co. Limited's Database\n(bellybrewdb) as per the following screenshot in Figure 10.12:\nFigure 10.12: Belly Brew Co. Limited's database\nClick Next\nProvide a name for your secret in the Secret name and description section. For\nexample, you can name your secret bb ‑ db ‑ secret\nScroll a bit further down the webpage and click Next\nClick the toggle switch to turn Automatic rotation on in the Configure automatic\nrotation section\nNext, in the Rotation schedule, ensure that the Schedule expression builder is\nselected\nSet the Time unit to Days and the Days to 7\nScroll down to the Rotation function section. Here, provide a name for your Lambda\nrotation function, for idx_7fdfa698 example, bb ‑ rotation. AWS Secrets Manager will add a prefix\nto the name with the word SecretsManager. Note that the function name, including\nthe prefix, must be a maximum of 64 characters. This Lambda rotation function,\ncreated by AWS, is responsible for updating the database credentials. It will also update\nthe secret stored in AWS Secrets Manager.\nEnsure that Single user is selected as the Rotation strategy\nNext, click Next\nScroll to the bottom of the Review webpage and click the Store button\nAWS will store the secret bb ‑ db ‑ secret. It will take a few minutes to configure the rotation\nsettings. After a few minutes, you can refresh the page and then click on the secret name,\nbb ‑ db ‑ secret. You will note the Secret details as per the following screenshot in Figure 10.13:"],[25667,"Chapter 10 298\nwe will also discuss other types of networks that have a global scope, meaning they can span whole clusters of\nhosts.\nNow, let's look a little bit deeper into what this bridge network is all about. For this, we are going to use the Docker\ninspect command:\n$ docker network inspect bridge\nWhen executed, this outputs a big chunk of detailed information about the network in question. This information i d x _ 6 0 d 9 b 4 5 9\nshould look as follows:\nFigure 10.5 – Output generated when inspecting the Docker bridge network\n299 Using Single-Host Networking\nWe saw the ID, Name, Driver, and Scope values when we listed all the networks, so that is nothing new, but i d x _ d 4 f d e c 7 e let's\nhave a look at the IP Address Management (IPAM) block.\nIPAM is a i d x _ a a b c b 5 f a piece of software that is used to track the IP addresses that are used on a computer. The important part of\nthe IPAM block is the config node with its values for the subnet and gateway. The subnet for the bridge network is\ndefined by default as 172.17.0.0/16. This means that all containers attached to this network will get an IP address\nassigned by Docker that is taken from the given range, which is 172.17.0.2 to 172.17.255.255. The 172.17.0.1\naddress is reserved for the router of this network, whose role in this type of network is taken by the Linux bridge.\nWe can expect that the very first container that will be attached to this network by Docker will get the 172.17.0.2\naddress. All subsequent containers will get a higher number; the following diagram illustrates this fact:\nFigure 10.6 – The bridge network\nIn the preceding i d x _ d 9 2 c 7 c b e diagram, we can see the network namespace of the host, which includes the host's eth0 endpoint,\nwhich is typically a NIC if the Docker host runs on bare metal or a virtual NIC if the Docker host is a VM. All traffic\nto the host comes through eth0. The Linux bridge is responsible for routing the network traffic between the host's\nnetwork and the subnet of the bridge network.\nAvoiding subnet conflicts\nIn corporate environments or when using VPNs, the default 172.17.0.0/16 range may clash with existing\nnetworks. To avoid this, we can change the default bridge subnet by editing Docker's daemon\nconfiguration file:\n{\n\"bip\": \"192.168.100.1/24\"\n}\nSave this as /etc/docker/daemon.json (Linux) or use Docker Desktop's Settings | Docker Engine tab,\nthen restart Docker. The bip value defines the bridge's gateway address and subnet in CIDR notation.\nNote"],[98401,"Chapter 10 299\n5. Claude Code will propose an incident_response_system.py file. You have the option to\ncreate the file or iterate on the script.\nFigure 10.21: Claude Code creating an incident_response_system.py file\n6. Start to engage in other conversations, such as What is the best way to structure this\nso that it can handle multiple incident types and integrate with different\nsecurity tools?.\nHow it works…\nThe Claude Code conversation approach to incident response development provides several\nadvantages over simple code completion:\n• Natural language problem-solving: Claude Code maintains context across complex,\nmulti-turn discussions.\n• Architectural guidance: Claude arguably has a better understanding of architectural\nguidance. Whether it is better than, say, GitHub Copilot is a judgment call, but it is defi -\nnitely better than traditional code completion.\n• Terminal integration: Claude Code directly integrates into the terminal and offers a\nsmoother experience for experienced CLI users.\nNetwork Security through Vibe Coding 300\n• Iterative refinement: Just like GitHub Copilot, Claude Code allows for real-time refine -\nment of response logic, escalation procedures, and integration approaches based on user\nfeedback.\nThere’s more…\nClaude Code can also work with Python code if we use claude-code-sdk on PyPI: https://docs.\nanthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/sdk#python. The following terminal command installs\nclaude-code-sdk for Python:\n$ pip install claude-code-sdk\nHere’s the output.\nFigure 10.22: Claude Code Python SDK basic usage\nSee also\nCommon workflows: https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/common-workflows."],[23942,"Chapter 10 299\nSo now, $__internalvar is really the result variable with Shell scripting is cool! as its assigned\nvalue.\nUnderstand though, that you don’t want the variable name that you pass into a function to be the same\nas the name of the local variable that’s inside the function. Otherwise, the eval command won’t be\nable to properly set the value of the variable. That’s why I prefaced the name of the local variable with\na pair of underscores, so that I can be sure to prevent having a variable with the same name outside\nof the function. (Of course, only one underscore would have sufficed, but the accepted convention is\nto use two underscores.)\nNext, let’s create some libraries.\nCreating Function Libraries\nFor a convenient way to make your functions reusable, just place them into a function library. Then,\nfrom within your scripts, call the desired libraries with either a source command or a dot command.\nIf you’re the only one who will ever use the libraries, you can place them in your own home directory.\nIf you want to make them available for everyone, you can place them in a central location, such as in\nthe /usr/local/lib/ directory.\nFor a simple demonstration, I’ve created the donnie_library file in the /usr/local/lib/ directory\nwith two simple functions. It looks like this:\nhowdy_world() {\necho \"Howdy, world!\"\necho \"How's it going?\"\n}\nhowdy_georgia() {\necho \"Howdy, Georgia!\"\necho \"How's it going?\"\n}\n(I live in the state of Georgia, which explains the second function.)\nThere’s no need to set the executable permission on this file, because it only contains code that will\nbe called in by an executable script.\nNext, I created the library_demo1.sh script, which looks like this:\n#!/bin/bash\nRemember that the value of the __internalvar variable is result, which is the name\nof the variable that we passed into the function. Also, be aware that this is now a global\nvariable, because I didn’t specify the local keyword here. This means that its value can\nbe passed back to the main body of the script.\nUnderstanding Functions 300\nsource /usr/local/lib/donnie_library\nhowdy_world\nYou see that I used the source command to reference the donnie_library file. I then called the\nhowdy_world function as I would normally do. Running the script looks like this:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ ./library_demo1.sh\nHowdy, world!\nHow's it going?\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\nI also created the library_demo2.sh file, which looks like this:\n#!/bin/bash\n. /usr/local/lib/donnie_library\nhowdy_georgia\nThis time I replaced the source command with a dot, to show you that they both do the exact same thing.\nI then called the second function in the library file. Running this script looks like this:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ ./library_demo2.sh\nHowdy, Georgia!\nHow's it going?\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\nFor something a bit more practical, let’s add a network checking function, using a case..esac con -"],[25668,"Chapter 10 300\nBy default, only egress traffic is allowed, and all ingress is blocked. What this means is that while containerized\napplications can reach the internet, they cannot be reached by any outside traffic. Each container attached to the\nnetwork gets its own virtual Ethernet (veth) connection to the bridge. This is illustrated in the following diagram:\nFigure 10.7 – Details of the bridge network\nThe preceding diagram shows i d x _ 6 0 9 8 d e 4 e us the world from the perspective of the host. We will explore what this situation\nlooks like from within a container later on in this section. We are not limited to just the bridge network, as Docker\nallows us to define our own custom bridge networks. This is not just a feature that is nice to have; it is a\nrecommended best practice not to run all containers on the same network. Instead, we should use additional\nbridge networks to further isolate containers that have no need to communicate with each other.\nWhat is a NIC?\nA Network Interface Card (NIC), sometimes i d x _ 8 b e 8 d d 5 d referred to as a network interface connector, is a i d x _ 6 2 a d 2 d d b hardware\ncomponent that enables a computer or device to connect to a network. It serves as an interface between\nthe computer and the network, allowing data to be transmitted and received. NICs are typically built-in\ncomponents on motherboards or installed as expansion cards and support various types of network\nconnections, such as Ethernet, Wi-Fi, or fiber-optic connections.\nNote\n301 Using Single-Host Networking\nCreating a custom bridge network\nContainers connected to the same user-defined bridge network automatically receive DNS resolution via Docker's\nembedded DNS server. This allows containers to reach each other using container names instead of IP addresses.\nTo create a i d x _ 7 2 5 d 5 f 5 5 custom bridge network called sample-net, use the following command:\n$ docker network create \\\n--driver bridge \\\nsample-net\nIf we do this, we can then inspect what subnet Docker has created for this new custom network, as follows:\n$ docker network inspect sample-net | grep Subnet\nThis returns the following value:\n\"Subnet\": \"172.18.0.0/16\",\nEvidently, Docker has just assigned the next free block of IP addresses to our new custom bridge network. If, for\nsome reason, we want to specify our own subnet range when creating a network, we can do so by using the --\nsubnet parameter:\n$ docker network create \\\n--driver bridge \\\n--subnet \"10.1.0.0/16\" \\\ntest-net\nUse the docker network inspect command to double-check that it worked.\nIPv6 and dual-stack bridge networks\nSince Docker 20.10, we i d x _ 8 6 2 f d 4 4 e can create bridge networks that support both IPv4 and IPv6. This is particularly useful for\nenvironments migrating to IPv6-only infrastructure:\n$ docker network create \\"],[86423,"Chapter 10 300\nYour secret is now configured for rotation and securely stored in AWS Secrets Manager. Later,\nyou will update the application code with the secret name so that it can dynamically retrieve\nthe database credentials to connect idx_d4119e1b to the backend database.\nIn the next project task, you will create an Amazon S3 bucket that will be used to host the Belly\nBrew Co. Limited recipe eBook. Later, the application running on the EC2 instance will access\nthe bucket and generate a pre-signed URL to send the eBook to users who register on the\nwebsite.\nProject task 10.4 – Creating an Amazon S3 bucket for\nrecipe eBook\nIn this project task, you will create an idx_ca86f850 Amazon S3 bucket to host the recipe eBook, which will\nbe sent to all end users who subscribe to the website.\nIn the top search bar of the AWS Management Console, search for and navigate to\nAmazon S3\nEnsure that you are still in the London region\nFrom the left-hand menu, click on General-purpose buckets\nIn the right-hand pane, click the Create bucket button\nEnsure the Global namespace is selected as the Bucket namespace option. Provide a\nsuitable name for your bucket. Remember, bucket names are unique across the AWS\necosystem, so no two buckets can idx_c4c8b379 have the same name. For example, to complete this\nproject guide, a bucket with the name belly ‑ brew ‑ recipes is being created\nLeave all the remaining settings at default values and click the Create bucket button at\nthe bottom of the webpage\nYou will then be redirected to the webpage listing all your general-purpose buckets in\nyour AWS account. Click on the bucket you just created to navigate to the bucket\nwebpage as per the screenshot in Figure 10.15:"],[23944,"Chapter 10 301\n. . .\n. . .\n}\nNow, the library_demo3.sh script that uses this new function looks like this:\n#!/bin/bash\n. /usr/local/lib/donnie_library\nnetwork $1\nRunning the script without supplying an argument looks like this:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ ./libary_demo3.sh\n2023-10-27 . . . Success! google.com is reachable.\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\nHere’s how it looks when I supply a valid site name as the argument:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ ./libary_demo3.sh www.civicsandpolitics.com\n2023-10-27 . . . Success! www.civicsandpolitics.com is reachable.\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\nAnd finally, here’s what I get when I supply an invalid site name:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ ./libary_demo3.sh www.donnie.com\nping: www.donnie.com: Name or service not known\n2023-10-27 . . . Network Failure for site www.donnie.com!\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\nI know, it’s a crying shame that there’s no website that’s named after me. (I might have to correct that\nproblem some day.)\nAnd, guess what? That’s really all I have to say about function libraries. Let’s move on to the real world.\nLooking at Some Real-World Examples\nHere are a few cool examples of functions in action. Enjoy!\nChecking Network Connectivity\nThis function is essentially the same as the one that I’ve just shown you in the previous section. The\nonly real difference is that I’m using an if..then construct instead of the case..esac construct.\nNote how for the first condition I used a \"\") construct. The pair of double-quotes, with\nno blank space between them, means that no value has been passed in for the positional\nparameter. This also means that google.com will be used as the default site name.\nUnderstanding Functions 302\nAlso, I placed this function into the actual executable script. But, you can easily move it into a library\nfile if you desire. (I can’t show you the entire script here due to book formatting constraints, but you can\ndownload it from the Github repository. It’s the network_func.sh file.) Anyway, here’s the important\npart that I really want you to see:\n#!/bin/bash\nnetwork() {\nif [[ $# -eq 0 ]]; then\nsite=\"google.com\"\nelse\nsite=\"$1\"\nfi\n. . .\n. . .\n}\nnetwork\nnetwork www.civicsandpolitics.com\nnetwork donnie.com\nYou see that after I defined the network() function, I called it three different times. I first called it with -\nout a positional parameter, so that it would ping google.com. I then called it with another real website\nand then with a fake website as the positional parameter. Here’s what I get when I run this new script:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ ./network_func.sh\n2023-10-27 . . . Success for google.com!\n2023-10-27 . . . Success for www.civicsandpolitics.com!\nping: donnie.com: Name or service not known\n2023-10-27 . . . Network Failure for donnie.com!\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\nThat part works. Let’s now look in the system log file to see if the logger utility that’s in the function"],[55446,"Chapter 10 301\nDashboard architecture\nBefore diving into the specifics of how the dashboard authenticates a user, it’s important to under-\nstand the basics of how the dashboard works. The dashboard, at a high level, has three logical layers:\n• User interface: This is the Angular + HTML frontend that is displayed in your browser and\nthat you interact with\n• Middle tier: The frontend interacts with a set of APIs hosted in the dashboard’s container to\ntranslate calls from the frontend into Kubernetes API calls\n• API server: The middle-tier API interacts directly with the Kubernetes API server\nThis three-layered architecture of the Kubernetes Dashboard can be seen in the following diagram:\nFigure 10.1: Kubernetes Dashboard logical architecture\nWhen a user interacts with the dashboard, the user interface makes calls to the middle tier, which\nin turn makes calls to the API server. The dashboard doesn’t know how to collect credentials; there’s\nno place to supply a username or password to log in to the Dashboard. It has a very simple session\nmechanism system based on cookies, but for the most part, the dashboard doesn’t really know, or\ncare, who the currently logged-in user is. The only thing the dashboard cares about is what token to\nuse when communicating with the API server.\nDeploying a Secured Kubernetes Dashboard 302\nWhile this is the logical architecture, the physical architecture divides these components across dif-\nferent containers:\nFigure 10.2: Kubernetes Dashboard container architecture\nStarting with vertion 7.0, the dashboard is now broken into five components in their own containers:\n• Web: The user interface for the dashboard, serving the HTML and JavaScript rendered by your\nbrowser. This component has no authentication and doesn’t need it.\n• Api: This container hosts the workhorse of the dashboard. It’s the component that interacts\nwith the API server on your behalf. This container needs to know who you are.\n• Auth: The auth container is used to tell the frontend if your token is valid. If the token isn’t\nvalid, the UI will redirect you to login by providing the token to the API server for verification.\n• Metrics: This container provides a metrics endpoint for Prometheus of the Kubernetes Dash-\nboard.\n• Ingress Controller: Since each of these containers provide their own path from the same host,\nsomething needs to combine them into a single URL. The default deployment includes Kong’s\nIngress controller.\nIf you looked at the pods running in Chapter 6, you’ll notice that neither Kong nor the auth container\nare running. We’ll cover that a bit later. Now that we understand how the dashboard is architected,\nhow does the dashboard know who you are? Let’s walk through the options.\nAuthentication methods\nThere are two ways that the dashboard can determine who a user is:"],[98402,"Chapter 10 301\nSummary\nIn this chapter, we introduced vibe coding as a conversational approach to developing network\nsecurity automation. Unlike the traditional development approach, vibe coding prioritizes rapid\nprototyping through natural language conversation with AI assistants. Some of its core concepts\ninclude the following:\n• Conversation over formal specification requirements: Instead of providing a formal\ndocument, security professionals just describe the problem and intended solutions\n• Speed over perfection: We rarely get the script right the first time, or the second time,\nbut since the time-to-script is so fast, we get to iterate really quickly\n• Domain expertise first: Vibe coding allows subject matter experts to leverage their domain\nexpertise, and allows the vibe coding tool to write code\nUsing GitHub Copilot, we are able to use vibe coding directly in VS Code. The assistant can behave\nin Ask or Agent mode, with a different mix-and-match of models to choose from. With Claude\nCode, the vibe coding assistant is integrated into the terminal.\nThis is the last chapter of this book. Thank you for taking this journey with me through the evolving\nlandscape at the intersection of AI LLMs and network engineering. The time and effort you have\ninvested in this book should serve you well for the fundamental shift in how network professionals\ncan solve complex problems. This book is intended as your starting point, not the destination.\nThe real magic happens when you take these patterns and adapt them for your own usage.\nI want to thank you again for giving this book a piece of your time. I hope the solutions provided\nhave offered value to you, and I want to wish you the best of luck on your AI LLM network engi-\nneering journey. The future of network engineering will be written by practitioners like you, using\nAI to amplify and not to replace human engineers. Onward and upward!\nGet This Book’s PDF Version and\nExclusive Extras\nScan the QR code (or go to packtpub.com/unlock). Search for this\nbook by name, confirm the edition, and then follow the steps on\nthe page.\nNote: Keep your invoice handy. Purchases made directly from Packt\ndon’t require an invoice.\n11\nUnlock Your Exclusive Benefits\nYour copy of this book includes the following exclusive benefits:\n• Next-gen Packt Reader\n• DRM-free PDF/ePub downloads\nFollow the guide below to unlock them. The process takes only a few minutes and needs to be\ncompleted once.\nUnlock this Book’s Free Benefits in 3 Easy Steps\nStep 1\nKeep your purchase invoice ready for Step 3. If you have a physical copy, scan it using your phone\nand save it as a PDF, JPG, or PNG.\nFor more help on finding your invoice, visit https://www.packtpub.com/unlock-benefits/help.\nNote: If you bought this book directly from Packt, no invoice is required. After Step 2,"],[86430,"Chapter 10 302\nFigure 10.16: Upload of eBook to Amazon S3\nYour recipe eBook has now been uploaded to the Amazon S3 bucket. Make a note of the bucket\nname and the path to your file. In idx_f44ef27e this example, the eBook is in the recipe ‑ book folder. Note\nthat your bucket name will be different.\nThe Belly Brew Co. Limited website application that is running on your EC2 instances needs to\naccess various other AWS services, such as AWS Secrets Manager, Amazon S3, and the Amazon\nSES service. To ensure the instances have the necessary permissions, you must create an IAM\nrole that will be added to the instance profile for those EC2 instances. The next project task\nwalks you through this process.\n303 High Availability, Fault Tolerance, and Elasticity on AWS\nProject task 10.5 – Configuring IAM roles\nIn this project task, you will configure the necessary IAM roles to allow the EC2 instances to\ncommunicate with several services, such idx_80ee99fb as AWS Secrets Manager, Amazon S3, and Amazon\nSES.\nHere's what you have to do:\nIn the search bar at the top of the AWS Management Console, search for and navigate\nto the IAM service\nFrom the left-hand menu, click on Roles\nIn the right-hand pane, click the Create role button\nFor the Trusted entity type, select AWS service\nIn the Use case section, select EC2 from the drop-down list under Service or use case\nClick Next\nOn the Add permissions webpage, search for and select the following permissions:\nAmazonS3FullAccess\nAmazonSESFullAccess\nClick Next\nProvide a name for the role, such as bb ‑ iam ‑ role\nScroll down the webpage and click the Create role button\nNext, you must add additional permissions for the role to enable your web application to\naccess the secret in AWS Secrets Manager. This will enable the application to authenticate\nagainst the backend MySQL database on Amazon RDS. This is how that is done:\nNavigate back to the list of IAM roles. You can click on the Roles link again on the left-\nhand menu. In the right-hand pane, search for the bb-iam-role role. Click on the role\nto access its settings as shown in Figure 10.17:"],[25670,"Chapter 10 302\nFigure 10.8: Custom bridge network using IPv6\nConfiguring the MTU and other options\nSome network environments (especially those using VPNs or overlay networks) require adjusting i d x _ 8 a c 8 e 2 6 1 the Maximum\nTransmission Unit (MTU). We can do this when creating a network:\n$ docker network create \\\n--driver bridge \\\n--opt com.docker.network.driver.mtu=1400 \\\nmynet-mtu\nThis prevents fragmentation issues when packets traverse networks with lower MTU limits.\nAttaching containers to custom bridge networks\nNow that we have i d x _ 6 b e 9 4 6 e 1 discussed what a bridge network is and how we can create a custom bridge network, we want to\nunderstand how we can attach containers to these networks.\nTo avoid conflicts due to duplicate IP addresses, make sure you avoid creating networks with overlapping\nsubnets.\nNote\n303 Using Single-Host Networking\nFirst, let's interactively run an Alpine container without specifying the network to be attached:\n$ docker container run \\\n--name c1 -it --rm \\\nalpine:3.22 /bin/sh\nIn another terminal window, let's inspect the c1 container:\n$ docker container inspect c1 | jq '.[0].NetworkSettings'\nTo limit the vast i d x _ 4 b a 6 5 a 3 4 output, we have used the jq tool. Let's concentrate for a moment on the part that provides\nnetwork-related information. This can be found under the NetworkSettings node. I have it listed in the following\noutput:\nFigure 10.9 – The NetworkSettings section of the container metadata"],[39312,"Chapter 10 302\nThe Podman Swiss Army knife – subuid and subgid\nModern Linux distributions use a version of the shadow-utils package that leverages two\nfiles: /etc/subuid and /etc/subgid. These idx_99795dd1 files are used idx_72946681 to determine which UIDs and GIDs\ncan be used to map a user namespace.\nThe default allocation for every user is 65536 UIDs and 65536 GIDs.\nWe can run the following simple command to check how the subuid and subgid allocation\nworks in rootless containers:\n$ id\nuid=1000(alex) gid=1000(alex) groups=1000(alex),10(wheel)\n$ podman run alpine cat /proc/self/uid_map /proc/self/gid_map\nResolved \"alpine\" as an alias (/etc/containers/registries.conf.d/000-\nshortnames.conf)\nTrying to pull docker.io/library/alpine:latest...\nGetting image source signatures\nCopying blob 59bf1c3509f3 done\nCopying config c059bfaa84 done\nWriting manifest to image destination\nStoring signatures\n0 1000 1\n1 100000 65536\n0 1000 1\n1 100000 65536\nAs we can see, both files indicate that they start mapping UID and GID 0 with the current\nuser's UID/GID that we idx_9062453b just ran the container with; that is, 1000. After that, it maps UID and\nGID 1, starting idx_eb0c0a4d from 100000 and arriving at 165536. This is calculated by summing the starting\npoint, 100000, and the default range, 65536.\nFor a comparison, let's see the same example on the host:\n$ id\nuid=1000(alex) gid=1000(alex) groups=1000(alex),10(wheel)\n$ cat /proc/self/uid_map /proc/self/gid_map\n0 0 4294967295\n0 0 4294967295\n303 Securing Containers\nBecause it starts at 0 for both sides and covers the full range of 32-bit UID available on Linux,\n4294967295, it means UID/GID 1000 on the host system is seen as UID/GID 1000 by the\nprocess. There is no translation happening.\nUsing rootless containers is not the only best practice we can implement for our container\nenvironments. In the next section, we'll learn why we shouldn't run a container with UID 0.\nAvoiding running containers with UID 0\nContainer runtimes can be idx_86dc9fb1 instructed to run processes inside idx_d5801c0c a container with a user ID\ndifferent from the one that initially created the container. Running the processes inside the\ncontainer as a non-root user can be helpful for security purposes. While container runtimes\nuse Linux namespaces to isolate processes, a vulnerability in the container engine or the kernel\ncould allow a root user (UID 0 in the container) to break out of the container and gain the same\nprivileges as the host root user (UID 0 in the host). Using an unprivileged user (with a non-0\nUID) in a container can limit the privilege escalation and thus the attack surface inside and\noutside the container.\nBy default, a Dockerfile and Containerfile may set the default user as root (that is, UID=0). To\navoid this, we can leverage the USER instruction in those build files – for example, USER 1001 –"],[55448,"Chapter 10 303\n• Token from a reverse proxy: If there’s an authorization header containing a bearer token in\nrequests from the user interface to the middle tier, the middle tier will use that bearer token\nin requests to the API server. This is the most secure option and the implementation that will\nbe detailed in this chapter.\nIf you’ve read our previous editions, or have used previous versions of the dashboard, you may be\nwondering what happened to using the dashboard’s own identity and skipping login. The architec-\ntural changes in version 7.x meant the removal of this option. No matter what, there MUST be an\nAuthorization header with a token in each request. This is a very positive development as it makes it\nvery hard to deploy a dashboard that can be taken over by an anonymous request. In fact, we removed\nthe section of this chapter that talked about how to compromise an improperly deployed dashboard\nbecause that attack vector is no longer valid.\nThroughout the rest of this chapter, the first option will be explored as an anti-pattern for accessing\nthe dashboard, and we will explain why the reverse proxy pattern is the best option for accessing a\ncluster’s dashboard implementation from a security standpoint and a user experience standpoint.\nLet’s now try to understand dashboard security risks.\nUnderstanding dashboard security risks\nThe question of the dashboard’s security often comes up when setting up a new cluster. Securing the\ndashboard boils down to how the dashboard is deployed, rather than if the dashboard itself is secure.\nGoing back to the architecture of the dashboard application, there is no sense of “security” being built\nin. The middle tier simply passes a token to the API server.\nWhen talking about any kind of IT security, it’s important to look at it through the lens of defense in\ndepth. This is the idea that any system should have multiple layers of security. If one fails, there are\nother layers to fill the gap until the failed layers can be addressed. A single failure doesn’t give an\nattacker direct access.\nThe most often cited incident related to the dashboard’s security was the breach of Tesla in 2018 by\ncrypto miners. Attackers were able to access pods running in Tesla’s clusters because the dashboard\nwasn’t secured.\nThe cluster’s pods had access to tokens that provided the attackers with access to Tesla’s cloud providers\nwhere the attackers ran their crypto-mining systems. It’s important to note that this attack would not\nhave worked in version 7.x and above because the api container will not accept requests that don’t\nhave Authorization headers.\nDashboards in general are often an attack vector because they make it easy to find what attackers are\nlooking for and can easily be deployed insecurely. Illustrating this point, at KubeCon NA 2019, a Cap-"],[23946,"Chapter 10 303\nNext, is a little something that I whipped up for myself.\nUsing the CoinGecko API\nCoinGecko is a cool website where you can go to check on the prices and statistics of pretty much\nevery cryptocurrency there is. It’s fairly quick to use if you just need to check on one coin, but it can\nbe fairly time-consuming if you need to check on multiple coins.\nYou can create a custom portfolio of coins, which is useful if you always want to see the same list of\ncoins. But, it’s not so useful if you always want to check on different lists of coins. So, you might like\nto streamline the process a bit. For that, you can create a shell script that uses the CoinGecko Appli -\ncation Programming Interface (API).\nTo take full advantage of all of the CoinGecko API features, you’ll need to become a paying customer,\nwhich is rather expensive. Fortunately, you don’t need to do that for this demo. Instead, you’ll just use\nCoinGecko’s public API key, which is free-of-charge. To demo this, let’s do a hands-on lab.\nHands-on Lab – Creating the coingecko.sh Script\nTo begin, take a look at CoinGecko’s documentation page (https://www.coingecko.com/api/\ndocumentation), and follow these steps as mentioned below:\n1. Scroll down the page, and you’ll see a list of the various functions that you can perform with\nthe public API. You can use the forms in the drop-down lists to create the code that you’ll copy\ninto a shell script. I’ve already created the coingecko.sh shell script that performs three of\nthose functions, which you can download from the Github repository. Before you use it though,\nyou’ll need to ensure that the curl and jq packages are installed on your system. Anyway,\nhere’s how the script works.\n2. At the top of the gecko_api function, I defined the coin and currency variables as positional\nparameters $1 and $2. The if [ -z $coin ]; then line checks to see if the value of coin is\nzero length. If it is, it means that I haven’t entered any arguments when I invoked the script.\nSo, it will then echo a series of statements about how to invoke the script.\n3. Next, you see this elif stanza:\nelif [ $coin == list ]; then\ncurl -X 'GET' \\\n\"https://api.coingecko.com/api/v3/coins/list?include_platform=true\" \\\n-H 'accept: application/json' | jq | tee coinlist.txt\n4. I copied this curl command from the CoinGecko documentation page, and pasted it into the\nscript.\nBe aware that I tested this script on a Fedora machine, which still uses the old-style rsyslog\nlog files. If you try this on Debian, you’ll need to install rsyslog yourself. By default, Debian\n12 now only uses the journald logging system, and doesn’t come with rsyslog installed.\nUnderstanding Functions 304\n5. Then, I piped the output into the jq utility to convert the normal json output to pretty json\nformat.\n6. I then piped that output into tee in order to both view the output on screen and save it to a text"],[39314,"Chapter 10 304\n}\n}\nThe nginx configuration idx_bb9fca16 file is really simple: we define the listening idx_4b51dd61 port (80) and the content\nmessage to return once a request arrives on the server.\nThen, we can create a simple Dockerfile to leverage one of the official nginx container images:\n$ cat Dockerfile\nFROM docker.io/library/nginx:mainline-alpine\nRUN rm /etc/nginx/conf.d/*\nADD hello-podman.conf /etc/nginx/conf.d/\nThe Dockerfile contains three instructions:\nFROM: For selecting the official nginx image\nRUN: For cleaning the configuration directory of any default config example\nADD: For copying the configuration file that we just created\nNow, let's build the idx_f66908fc container idx_635ad8e5 image with Buildah:\n$ buildah bud -t nginx-root:latest -f .\nSTEP 1/3: FROM docker.io/library/nginx:mainline-alpine\nSTEP 2/3: RUN rm /etc/nginx/conf.d/*\nSTEP 3/3: ADD hello-podman.conf /etc/nginx/conf.d/\nCOMMIT nginx-root:latest\nGetting image source signatures\nCopying blob 8d3ac3489996 done\n...\nCopying config 21c5f7d8d7 done\nWriting manifest to image destination\nStoring signatures\n--> 21c5f7d8d70\nSuccessfully tagged localhost/nginx-root:latest\n21c5f7d8d709e7cfdf764a14fd6e95fb4611b2cde52b57aa46d43262a6489f41\nOnce you've built the image, name it nginx-root. Now, we are ready to run our container:\n$ podman run --name myrootnginx -p 127.0.0.1::80 -d nginx-root\n364ec7f5979a5059ba841715484b7238db3313c78c5c577629364aa46b6d9bdc\n•\n•\n•\n305 Securing Containers\nHere, we used the –p option to publish the port and make it reachable from the host. Let's find\nout which local port has been chosen, randomly, in the host system:\n$ podman port myrootnginx 80\n127.0.0.1:38029\nFinally, let's call our containerized web server:\n$ curl localhost:38029\nHello Podman user!\nServer address: 10.0.2.100:80\nThe container is finally running, but what user is our container using? Let's find out:\n$ podman ps | grep root\n364ec7f5979a localhost/nginx-root:latest nginx -g daemon o... 55 minutes ago\nUp 55 minutes ago 0.0.0.0:38029->80/tcp myrootnginx\n$ podman exec 364ec7f5979a id\nuid=0(root) gid=0(root)\nAs expected, the idx_7efb90c0 container is running as root!\nNow, let's make a few idx_3aef7dc5 edits to change the user. First, we need to change the listening port in\nthe nginx server configuration:\n$ cat hello-podman.conf\nserver {\nlisten 8080;\nlocation / {\ndefault_type text/plain;\nexpires -1;\nreturn 200 'Hello Podman user!\\nServer address: $server_addr:\n$server_port\\n';\n}\n}\nHere, we replaced the listening port (80) with 8080; we cannot use a port that's below 1024\nwith unprivileged users."],[86434,"Chapter 10 304\nFigure 10.17: IAM role with permissions for Belly Brew Co. Limited's web app\nIn the Permissions policies section, select Create inline policy from the drop-\ndown list under Add permissions in the far right idx_8aaeee15 of the section\nOn the Specify permissions webpage that appears, select the Secrets Manager service\nfrom the drop-down list in the Select a service section\nUnder Actions allowed, expand the Read option under Access level"],[25671,"Chapter 10 304\nIn the preceding i d x _ c 3 e b 7 e 8 8 output, we can see that the container is indeed attached to the bridge network since NetworkID is\nequal to c8bfb1e5..., which we can see from the preceding code being the ID of the bridge network. We can also\nsee that the container was assigned the IPAddress value of 172.17.0.2 as expected and that the gateway is at\n172.17.0.1.\nPlease note that the container also had a MacAddress value associated with it. This is important as the Linux bridge\nuses the MacAddress value for routing.\nSo far, we have approached this from the outside of the container's network namespace. Now, let's see what the\nsituation looks like when we're not only inside the container but inside the container's network namespace. Inside\nthe c1 container, let's use the ip tool to inspect what's going on. Run the ip addr command and observe the output\nthat is generated, as follows:\nFigure 10.10 – Container namespace, as seen by the IP tool\nThe interesting part i d x _ a 7 d 7 2 c 4 4 of the preceding output is 11:, that is, the eth0 endpoint. The veth0 endpoint that the Linux\nbridge created outside of the container namespace is mapped to eth0 inside the container. Docker always maps the\nfirst endpoint of a container network namespace to eth0, as seen from inside the namespace. If the network\nnamespace is attached to an additional network, then that endpoint will be mapped to eth1, and so on.\nSince at this point we're not really interested in any endpoint other than eth0, we could have used a more specific\nvariant of the command, which would have given us the following:\n305 Using Single-Host Networking\nFigure 10.11 – eth0 endpoint as seen from inside the container\nIn the output, we can also see what MAC address (32:82:29:aa:8c:ed) and IP (172.17.0.2) have been associated\nwith this container network namespace by Docker.\nWe can also get some information about how requests are routed by using the ip route command:\n/ # ip route\nThis gives us the following output:\ndefault via 172.17.0.1 dev eth0\n172.17.0.0/16 dev eth0 scope link src 172.17.0.2\nThis output tells us that all the traffic to the gateway at 172.17.0.1 is routed through the eth0 device.\nNow, let's run another container called c2 on the same network and in detach mode:\n$ docker container run \\\n--name c2 -d --rm \\\nalpine:3.22 ping 127.0.0.1\nThe c2 container will i d x _ b 3 3 1 2 3 e a also be attached to the bridge network since we have not specified any other network. Its IP\naddress will be the next free one within the subnet, which is 172.17.0.3, as we can readily test with the following\ncommand:\n$ docker container inspect \\\n--format \"{{range .NetworkSettingsNetworks}}{{.IPAddress}}{{end}}\" \\\nc2\nThis results in the following output:\n172.17.0.3\nNow, we have two containers attached to the bridge network. We can try to inspect this network once again to find"],[23948,"Chapter 10 305\n}\n}\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\n10. So, it looks like the current price of Bitcoin is $34664 US.\nYou can view multiple coins at a time by entering a comma-separated list, like this:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ ./coingecko.sh bitcoin,ethereum,dero usd\n% Total % Received % Xferd Average Speed Time Time Time\nCurrent\nDload Upload Total Spent Left\nSpeed\n100 471 0 471 0 0 3218 0 --:--:-- --:--:-- --:--:-\n- 3226\n{\n\"bitcoin\": {\n\"usd\": 34662,\n\"usd_market_cap\": 675835016546.567,\n\"usd_24h_vol\": 27417000940.815952,\n\"usd_24h_change\": 2.647099155664393,\n\"last_updated_at\": 1698262727\n. . .\n. . .\n[donnie@fedora ~]$\n11. If you’re a trader, you might want to check the price of an altcoin in terms of some other ver -\nsus currency, such as Bitcoin satoshis. Here, I’m checking the Bitcoin satoshi price of Dero:\n[donnie@fedora ~]$ ./coingecko.sh dero sats\n% Total % Received % Xferd Average Speed Time Time Time\nCurrent\nDload Upload Total Spent Left\nSpeed\n100 161 0 161 0 0 746 0 --:--:-- --:--:-- --:--:-\n- 748\n{\n\"dero\": {\n\"sats\": 8544.47,\n\"sats_market_cap\": 106868665749.11504,\n\"sats_24h_vol\": 50996466.4091239,\n\"sats_24h_change\": -3.2533636291339687,\n\"last_updated_at\": 1698263552\n}\nUnderstanding Functions 306\n}\n[donnie@fedora ~]\n12. So, the current satoshi price of Dero is 8544.47 sats. (Of course, these prices will be different\nby the time you look at them.)\n13. The top three lines of the output are from the curl command that the script used to download\ninformation about the coins. For some strange reason, bash considers this curl message as an\nerror message, even though it really isn’t. But, that’s actually good, because you can prevent\nhaving to see this message by using a stderr redirector at the end of your command, like this:\ndonnie@fedora:~$ ./coingecko.sh dero sats 2>/dev/null\n{\n\"dero\": {\n\"sats\": 3389.23,\n\"sats_market_cap\": 42965410638.71136,\n\"sats_24h_vol\": 112468964.13986385,\n\"sats_24h_change\": 2.81262920882357,\n\"last_updated_at\": 1718482347\n}\n}\ndonnie@fedora:~$\n14. Oh, I almost forgot about the most important part of the script, which is the command that\ncalls the function:\ngecko_api $1 $2\nIt asks for two positional parameters, but will work just fine if you just enter either list or\ncurrencies as a single argument.\n15. Okay, this is good. But, what if you have a very long list of coins that you want to track, and you\ndon’t want to type that whole long list in every time that invoke this script? Well, that’s easy. I’ll\njust create the mycoins.sh script, which will call the coingecko.sh script. Here’s how it looks:\n#!/bin/bash\n./coingecko.sh bitcoin,dero,ethereum,bitcoin-cash,bitcoin-cash-\nsv,bitcoin-gold,radiant,monero usd 2>/dev/null\nOf course, you can edit this script to add your own list of favorite coins.\n16. For a variation on this theme, download the coingecko-case.sh script, too. It’s the same script,"],[55451,"Chapter 10 305\nWhen you deploy your dashboard, you should enable encryption between the reverse proxy and the\napi container. We’ll walk through that in the next section. The web and metrics containers aren’t as\nimportant. The auth container doesn’t support any encryption, which is an issue, but with the right\nconfiguration can be bypassed.\nAn alternative approach is to rely on a service mesh like Istio. If you enable a mesh, you could use that\nto rely on for encryption, but that’s an additional component to add.\nGiven how easy it is to create an internal certificate authority, there really isn’t any reason to not have\nthese connections encrypted.\nWhile we’ve focussed on the security concerns of the default Kubernetes Dashboard installation, we’ll\nnext move on to how to correctly deploy the dashboard.\nDeploying the dashboard with a reverse proxy\nProxies are a common pattern in Kubernetes; there are proxies at every layer in a Kubernetes cluster.\nThe proxy pattern is also used by most service mesh implementations on Kubernetes, creating side-\ncars that will intercept requests. The difference between the reverse proxy described here and these\nproxies is in their intent. Microservice proxies often do not carry a session, whereas web applications\nneed a session to manage the state.\nThe following diagram shows the architecture of a Kubernetes Dashboard with a reverse proxy:\nFigure 10.3: Kubernetes Dashboard with a reverse proxy\nDeploying a Secured Kubernetes Dashboard 306\nThe reverse proxy shown in Figure 10.3 performs four roles:\n• Routing: Each of the containers used by the dashboard has its own path off of the host URL.\nThe reverse proxy is responsible for routing requests to the correct container.\n• Authentication: The reverse proxy intercepts unauthenticated requests (or stale sessions) and\ntriggers the authentication process with an OpenID Connect identity provider to authenticate\nthe user.\n• Session management: The Kubernetes Dashboard is a user-facing application. It should have\nthe typical controls put in place to support session timeouts and revocation. Be wary of a\nreverse proxy that stores all session data in a cookie. These methods are difficult to revoke.\n• Identity injection: Once the proxy has authenticated a user, it needs to be able to inject an\nHTTP authorization header on each request that is a JWT identifying the logged-in user, is\nsigned by the same OpenID Connect identity provider, and has the same issuer and recipient\nas the API server. The exception to this is using impersonation, which, as discussed in Chapter\n6, Integrating Authentication into Your Cluster, injects specific headers into the requests.\nWhat’s important is that when you configure your reverse proxy, it should:\n1. Encrypt traffic to the api and the auth containers: These two containers are the ones that need"],[86437,"Chapter 10 306\nFigure 10.19: Specify the ARN of the Belly Brew secret\nClick the Add ARNs button\nClick Next\nIn the Review and create webpage that appears, provide a name for the policy, such as\nbb ‑ secret ‑ iam ‑ policy\nClick the Create policy button\nYour IAM role has now been configured idx_4ee5009d with an inline policy to grant read access to the secret\nyou created in Project task 10.3.\nWith the IAM role now in place, which will be used by your EC2 instances, the next project task\nis to configure Amazon SES to send your customers the email containing a link to the recipe\neBook. The next project task provides step-by-step instructions to configure Amazon SES in a\nsandbox environment.\nProject task 10.6 – Configuring Amazon SES\nIn this project task, you will configure Amazon SES to send emails containing the eBook link to\nanyone who registers on Belly Brew Co. Limited's idx_05cdb27a website."],[39315,"Chapter 10 306\nThen, we need to edit our Dockerfile:\n$ cat Dockerfile\nFROM docker.io/library/nginx:mainline-alpine\nRUN rm /etc/nginx/conf.d/*\nADD hello-podman.conf /etc/nginx/conf.d/\nRUN chmod -R a+w /var/cache/nginx/ \\\n&& touch /var/run/nginx.pid \\\n&& chmod a+w /var/run/nginx.pid"],[25673,"Chapter 10 306\nThis information can be found under the Containers node:\nFigure 10.12 – The Containers section of the output of the Docker network inspect bridge\nWe have used the jq tool to provide a shortened and formatted output of the relevant part for readability.\nNow, let's create two i d x _ b c 5 3 1 3 3 c additional containers, c3 and c4, and attach them to sample-net, which we created earlier.\nFor this, we'll use the --network parameter:\n$ docker container run \\\n--name c3 --rm -d \\\n--network sample-net \\\nalpine:3.22 ping 127.0.0.1\n$ docker container run \\\n--name c4 --rm -d \\\n--network sample-net \\\nalpine:3.22 ping 127.0.0.1\nLet's inspect the sample-net network and confirm that c3 and c4 are indeed attached to it:\n$ docker network inspect sample-net | jq '.[0].Containers'\nThis will give us the following output for the Containers section:\n307 Using Single-Host Networking\nFigure 10.13 – The Containers section of the docker network inspect test-net command\nThe next question we're i d x _ 9 7 7 d b a d 0 going to ask ourselves is whether the c3 and c4 containers can freely communicate with\neach other. To demonstrate that this is indeed the case, we can exec into the c3 container:\n$ docker container exec -it c3 /bin/sh\nOnce inside the container, we can try to ping container c4 by name or by IP address:\n/ # ping c4\nWe should get this output:\nPING c4 (172.20.0.3): 56 data bytes\n64 bytes from 172.20.0.3: seq=0 ttl=64 time=3.092 ms\n64 bytes from 172.20.0.3: seq=1 ttl=64 time=0.481 ms\n...\nInstead of the container name, here, we use c4 's IP address:\n/ # ping 172.20.0.3\nWe should see the following result:\nPING 172.20.0.3 (172.20.0.3): 56 data bytes\n64 bytes from 172.20.0.3: seq=0 ttl=64 time=0.200 ms\n64 bytes from 172.20.0.3: seq=1 ttl=64 time=0.172 ms\n..."],[55453,"Chapter 10 307\nFigure 10.4: Visual Studio Code with the Kubernetes plugin\nThe Kubernetes plugin for Visual Studio Code is able to refresh its OpenID Connect token because\nit’s built with the client-go SDK, the same client libraries used by kubectl. When evaluating a client\ndashboard, make sure it works with your authentication type, even if it isn’t OpenID Connect. Many\nof the SDKs for Kubernetes don’t support OpenID Connect token refreshes. The Java and Python SDKs\nonly recently (as of the published date of this book) began supporting the refresh of OpenID Connect\ntokens the way the client-go SDK does. When evaluating a local dashboard, make sure it’s able to\nleverage your short-lived tokens and can refresh them as needed, just like kubectl can.\nThere is no shortage of different dashboards in the Kubernetes ecosystem, all with their own spins on\nmanagement. I don’t want to simply provide a list of these dashboards without giving you an in-depth\nreview of their benefits and security impacts. Instead, let’s focus on what’s important when evaluating\nwhich dashboard you want to use:\n• If the dashboard is web-based:\n• Does it support OpenID Connect directly?\n• Can it run behind a reverse proxy and accept both tokens and impersonation headers?\n• Does it require any permissions for its own service account? Do these permissions\nadhere to a least-privilege approach?\nDeploying a Secured Kubernetes Dashboard 308\n• If the dashboard is local:\n• Does the client SDK support OpenID Connect, with the ability to automatically refresh\ntokens as kubectl does, using the client-go SDK?\nThese are important evaluation questions not just for the Kubernetes Dashboard, but for dashboards\nthat you may use for other cluster management applications. As an example, the TektonCD dashboard,\nwhich is a web application for managing your pipelines, requires deleting several RBAC bindings to\nmake sure the dashboard has to use the user’s identity and can’t be co-opted to use its ServiceAccount\nidentity.\nOther cluster-level applications\nThe introduction of this chapter discussed how a cluster is made up of several applications besides\nKubernetes. Other applications will likely follow the same model as the dashboard for security, and\nthe reverse proxy method is a better method for exposing those applications than kubectl port-for-\nwarding, even when the application has no built-in security. Take the common Prometheus stack as\nan example. Grafana has support for user authentication, but Prometheus and Alert Manager do not.\nHow would you track who had access to these systems or when they were accessed using port-for-\nwarding?\nUsing a reverse proxy, logs of each URL and the user that was authenticated to access the URL can be\nforwarded to a central log management system and analyzed by a Security Information and Event"],[23950,"Chapter 10 307\nSummary\nFunctions are an important part of most every modern programming language. In this chapter I’ve\nshown you how to construct them, how to call them, and how to create function libraries that you\ncan either use for yourself or share with other users.\nIn the next chapter, I’ll show you how to perform mathematical operations with shell scripts. I’ll see\nyou there.\nQuestions\n1. Which two of the following methods would you use to create a function? (Choose two.)\na. function function_name()\nb. function_name()\nc. declare -f function_name\nd. declare -F function_name\n2. You want to create a function that will pass the value of a variable back to the main program.\nWhich of the following should you use to do that?\na. A global variable\nb. A local variable\nc. A return statement\nd. A continue statement\n3. You want to create a function that any user can use in his or her own scripts. What is the best\nway to do that?\na. Create a function library file in a central location, and make it executable.\nb. Add the new functions to the /etc/bashrc file.\nc. Create a function library file in a central location, but don’t make it executable.\nd. D. Create a function library file in your own home directory.\n4. Which two of the following commands would you use to reference a function library in your\nshell scripts? (Choose two.)\na. load\nb. reference\nc. source\nd. .\n5. You want to see the list of functions that are loaded into memory, along with their associated\nsource code. Which command would you use?\na. declare -f\nb. declare -F\nUnderstanding Functions 308\nc. debugger -f\nd. debugger -F\nFurther Reading\n• Bash Functions: https://linuxize.com/post/bash-functions/\n• Returning Values from Bash Functions: https://www.linuxjournal.com/content/return-\nvalues-bash-functions\n• Bash Function & How to Use It {Variables, Arguments, Return}: https://phoenixnap.com/\nkb/bash-function\n• Writing shell scripts-Lesson 15: Errors and Signals and Traps: http://linuxcommand.org/\nlc3_wss0150.php\n• (Note that the author of this tutorial uses all upper-case letters for the names of scripting vari -\nables, which is something that I don’t advocate. Other than that, it’s a good tutorial.)\n• Advance Bash Script Guide: https://tldp.org/LDP/abs/html/abs-guide.html#FUNCTIONS\nAnswers\n1. a and b\n2. b\n3. c\n4. c and d\n5. a\nJoin our community on Discord!\nRead this book alongside other users, Linux experts, and the author himself.\nAsk questions, provide solutions to other readers, chat with the author via Ask Me Anything sessions,\nand much more. Scan the QR code or visit the link to join the community.\nhttps://packt.link/SecNet\n11\nPerforming Mathematical\nOperations\nThe various operating system shells all have means of performing mathematical operations either\nfrom the command-line, or from within a shell script. In this chapter, we’ll look at how to perform"],[86442,"Chapter 10 308\nFigure 10.21: Verified identity status\nYou can now send emails to this address. You will use the same email address as the From:\naddress, as well.\nIn the next project task, you will create another Amazon S3 bucket, which will be used to host\nthe application source code. The Auto Scaling service, which you will deploy in Project task 10.8,\ndynamically provisions the required EC2 instances. As part of that process, the application\nsource code must be downloaded and configured on the instances. This requires you to host\nthe source code in a supported repository. In this case, you will be using an Amazon S3 bucket\nas that repository.\nProject task 10.7 – Creating additional Amazon S3 buckets\nIn this project task, you will create an additional idx_5e2bf744 Amazon S3 bucket that will serve as the\napplication source code repository. You should have downloaded the source code from the\nGitHub repository and extracted the files as per the following screenshot in Figure 10.22:\nFigure 10.22: Application Source Code and Recipe eBook\nNote the following:\nThe bellybrewcode.zip should be decompressed (extracted) to reveal the\nbellybrewcode folder. Within this folder is another folder called bellybrew, which\ncontains all the application source code.\n•\n309 High Availability, Fault Tolerance, and Elasticity on AWS\nThe recipe ‑ book.zip folder, when decompressed (extracted), will reveal the\nrecipe ‑ book folder containing the ebook that you uploaded in Project task 10.4.\nThe user_data.sh is the bash script you will use to configure your EC2 instances to\nhost the web application.\nThe following is the list of instructions to follow:\nIn your Windows file explorer or MacOS Finder window, navigate to the contents of the\nbellybrew folder\nUsing your favorite IDE, such as Visual Studio Code, open the index.php file. This\ncontains the main source code idx_747211b2 of the application. A screenshot is depicted in Figure\n10.23 for your reference.\nFigure 10.23: Screenshot of the index.php file\nYou must edit this code as follows:\nAt line number 75, you must provide the secret name of the secret you created in\nProject task 10.3. See the screenshot in Figure 10.24. You can get the secret name\nfrom AWS Secrets Manager by simply navigating to the service and clicking on\nSecrets.\n•\n•"],[39318,"Chapter 10 308\nSigning images with Sigstore and Podman\nIn this section, we will create a basic Sigstore key pair and configure Podman to push and sign\nthe image while storing the signature in a staging store. For the sake of clarity, we will run a\nregistry using the basic Docker Registry V2 container image without any customization.\nBefore testing the idx_547112f7 image pull and signature validation workflow, we idx_725bdc6e will look at how Podman\nhandles the idx_a35bd95d signature as an OCI artifact or via a idx_5d0fcdf8 separate idx_3aeb52c2 web server.\nTo create image signatures with Sigstore, we idx_89c89166 need to create a valid key pair or use an existing\none. For this reason, we will provide a short recap on asymmetric key pairs to help you\nunderstand how image signatures work.\nA key pair is composed of a private key and a public key. The public key can be shared\nuniversally, while idx_31a5f24a the private key is kept private and never shared with anybody. The private\nkey is used by idx_0e53eb66 the sender (the image builder) to sign the image. In this way, anyone with\naccess to the idx_a43234e2 corresponding public key can verify that the image was not tampered with and\ntruly originated from the sender.\nWe can easily translate this concept into container images: the image owner who pushes it to\nthe remote registry can sign it using a keypair and store the detached signature on a signature\nstore that is publicly accessible by users. Here, the signature is separated by the image itself –\nthe registry will store the image blobs while the signature store will hold and expose the image\nsignatures.\nUsers who are pulling the image will be able to validate the image signature using the\npreviously shared public key.\nNow, let's idx_6fdb0586 go back to creating the Sigstore key pair. We are going to create a simple one with\nthe Cosign utility, a lightweight tool designed specifically for signing and verifying container\nimages. It idx_638faa7d adheres to Open Container Initiative (OCI) standards, ensuring compatibility and\nseamless integration with popular container registries.\nLet's start with Cosign installation and configuration. As usual, there are several installation\nmethods available; here, we are reporting the most structured way to install it via a package\nmanager in your operating system:\n$ LATEST_VERSION=$(curl https://api.github.com/repos/sigstore/cosign/releases/\nlatest | grep tag_name | cut -d : -f2 | tr -d \"v\\\", \")\n$ curl -O -L \"https://github.com/sigstore/cosign/releases/latest/download/cosign-\n${LATEST_VERSION}-1.x86_64.rpm\"\n$ sudo rpm -ivh cosign-${LATEST_VERSION}-1.x86_64.rpm\n309 Securing Containers\nWe can idx_4f3b59dd check if the idx_9ef2ed54 installation was idx_7189c41f successful by running the following idx_aaac2101 command:\n$ cosign version"],[25674,"Chapter 10 308\nThe answer in both i d x _ f f a 5 0 4 f d cases confirms to us that the communication between containers attached to the same\nnetwork is working as expected. The fact that we can even use the name of the container we want to connect to\nshows us that the name resolution provided by the Docker DNS service works inside this network.\nNow, we want to make sure that the bridge and sample-net networks are firewalled from each other. To\ndemonstrate this, we can try to ping the c2 container from the c3 container, either by its name or by its IP address.\nLet's start pinging by name:\n/ # ping c2\nThis results in the following output:\nping: bad address 'c2'\nThe following is the result of the ping using the IP address of the c2 container instead:\n/ # ping 172.17.0.3\nIt gives us this output:\nPING 172.17.0.3 (172.17.0.3): 56 data bytes\n^C\n--- 172.17.0.3 ping statistics ---\n11 packets transmitted, 0 packets received, 100% packet loss\nThe preceding command remained hanging, and we had to terminate the command with Ctrl + C. From the output\nof pinging c2, we can also see that the name resolution does not work across networks. This is the expected\nbehavior. Networks provide an extra layer of isolation, and thus security, to containers.\nEarlier, we learned i d x _ e 8 5 d 3 6 9 1 that a container can be attached to multiple networks. Let's first create a network called\nsample-net-2. Note that the following command does not define the driver of the network; thus, the default driver\nis used, which happens to be the bridge driver:\n$ docker network create sample-net-2\nThen, we attach a container, c5, to our sample-net network:\n$ docker container run \\\n--name c5 --rm -d \\\n--network sample-net \\\nalpine:3.22 ping 127.0.0.1\n309 Using Single-Host Networking\nThen, we create another container, c6, attached to the new sample-net-2:\n$ docker container run \\\n--name c6 --rm -d \\\n--network sample-net-2 \\\nalpine:3.22 ping 127.0.0.1\nWe then attach c6 also to the sample-net network with this command:\n$ docker network connect sample-net c6\nNow, after attaching the c6 container to the same network, sample-net, as c5, we can test whether it is reachable i d x _ b 1 8 e 5 6 2 d\nfrom c6, as well as from the c3 container, which is also attached to the sample-net network. The result will show\nthat the connection indeed works:\nFigure 10.14: Container c6 attached to two networks\nIf we want to remove an existing network, we can use the docker network rm command, but note that we cannot\naccidentally delete a network that has containers attached to it:\n$ docker network rm sample-net-2\nIt results in this output:\nError response from daemon: error while removing network: network sample-net-2 has active\nendpoints (c6)\nexit status 1"],[55455,"Chapter 10 309\nThis diagram shows how the dashboard’s user interface interacts with its server-side component with\na reverse proxy injecting the user’s id_token:\nFigure 10.5: OpenUnison integration with the dashboard\nThe dashboard uses the same OpenID Connect identity provider as the API server but doesn’t use the\nid_token provided by it. Instead, OpenUnison has a plugin that will generate a new id_token indepen-\ndent of the identity provider with the user’s identity data in it. OpenUnison can do this because the key\nused to generate an id_token for the OpenID Connect identity provider, used by kubectl and the API\nserver, is stored in OpenUnison. This is different from how you would integrate the dashboard with\nKeyCloak or Dex because you would need an additional component to authenticate users and main-\ntain the id_token that is injected into the requests. This is often done with the OAuth2 proxy, which\nwould need to be integrated with both your identity provider (i.e., Dex or KeyCloak), the dashboard,\nand your ingress controller. OpenUnison did all these steps for you.\nA new, short-lived token is generated separately from the OpenID Connect session used with kubectl.\nThis way, the token can be refreshed independently of a kubectl session. This process provides the\nbenefits of a 1- to 2-minute token life with the convenience of a direct login process.\nYou’ll also notice that there is no auth container. The auth container’s only role is to return some JSON\nto tell the UI that the user is still authenticated. Since this container doesn’t support any encryption,\nwe don’t bother calling it and instead generate the JSON directly in OpenUnison. This cuts out the\nneed for the auth container and any issues that might arise from not having a TLS network connection\nwith a bearer token.\nDeploying a Secured Kubernetes Dashboard 310\nIf you have an eye for security, you may point out that this method has a glaring single point of failure\nin the security model: a user’s credentials! An attacker generally just needs to ask for credentials in\norder to get them. This is often done via email in an attack called phishing, where an attacker sends\na victim a link to a page that looks like their login page but really just collects credentials. This is why\nmulti-factor authentication is so important for infrastructure systems.\nIn a 2019 study, Google showed that multi-factor authentication stopped 99% of automated and phish-\ning attacks (https://security.googleblog.com/2019/05/new-research-how-effective-is-basic.\nhtml). Adding multi-factor authentication to the identity provider OpenUnison authenticates against,\nor integrating it directly into OpenUnison, is one of the most effective ways to secure the dashboard\nand your cluster.\nNext, we’ll look at what’s changed with the new release of the dashboard."],[39320,"Chapter 10 310\n--name local_registry \\\n-p 5000:5000 \\\n-v ./registry_data:/var/lib/registry:z \\\n--restart=always registry:2\nWe now have a local registry without authentication that can be used to push the test images.\nAs we mentioned previously, the registry is unaware of the image's detached signature.\nPodman must be able to write signatures on a staging sigstore. There is already a default\nconfiguration in the /etc/containers/registries.d/default.yaml file, which looks as\nfollows:\ndefault-docker:\n# sigstore: file:///var/lib/containers/sigstore\nsigstore-staging: file:///var/lib/containers/sigstore\nuse-sigstore-attachments: true\nThe sigstore-staging path is where Podman writes image signatures; this path must be a\nwritable folder, and it is possible to customize it or keep the default configuration as is. The\nuse-sigstore-attachments: true option gives Podman permissions to upload sigstores as\nsidecar files compatible with the OCI artifact format.\nFor rootless users, defining a custom path in the home directory allows Podman to successfully\nwrite signatures without requiring elevated privileges.\nIf we want to create multiple user-related sigstores, we can create the $HOME/.config/\ncontainers/registries.d/default.yaml file and define a custom sigstore-staging path in\nthe user's home directory, following the same syntax that was shown in the previous example.\nThis will allow users to run Podman in rootless mode and successfully write to their sigstore.\nSince we want to idx_91845d50 demonstrate the native integration between idx_9127120c Podman and Cosign, we will use\nthe keys we idx_9642b985 generated to sign the image during the push process. Unlike idx_ce3d09a6 older GPG-based\nImportant\nIn Podman 5.x, the preferred method is to store signatures directly in the registry as OCI\nartifacts. This removes the need to manage a separate sigstore directory or worry about\ncross-user filesystem permissions.\nNote\n311 Securing Containers\nworkflows, Podman 5.x allows rootless users to sign and push images seamlessly as long as\nthey have access to their cosign.key file.\nThe following example shows the Dockerfile of a custom httpd image that's been built using"],[25675,"Chapter 10 310\nBefore we continue, let's clean up and remove all the containers:\n$ docker container rm -f $(docker container ls -aq)\nNow, we can remove the two custom networks that we created explicitly:\n$ docker network rm sample-net\n$ docker network rm sample-net-2\n...\nAlternatively, we i d x _ 4 3 5 2 7 e c 1 could remove all the networks that have no container attached to them with the prune command:\n$ docker network prune --force\nWe used the --force (or -f) argument here to prevent Docker from reconfirming that we really want to remove all\nunused networks. In our case, we recommend the latter command:\nFigure 10.15: Pruning all unused Docker networks\nDouble-check with i d x _ 7 1 9 0 4 5 7 3 the docker network ls command that you are only left with the three default networks\nprovided by Docker.\nThe next network types we are going to inspect a bit are the host and null network types.\nThe host and null network types\nIn addition to the bridge driver, Docker offers two special local network drivers: host and none. Both are part of the\ncore CNM implementation, but each serves a very different purpose. Let's start with the former.\nThe host network\nThe host driver i d x _ 3 2 a d d e 9 a removes the network namespace isolation between the container and the host. This means the\ncontainer shares the host's network stack directly—it does not get its own private IP address. Instead, it uses the\nhost's IP and can bind directly to the host's interfaces.\n311 Using Single-Host Networking\nThe following are some i d x _ 0 8 1 4 0 b f 5 use cases:\nRunning high-performance network services where the extra NAT layer would add latency\nContainers that need to discover services via broadcast/multicast on the host's network\nDebugging tools that need to inspect the host's network directly\nThe following are i d x _ b b a 0 8 8 b 9 important caveats:\nLinux only: The --network host mode is only fully supported on native Linux.\nDocker Desktop (Mac/Windows): Containers run inside a lightweight VM, so --network host attaches\nto the VM's network, not your physical host. To access services from your Mac or Windows host, you\nmust still publish ports.\nSecurity: Host networking removes isolation—if a container is compromised, the attacker can sniff all\nnetwork traffic on the host. When running business software in containers, there is no good reason to\never run the respective containers attached to the host's network. For security reasons, it is strongly\nrecommended that you do not run any such container attached to the host network in a i d x _ 0 c b 2 1 4 1 3 production or\nproduction-like environment.\nThat said, how can we run a container inside the network namespace of the host? Simply by attaching the\ncontainer to the host network:\nRun an Alpine i d x _ a 8 1 b 8 1 1 3 container and attach it to the host network:"],[86444,"Chapter 10 310\nFigure 10.24: Provide your secret name, replacing the placeholder shown\nAt line number 157, provide the bucket name that contains the recipe book,\nreplacing the placeholder\nAt line number 158, provide the file name and path of the recipe eBook, replacing\nthe idx_512cdd15 placeholder. For example, the full path would be recipe ‑ book/ebook.pdf –\nsee screenshot in Figure 10.25:\nFigure 10.25: Provide the bucket name and file path of your recipe book\nAt line number 166, replace the example email address with the email address\nyou verified in Project task 10.6\nSave the file with the changes made\nNext, back in your AWS Management console, search for and navigate to the Amazon\nS3 service\nEnsure that you are still in the London region\nFrom the left-hand menu, click General purpose buckets\nIn the right-hand pane, click the Create bucket button\nb.\nc.\nd."],[55457,"Chapter 10 311\nb. Username and password\nc. service account\nd. Multi-factor authentication\n3. How does the dashboard track the session state?\na. Sessions are stored in etcd\nb. Sessions are stored in custom resource objects called DashboardSession\nc. There are no sessions\nd. If a token is uploaded, it’s encrypted and stored in the browser as a cookie\n4. When using a token, how often can the dashboard refresh it?\na. Once a minute\nb. Every thirty seconds\nc. When the token expires\nd. None of the above\n5. What’s the best way to deploy the dashboard?\na. Using kubectl port-forward\nb. Using kubectl proxy\nc. With a secret Ingress host\nd. Behind a reverse proxy\n6. The dashboard doesn’t support impersonation.\na. True\nb. False\n7. OpenUnison is the only reverse proxy that supports the dashboard.\na. True\nb. False\nAnswers\n1. b\n2. a – There must be a token\n3. c – When your token expires, you’ll be asked for a new one\n4. d – The dashboard can’t refresh tokens\n5. d – Better security and usability\n6. b\n7. b\n11\nExtending Security Using Open\nPolicy Agent\nSo far, we have covered Kubernetes’ built-in authentication and authorization capabilities, which help\nto secure a cluster. While this will cover most use cases, it doesn’t cover all of them. Some security\nbest practices that Kubernetes can’t handle are pre-authorizing container registries and ensuring that\nIngress objects don’t overlap (though most Ingress controllers do check, such as NGINX).\nThese tasks are left to outside systems and are called dynamic admission controllers. Open Policy\nAgent (OPA) and its Kubernetes native sub-project, Gatekeeper, is one of the most popular ways to\nhandle these use cases. This chapter will detail the deployment of OPA and Gatekeeper, how OPA is\narchitected, and how to develop policies.\nIn this chapter, we will cover the following topics:\n• Introduction to dynamic admission controllers\n• What is OPA and how does it work?\n• Using Rego to write policies\n• Enforcing Ingress policies\n• Mutating objects and default values\n• Creating policies without Rego\nOnce you’ve completed this chapter, you’ll be on your way to developing and implementing important\npolicies for your cluster and workloads.\nTechnical requirements\nTo complete the hands-on exercises in this chapter, you will require an Ubuntu 22.04 server.\nYou can access the code for this chapter at the following GitHub repository: https://github.com/\nPacktPublishing/Kubernetes-An-Enterprise-Guide-Third-Edition/tree/main/chapter11.\nExtending Security Using Open Policy Agent 314\nIntroduction to dynamic admission controllers\nAn admission controller is a specialized webhook in Kubernetes that runs when an object is created,\nupdated, or deleted. When one of these three events happens, the API server sends information about\nthe object and operation to the webhook. Admission controllers can be used to either determine if an"],[86449,"Chapter 10 312\nFigure 10.27: Upload the application source code folder to the Amazon S3 bucket\nScroll to the bottom of the webpage and click the Upload button. The files and folder\nstructure will be uploaded to the bucket.\nClick the Close button once the upload is complete\nYour application source code has now been uploaded to the Amazon S3 repository. Next you\nwill configure the Auto Scaling service to provision your EC2 instances, and create a user-data\nscript that will be used to download and deploy this application source code to the EC2\ninstances provisioned by the Auto Scaling Group.\nProject task 10.8 – Launching an AWS auto scaling group\nIn this project task, you will create a launch template and an auto scaling group to\nautomatically deploy the application to a couple idx_da65e4a0 of EC2 instances, which will be deployed into\nthe app subnets of Belly Brew Co. Limited's VPC. The EC2 instances will be configured with the\nnecessary IAM roles to connect with the relevant backend services. The application code in the\nindex.php file has also been updated with the required settings of your environment to ensure\nfunctionality.\nFrom the GitHub repository, you should have also downloaded the user_data.sh bash script\nfile. This file will need to be updated as per your environment and then used to deploy the\napplication on the EC2 instances, which are deployed by the auto scaling group.\nThe following instructions detail the steps to follow to create an Auto Scaling Group for your\napplication:\nUsing your IDE, such as Visual Studio Code, open the user_data.sh bash script file as\nper the following screenshot in Figure 10.28:"],[25677,"Chapter 10 312\nHere, I can see i d x _ 7 a c 8 0 4 f e that 192.168.65.3 is the IP address that the host has been assigned and that the MAC\naddress shown here also corresponds to that of the host.\nWe can also inspect the routes:\n/ # ip route\nOn my macOS system, this is what I get:\nFigure 10.17 – Routes from within a container\nWhen done, exit the container with Ctrl + D to clean up.\nBefore we move on to the next section of this chapter, I want to once again point out that running a container on\nthe host network can be dangerous due to potential security vulnerabilities and conflicts:\nSecurity risks: By using the host network, the container has the same network access as the host\nmachine. This means that if an application running within the container has a vulnerability that is\nexploited, the attacker could gain access to the host network and potentially compromise other services\nor data.\nPort conflicts: When a container uses the host network, it shares the same network namespace as the\nhost. This means that if your containerized application and a host application listen on the same port,\nthere can be conflicts.\nIsolation: One of the major benefits of using Docker is the isolation it provides at various levels (process,\nfilesystem, or network). By using the host network, you lose this level of isolation, which could lead to\nunforeseen issues.\nTherefore, it's generally recommended to use a user-defined network instead of the host network when running\nDocker containers, as it provides better isolation and reduces the risk of conflicts and security vulnerabilities.\nThe null (none) network\nThe null (or none) driver creates a i d x _ d 0 8 e e 6 8 0 network namespace for the container but doesn't connect it to any network. The\ncontainer will still have a loopback interface (lo) but cannot reach any other container or the outside world.\nThe following are use cases:\nRunning i d x _ 2 a 6 6 8 f b 1 security-sensitive workloads that should not have any external connectivity\nContainers performing offline processing where all required data is mounted via volumes\nForensics or analysis containers where isolation from all other network traffic is required\nSometimes, we need to run a few application services or jobs that do not need any network connection at all to\nexecute the task at hand. It is strongly advised that you run those applications in a container that is attached to the\n3.\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n313 Using Single-Host Networking\nnone network. This container will be completely isolated and is thus safe from any outside access. Let's run such a\ncontainer:\n$ docker container run \\\n--rm -it \\\n--network none \\\nalpine:3.22 /bin/sh\nOnce inside the container, we can verify that there is no eth0 network endpoint available:\n/ # ip addr show eth0\nip: can't find device 'eth0'\nThere is also no routing i d x _ 6 4 c 2 c 2 1 9 information available, as we can demonstrate by using the following command:"],[39323,"Chapter 10 312\nStoring signatures\nThe preceding idx_259b9125 code successfully pushed the image blobs to the idx_27b9410f registry and stored the image\nsignature. With that, we idx_14c44362 have successfully pushed and signed the idx_333e682c image, making it more\nsecure for future use. Now, let's learn how to configure Podman to retrieve signed images.\nConfiguring Podman to pull signed images\nTo successfully idx_bad368ea pull a signed image, Podman must be able to retrieve the signature from a\nsignature store and have access to a public key to verify it.\nIn the modern Sigstore workflow used by Podman 5.x, the registry is signature aware. This\nmeans that when you push an image with a signature, Podman stores that signature directly in\nthe registry as an OCI artifact (a sidecar object).\nBecause the signature is now stored alongside the image, we no longer need to maintain an\nexternal web server or a separate directory to host signature files. This greatly simplifies the\nDevOps pipeline, as the registry becomes the single source of truth for both the image layers\nand their cryptographic proof.\nNow, let's configure Podman for image pulling by defining which registries are allowed to use\nthis feature. Unlike the legacy detached model, Podman 5.x can retrieve signatures directly from\nthe registry. To ensure Podman looks for these integrated signatures, we edit the /etc/\ncontainers/registries.d/default.yaml file to enable Sigstore attachments:\ndocker:\nlocalhost:5000:\nuse-sigstore-attachments: true\nhttp://localhost:8080/\nWhile we are using integrated signatures for our local registry, you can still define external\nsignature stores for other registries. For example, the following code shows how Podman\nlocates signatures for the public Red Hat registry:\ndocker:\nregistry.access.redhat.com:\nsigstore: https://access.redhat.com/webassets/docker/content/sigstore\nBefore we test the image pulls, we must implement the public key that's used by Podman to\nverify the signatures. This public key (cosign.pub) must be stored in the host that pulls the\nimage and belongs to the key pair that's used to sign the image.\n313 Securing Containers\nThe configuration idx_10141e62 file that's used to enforce these security rules is /etc/containers/\npolicy.json.\nThe following code shows a custom configuration that requires a valid Sigstore signature for\nany image pulled from localhost:5000:\n{\n\"default\": [\n{\n\"type\": \"insecureAcceptAnything\"\n}\n],\n\"transports\": {\n\"docker\": {\n\"localhost:5000\": [\n{\n\"type\": \" sigstoreSigned \",\n\"keyPath\": \"/etc/pki/containers/cosign.pub\",\n\"signedIdentity\": { \"type\": \"matchRepository\" }\n}\n]\n},\n\"docker-daemon\": {\n\"\": [\n{\n\"type\": \"insecureAcceptAnything\"\n}\n]\n}\n}\n}\nTo verify the signatures of images that have been pulled from localhost:5000, we can use a\npublic key that's stored in the path defined by the keyPath field. The public key must exist in"],[86451,"Chapter 10 314\nBack in the web browser, from the search bar at the top of the AWS Management\nConsole, search for and navigate to the EC2 service\nFrom the left-hand menu, click on Launch Templates in the Instances section\nNext, in the right-hand pane, click the Create launch template button\nProvide a name and idx_e3bd1b6c description for your launch template. For example, you can name\nthe template bb ‑ app ‑ launch ‑ template\nClick the Quick Start tab under Application and OS Images (Amazon Machine\nImage)\nNext, select the Amazon Linux icon and ensure that the AMI is Amazon Linux 2023\nkernel-6.1 AMI\nSelect t3.micro from the Instance type drop-down list\nEnsure that the Key pair name is set to Don't include in launch template\nScroll further down and, in the Network settings section, ensure you select the Select\nexisting security group option. From the Security groups, select the App Security\nGroup you created in your VPC from the drop-down list as per the following screenshot\nin Figure 10.30:\nFigure 10.30: Select the App Security Group for your EC2 instances"],[25679,"Chapter 10 314\nnamespace of an existing container. With this technique, we can run multiple containers in a single network\nnamespace:\nFigure 10.18 – Multiple containers running in a single network namespace\nIn the i d x _ 4 9 d 7 a c 3 e preceding diagram, we can see that in the i d x _ 8 b c 2 2 8 5 a leftmost network namespace, we have two containers. The two\ncontainers, since they share the same namespace, can communicate on localhost with each other. The network\nnamespace (and not the individual containers) is then attached to the front network.\nThis is useful when we want to debug the network of an existing container without running additional processes\ninside that container. We can just attach a special utility container to the network namespace of the container to\ninspect. This feature is also used by Kubernetes when it creates a Pod. We will learn more about Kubernetes and\nPods in subsequent chapters of this book.\nNow, let's demonstrate how this works:\nFirst, we create a new bridge network:\n$ docker network create \\\n--driver bridge test-net\nNext, we run a container attached to this network:\n$ docker container run \\\n--name web -d \\\n--network test-net \\\nnginx:1.29-alpine\nFinally, we run another container and attach it to the network of our web container. Once again, we're\nusing the Alpine image for this:\n$ docker container run \\\n-it --rm \\"],[39325,"Chapter 10 314\nsecurity context, this line is the equivalent of leaving your front door wide open and hanging a\nWelcome sign for everyone.\nMost Linux distributions idx_2ddb75fe ship with this configuration enabled by default to ensure a smooth\nout-of-the-box experience.\nNow, we are ready to test the image pull and verify its signature. Ensure your cosign.pub file is\nin the path defined in your policy, then run the following:\n$ podman pull --tls-verify=false localhost:5000/custom_httpd\nGetting image source signatures\nChecking if image destination supports signatures\nCopying blob 23fdb56daf15 skipped: already exists\nCopying blob d4f13fad8263 skipped: already exists\nCopying blob 96b0fdd0552f done\nCopying config 569b015109 done\nWriting manifest to image destination\nStoring signatures\n569b015109d457ae5fabb969fd0dc3cce10a3e6683ab60dc10505fc2d68e769f\nThe image was idx_a3a4e5c6 successfully pulled into the local store after signature verification using the\npublic key provided. If the signature had been missing or tampered with, Podman would have\nrejected the pull immediately.\nNow, let's see how Podman behaves when it is unable to correctly verify the signature.\nTesting signature verification failures\nA security policy is only as idx_31b84d20 good as its ability to block untrusted content. To verify that our\npolicy is working, we should test what happens when verification fails. First, let's remove our\nlocal image to ensure Podman is forced to pull it from the registry and perform the check:\n$ podman rmi localhost:5000/custom_httpd\nWhat happens if an image exists in the registry but has not been signed? We can simulate this\nby pushing the same image with a different tag without using the --sign-by flag and then\ntrying to pull the image again:\n$ podman tag custom_httpd localhost:5000/custom_httpd:unsigned\n$ podman push --tls-verify=false localhost:5000/custom_httpd:unsigned\n$ podman pull --tls-verify=false localhost:5000/custom_httpd:unsigned\nError: unable to copy from source docker://localhost:5000/custom_httpd:unsigned:\nSource image rejected: A signature was required, but no signature exists\n315 Securing Containers\nThe preceding error demonstrates that since our policy.json requires a signature for any\nimage from localhost:5000, Podman will automatically reject it.\nAnother common failure occurs when the public key used for verification does not match the\nprivate key used for signing. To test this, we can modify our /etc/containers/policy.json\nfile to point to an incorrect public key. For example, we could temporarily point keyPath to a\npublic key from another project or generate a dummy key:\n# Generate a dummy \"wrong\" key\n$ cosign generate-key-pair --output-key-prefix dummy\nUpdate /etc/containers/policy.json to use the dummy.pub public key, then try to pull the"],[26921,"Chapter 10 315\nSELinux confines the processes and services to only perform what\nthey are supposed to do, and only over resources that are autho-\nrized. This is a really important feature that keeps things under\ncontrol. Even in the event of software bugs that might lead to\naccess to unexpected files or resources, SELinux will block them\nif they have not been authorized by the active policy.\nSELinux permissions al-\nways come after regular\nDAC. If a user has no ac-\ncess to a file because of\nimproper file permissions,\nSELinux has nothing to do\nwith it.\nBy default, the installation of a system sets the configuration of\nSELinux to the enforcing mode and uses the targeted policy. It\nis possible to check your current system status via the execution\nof sestatus:\nFigure 10.1 – Output of sestatus for our system\nAs we can see, our system has SELinux enabled, using the targeted\npolicy, and is currently in enforcing mode. Let’s learn what that\nmeans.\nSELinux works by defining a policy, that is, a set of predefined\nrules for granting or denying access to resources. The available\nones can be listed via sudo zypper search selinux-policy\nin your system, with targeted, sandbox, and minimum being the\nmost common ones.\nWe will focus on the targeted policy, which is the default one\napplied to the system.\nKeeping the System Hardened with SELinux 316\nThe mode, listed as enforcing, means that the policy is currently being enforced, and all security\nmeasures and restrictions are being applied. On the other hand, we have permissive, which, even\nwhen it is active, will only warn, but not protect.\nWhy do we have permissive instead of just disabling it? This question is a bit tricky, so let’s\nexplain a bit more about how this policy works to provide an answer.\nSELinux uses extended attributes in the filesystem to store the labels. Each time a file is created\nand SELinux is active, a label is assigned based on the policy. This makes disabled different from\npermissive because the first one will not create those labels for new files that are created, while\nthe second one will.\nAdditionally, SELinux in permissive mode, while not blocking anything, allows us to see the\nerrors that would be raised if a program has not received a good policy, has bad behavior for it,\nor if some file has no proper labels.\nIt is really easy to switch from enforcing to permissive, and vice versa, via the setenforce\ncommand, while we can use getenforce to retrieve the current status, as we can see in the fol-\nlowing figure:\nFigure 10.2 – Changing the SELinux enforcing status\nIt might look basic, and it’s really as easy as that—a matter of running a command. However, if\nthe status were disabled, it would be a completely different story.\nThe SELinux status is configured by editing the /etc/selinux/config file, but changes only take"],[86459,"Chapter 10 316\nFigure 10.32: App subnets in Belly Brew Co. Limited's VPC\nAccept the default settings idx_edefd378 under Availability Zone distribution – new and click Next\nIn the Load balancing section, ensure you select the option Attach to an existing load\nbalancer\nSelect the bb ‑ target ‑ group | HTTP from the Existing load balancer target groups\ndrop-down list you created in Project task 10.2. This will be the target group that the\nload balancer will register the EC2 instances it deploys to. This will ensure that\ninbound traffic to the Application Load Balancer is distributed to the instances\ndeployed by the Auto Scaling service\nNext, under Health checks, ensure that you select the option Turn on Elastic Load\nBalancer health checks\nClick Next\nUnder Group size, set the Desired capacity to 2\nFor Scaling, set the Min desired capacity to 2 and the Max desired capacity to 2. This\nwill ensure you always have two instances deployed – one in each App subnet and one\nin each Availability Zone. Should an EC2 instance in one subnet fail, the Auto Scaling\nservice will replace it to maintain the desired capacity of 2. Additionally, for this\nconfiguration, you will idx_c689fcce not need to set any scaling policies.\nScroll down to the bottom of the webpage and click Next"],[25682,"Chapter 10 316\nHow Kubernetes uses this concept\nThis capability is more than just a Docker trick—it's the foundation of Kubernetes Pods. A Pod i d x _ 4 7 e 2 0 e c c can run multiple\ncontainers, but all containers in the Pod share the following:\nThe same network namespace (same IP and ports)\nThe same loopback interface (lo)\nThe same localhost address space\nThis allows sidecar containers (for logging, monitoring, or service mesh) to interact with the main application\ncontainer as if they were part of the same process space. We will learn more about Kubernetes Pods and networks\nin Chapter 15, Introducing Kubernetes.\nPractical debugging use case\nSometimes, we need to troubleshoot networking from within the exact namespace of a running service container,\nwithout modifying that container's image.\nA great way to do this is by running the nicolaka/netshoot image, which includes a full suite of network\ndebugging tools. The following command assumes that your application is running in a container named myapp:\n$ docker container run --rm -it \\\n--network container:myapp \\\nnicolaka/netshoot\nFrom here, we can run commands such as curl, dig, tcpdump, or traceroute in the exact same network context as\nmyapp.\nIn the next section, we are going to learn how to expose container ports on the container host.\nManaging container ports\nNow that we know how we can i d x _ 0 d 2 a b 8 9 a isolate firewall containers from each other by placing them on different networks,\nand that we can have a container attached to more than one network, we have one problem that remains unsolved.\nHow can we expose an application service to the outside world? Imagine a container running a web server hosting\nour webAPI from before. We want customers from the internet to be able to access this API. We have designed it to\nbe a publicly accessible API. To achieve this, we have to, figuratively speaking, open a gate in our firewall through\nwhich we can funnel external traffic to our API. For security reasons, we don't just want to open the doors wide; we\nwant to have a single controlled gate that traffic flows through.\n•\n•\n•\nSecurity note\nSharing a network namespace also means sharing all of its network-level privileges. If one container is\ncompromised, the attacker could monitor or manipulate traffic for all containers in that namespace.\nNote\n317 Using Single-Host Networking\nWe can create this kind of gate by mapping a container port to an available port on the host. We're also calling this\ncontainer port to publish a port. Remember that the container has its own virtual network stack, as does the host.\nTherefore, container ports and host ports exist completely independently and by default have nothing in common\nat all, but we can now wire a container port with a free host port and funnel external traffic through this link, as"],[39327,"Chapter 10 316\nThe following code shows how to print the current configuration with the podman image\ntrust show command:\n$ podman image trust show\nhttp://localhost:8080/"],[26923,"Chapter 10 317\nThe general advice is to leave SELinux in the enforcing mode, but if, for whatever reason, it was\ndisabled, the recommendation is to switch SELinux to permissive as the first step when moving\nfrom disabled. This will allow us to check that the system actually works without being locked\nout of it because of the kernel blocking access to files and resources.\nWhy use disabled instead of setting it to permissive? For example, some software might re-\nquire setting SELinux to disabled even if it can later be reenabled for operations, or there might\nbe other reasons. Bear in mind that SELinux is a security feature that protects your system and\nshould be kept enabled.\nSELinux uses Access Vector Cache (AVC) messages that are logged to the /var/log/audit/audit.\nlog file as well as system journals. And yes, it’s a cache, so rules are not checked as frequently to\nspeed up the operations.\nLet’s go back to the idea of the filesystem storing labels, and let’s jump into the next section to\nsee how they relate to processes, files, and RBAC, as provided by SELinux.\nReviewing SELinux context for files and processes\nSELinux uses labels, also referred to as a security context attached to each file, and defines several\naspects. Let’s check one example in our home folder with the ls -l command, but with a special\nmodifier, Z, that will show SELinux attributes as well. We can see these in the following figure:\nFigure 10.3 – File listing showing SELinux attributes\nDuring the reboot after switching from disabled to permissive or enforcing,\nthe system will force a relabeling of the filesystem based on the policy. This is ac -\ncomplished by the creation of a file in the root folder of our filesystem named /\netc/selinux/.autorelabel, which will trigger the process and reboot afterward.\nKeeping the System Hardened with SELinux 318\nLet’s focus on the output for one of the files:\n-rw-------. 1 root root unconfined_u:object_r:admin_home_t:s0 15774 Jul 3\n20:08 .bash_history\nThe SELinux attributes are the ones listed as unconfined_u:object_r:admin_home_t:s0. Let’s\nunderstand the attributes:\n• The first part is the user mapping: unconfined_u\n• The second part is the role: object_r\n• The third part is the type: admin_home_t\n• The fourth part is used for the level: s0 in the multi-level and multi-category security\nSomething similar happens with processes, and similarly, we can append Z to many of the common\ncommands to get the contexts, for example, with ps Z, as we can see in the next figure:\nFigure 10.4 – The ps output with SELinux contexts\nAgain, let’s examine one of the lines:\nunconfined_u:unconfined_r:unconfined_t:s0 1321 pts/1 S 0:00 -bash\nAgain, we can see the same approach: user, role, type, and level for multi-level and multi-category\nsecurity.\nNow that we’ve introduced what it does look like, let’s focus on how it works in the targeted policy."],[86465,"Chapter 10 318\nFigure 10.34: Auto scaling group activity history\nFrom the left-hand menu, click on Target Groups in the Load Balancing section\nIn the right-hand pane, click to open the bb-target-group you configured earlier\nThe two EC2 instances idx_96d6c536 you deployed using the Auto Scaling group should be in a\nhealthy state as shown in Figure 10.35:\nFigure 10.35: EC2 instance reports healthy status\nFrom the left-hand menu, click Load Balancers in the Load Balancing section\nIn the right-hand pane, click the bb-alb application load balancer\nNote the details of the load balancer as per the following screenshot in Figure 10.36:"],[39329,"Chapter 10 318\nFrom now on, every idx_1f1f35f8 time a UBI9 image is pulled idx_0e70b724 from registry.access.redhat.com, its\nsignature will be pulled from the Red Hat signature store and validated using the provided\npublic key.\nSo far, we have looked at examples of managing keys in Podman, but it is also possible to\nmanage signature verification with Skopeo. In the next subsection, we are going to look at\nsome basic examples.\nManaging signatures with Skopeo\nWe can verify an image signature idx_faa5c07e using Skopeo when we're pulling an image from a valid\ntransport.\nThe following idx_f548bbed example uses the skopeo copy command to pull the image from idx_c73c1d49 our registry to\nthe local store. This command has the same effects as using a podman pull command but\nallows more control over the source and destination transports:\n$ skopeo copy --src-tls-verify=false \\\ndocker://localhost:5000/custom_httpd \\\ncontainers-storage:localhost:5000/custom_httpd\nSkopeo idx_0f0c2746 does not need any further configuration because it automatically idx_79d99b4e references the public\nkey path defined in /etc/containers/policy.json. If the signature in the registry does not\nmatch our public key, Skopeo will refuse to copy the image.\nWe can also use Skopeo to sign an image with our private key before copying it to a transport:\n$ skopeo copy \\\n--dest-tls-verify=false \\\n--sign-by-sigstore-private-key ./cosign.key \\\nTip\nRed Hat related registry files have been included in the github.com/containers/\ncommon repository to allow easier integration with Red Hat's ecosystem.\nIt is possible to create custom registry configuration files for different providers in the /\netc/containers/registries.d folder. For example, the preceding example could be\ndefined in a dedicated /etc/containers/registries.d/redhat.yaml file. This\nallows you to easily maintain and version registry sigstore configurations.\nNote\n319 Securing Containers\ncontainers-storage:localhost:5000/custom_httpd \\\ndocker://localhost:5000/custom_httpd\nIn this section, we learned how to verify image signatures and avoid potential MITM attacks. In\nthe next section, we will see how to simplify the keys' creation process with the help of Skopeo.\nGenerating a Sigstore key with Skopeo\nSkopeo provides idx_b35d65cb a useful option that simplifies the creation of keys for idx_2e721061 signing container\nimages.\nFirst of all, let's create a directory for holding the public and private keys we are going to\ngenerate:\n$ mkdir .skopeo\nWe are now ready to leverage Skopeo for the keys' creation process:\n$ skopeo generate-sigstore-key --output-prefix .skopeo/sig-ubi8-httpd\nPassphrase for key .skopeo/sig-ubi8-httpd.private:\nKey written to \".skopeo/sig-ubi8-httpd.private\" and \".skopeo/sig-ubi8-httpd.pub\"\nThe idx_033dcb09 command will also ask for a passphrase in case we want to idx_b88dc48c increase the security level of"],[25684,"Chapter 10 318\nThe Nginx container only exposes port 80, and we can see that it has been mapped to the host port 55000.\nNote that in your case, the port may be different.\nIf we open a new i d x _ d 1 3 0 c 9 1 e browser window and navigate to localhost:55000, we should see the following screen:\nFigure 10.21 – The welcome page of Nginx\nAn alternative way to find out which host port Docker is using for our container is to inspect it. The host\nport is part of the NetworkSettings node:\n$ docker container inspect web | grep HostPort\n\"HostPort\": \"55000\"\nFinally, the third way of getting this information is to list the container:\n$ docker container ls\nYou should see an output similar to this:\nFigure 10.22: Container port mapping in the output of docker container ls\nPlease note that in the i d x _ b 9 7 2 5 5 7 0 preceding output, the /tcp part tells us that the port has been opened for\ncommunication with the TCP protocol, but not for the UDP protocol. TCP is the default, and if we want to\nspecify that we want to open the port for UDP, then we have to specify this explicitly. The special (IP)"],[26925,"Chapter 10 319\nSELinux also features something named transitions. A transition allows one process started by\na user with a binary with some specific role to transition via the execution into some other role,\nwhich is used later to define the permissions for it.\nAs you might imagine, our user also has a SELinux context, and similarly, we can use the id -Z\ncommand to check it:\nunconfined_u:unconfined_r:unconfined_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023\nSo, going back to one example, Apache HTTP Server is provided by the apache2 package, which\ncan be installed via zypper install apache2. Once it is installed, let’s start it with systemctl\nstart apache2 and enable it with systemctl enable apache2. Then, open the firewall with\nfirewall-cmd --add-service=http and firewall-cmd --add-service=https. Finally, add the\nrules as permanent with firewall-cmd --add-service=http --permanent and firewall-cmd\n--add-service=https --permanent, as we’ve done with other services in previous chapters.\nThe aforementioned commands can be found in the script at https://github.com/\nPacktPublishing/SUSE-Linux-Enterprise-Server-16-Official-Administration-Guide/\nblob/main/chapter-10-selinux/install-apache.sh.\nLet’s see how all of this comes into play in this figure:\nFigure 10.5 – Web server SELinux contexts\nWe can see how the executable on disk, located in /usr/sbin, has the httpd_exec_t context,\nthe process is httpd_t, and the files/folder served by it are httpd_sys_content_t, and it works!\nLet’s now create an index.html file in our folder, and let’s move it to the Apache web root folder:\nuser@geeko:~> echo '<html><head><title>Our test</title></head><body>This\nis our test html</body></html>' > index.html\nuser@geeko:~> sudo cp index.html /srv/www/htdocs/index1.html\nuser@geeko:~> sudo mv index.html /srv/www/htdocs/index2.html\nKeeping the System Hardened with SELinux 320\nLet’s see what happens when we try to access the files. Look at the following figure:\nFigure 10.6 – Apache httpd service behavior with the generated files\nAs we can see, one file has one SELinux context and the other has a different one. On top of that,\nApache is denying access to the one we moved (index2.html), but showing the contents for the\none we copied (index1.html).\nWhat has happened here? We copied one file and moved the other out of the same source, but\nthey have two different SELinux contexts.\nWe can retry by disabling SELinux temporarily and setting it to the permissive mode, and then\nchecking what happens. It will be as shown in this figure:\nFigure 10.7 – Retrying with SELinux in permissive mode"],[25687,"Chapter 10 320\n--network host binds to the VM's network stack, not the physical host. We still need -p for host\naccess.\nFrom containers to host, we should use host.docker.internal instead of guessing i d x _ 2 1 0 2 b 1 1 7 the host IP.\nAdvanced publishing patterns\nLet's look at i d x _ f 5 6 7 f 2 e 6 some advanced publishing patterns. We'll start with port ranges. Port ranges are handy when dealing\nwith clusters of similar services:\n$ docker container run -d --name worker-a \\\n-p 12000-12010:12000-12010 \\\nmy/worker\nUse the following publishing pattern if you have IPv4 + IPv6 dual-stack enabled:\n$ docker container run -d --name site \\\n-p 0.0.0.0:8080:80 \\\n-p [::]:8080:80 \\\nnginx:alpine\nFinally, use this i d x _ b e 0 9 f e 4 7 publishing pattern to explicitly expose the protocol on different host ports:\n$ docker container run -d --name svc \\\n-p 9000:9000/tcp \\\n-p 9000:9000/udp \\\nmy/proto-svc\nMultiple services and avoiding collisions\nTwo containers can't share i d x _ f 8 b 4 6 8 0 9 the same host port. We need to give each a i d x _ 2 c 7 c c a d b unique host port:\n$ docker container run -d --name api-v1 -p 8080:80 my/api:v1\n$ docker container run -d --name api-v2 -p 8081:80 my/api:v2\nIf we need a single public entry point, we should only publish our reverse proxy (for example, Traefik or Nginx) and\nkeep all app ports unpublished on an internal bridge network. We will wire host → proxy and proxy → containers.\n◦\n◦\nFor local development, we should prefer loopback binding to avoid exposing dev services on our LAN:\n$ docker container run -d -p 127.0.0.1:8080:80 my/dev-app\nTip\n321 Using Single-Host Networking\nSecurity checklist\nHere is a short i d x _ 7 5 d 4 4 3 f 8 security checklist for your convenience:\nPublish only what you need. Internal services can stay unpublished and live on private user-defined\nbridge networks.\nPrefer 127.0.0.1 binding for local dev to avoid accidental exposure on Wi-Fi/LAN.\nFor production, terminate TLS at a reverse proxy/ingress and keep app ports private.\nBe mindful of UDP: it's connectionless and easier to spoof—only open what's truly i d x _ 1 8 f c a 9 7 3 required.\nIn the next section, we will talk about HTTP routing using a reverse proxy.\nHTTP-level routing using a reverse proxy\nImagine you have been i d x _ 7 f 1 e 1 c e 8 tasked with containerizing a monolithic application. The application has organically\nevolved over the years into an unmaintainable behemoth. Changing even a minor feature in the source code may\nbreak other features due to the tight coupling existing in the code base. Releases are rare due to their complexity\nand require the whole team to be on deck. The application has to be taken down during the release window, which\ncosts the company a lot of money due to lost opportunities, not to mention damage to its reputation."],[39331,"Chapter 10 320\nBefore diving into the implementation, let's clarify the roles of these two key players:\nCosign: A idx_2b0cff55 lightweight tool designed specifically for signing and verifying container\nimages. It adheres idx_aac688a9 to Open Container Initiative (OCI) standards, ensuring\ncompatibility and seamless integration with popular container registries.\nRekor: A idx_c959fe80 transparency log server that provides an immutable record of all metadata\ngenerated during the signing process. This immutability guarantees the integrity of\nsignatures, preventing any retroactive tampering or revocation.\nWe already idx_8f55a338 have our cosign binary installed on the system. For this idx_9486cc61 example, we are not going\nto deploy a Rekor server, even idx_61c98368 though the project page explains in detail all the idx_14fce412 prerequisites\nneeded. Instead, we are going to leverage the public Rekor server offered by the open source\ncommunity, available at https://rekor.sigstore.dev.\nWe are now ready to test both Cosign and Rekor for signing our first container image with the\nhelp of these tools.\nWe are going to sign the container image we created earlier, custom_httpd, through the sig-\nubi8-httpd key created through Skopeo. We execute the signature process through the\nfollowing command:\n$ cosign sign --key .cosign.key quay.io/alezzandro/ubi9-\nhttpd@sha256:cda838d6ef3ddf951c1c7f1086d1aa1ed895159a4e794b8629da66b35b94c83f\nEnter password for private key:\n...\ntlog entry created with index: 165166231\nPushing signature to: quay.io/alezzandro/ubi9-httpd\nAs shown in the previous command, the best practice is to sign a container image based on its\ndigest. This avoids any mismatch if we override an image tag with a newer release.\n•\n•\nNote\nThe previous command requires that the container image resides in a container registry\nthat is also capable of handling the image signatures. You should be authenticated\nagainst the container image registry, and you should have the right permissions for\nexecuting these actions.\nNote\n321 Securing Containers\nBehind the idx_d8f65457 scenes, Cosign generates a cryptographic signature idx_6d884a24 for your container image and\ncreates an entry in idx_b5c4587c the Rekor public server log, recording the idx_802ea7c9 signature along with crucial\nmetadata such as the image digest, public key used for signing, and timestamp.\nLet's inspect the image signature and its log stored on the Rekor public server.\nFirst, we are going to download one of the latest Rekor command-line interfaces from the\npublic GitHub repository:\n$ curl -O -L https://github.com/sigstore/rekor/releases/download/v1.3.8/rekor-\ncli-linux-amd64\n$ chmod +x rekor-cli-linux-amd64\nWe are now ready to query the Rekor public server with the following command:\n$ ./rekor-cli-linux-amd64 get --log-index 165166231"],[86468,"Chapter 10 320\nScroll down Belly Brew Co. Limited's webpage idx_4568d8a4 until you get to the customer signup\nsection. You will note the form on the webpage where customers would provide their\ncontact details and, in return, get a free recipe eBook as per the following screenshot in\nFigure 10.38:\nFigure 10.38: Website form encouraging customers to provide their contact details for a free recipe\neBook\nGo ahead and fill out the form using the same email address you verified for Project task"],[26926,"Chapter 10 321\nAs we can see in the figure, we are now able to access the file contents, so one could say, “ What is\nwrong with SELinux that does not allow my site to work? ”, but the right way to express it would be\n“ Look how SELinux has protected us from disclosing personal files on a website.”\nIf, instead of directly moving a file into Apache’s DocumentRoot directory (/srv/www/htdocs), an\nattacker were trying to reach our home folder files, SELinux would have denied access to those, as,\nby default, the httpd_t process cannot access the user_home_t context.\nA similar thing happens when we try to get Apache, or any other service under the targeted\npolicy, to listen on a port that is not the one configured by default. The best way to get familiar\nwith what we can or cannot do is to learn about the semanage utility.\nUsing semanage, we can list, edit, add, and delete the different values in the SELinux policy, and\neven export and import our customizations. So, let’s use it to learn a bit more about it using our\nexample with httpd.\nLet’s learn about semanage in the following section.\nTweaking the policy with semanage\nAs we introduced earlier, the targeted policy contains some configurations that are enforced\nfor the services it has defined, allowing the protection of those services while not interfering\nwith the ones it does not know about. The Apache httpd server is one of the services with the\nbest-defined SELinux policy.\nStill, there are times when we need to adjust certain settings—for example, allowing the http or\nssh daemon to listen on alternate ports, or granting access to specific file types—without losing\nthe extra layer of protection that SELinux provides. These are uncommon or unexpected behaviors,\nbut sometimes necessary. First, let’s ensure that policycoreutils, policycoreutils-python,\nand policycoreutils-python-utils are installed in our system with sudo zypper install\npolicycoreutils policycoreutils-python policycoreutils-python-utils, as they provide\nthe tools we will use in this and the next sections of this chapter.\nLet’s learn from an example. Let’s see which ports httpd_t can access with semanage port -l\n| grep http:\nuser@geeko:~> sudo semanage port -l | grep http\nhttp_cache_port_t tcp 8080, 8118, 8123, 10001-10010\nhttp_cache_port_t udp 3130\nhttp_port_t tcp 80, 81, 443, 488, 8008, 8009, 8443, 9000\nhttp_port_t udp 80, 443\nKeeping the System Hardened with SELinux 322\npegasus_http_port_t tcp 5988\npegasus_https_port_t tcp 5989\nAs we can see, the http_port_t used by the Apache daemon, by default, is allowed to use ports\n80, 81, 443, 488, 8008, 9009, 8443, and 9000 via tcp.\nThat means that if we want to run Apache on any of those ports, no changes to the policy will\nbe required.\nIf we repeat the command but for ssh, we only see port 22 opened (executing sudo semanage"],[25689,"Chapter 10 322\nCopy the sample folder with content directly into the chapter-10 folder:\n$ cp -r solutions/city-tours .\nNavigate to the city-tours folder:\n$ cd city-tours\nCreate a virtual environment using the uv tool. Activate the virtual environment and install the\napplication requirements:\n$ uv venv\n$ source .venv/bin/activate\n$ uv pip install -r requirements.txt\nYou should see something like this:\nFigure 10.23: Creating a virtual environment and installing dependencies\nDefine i d x _ 1 9 6 c 1 3 e f necessary environment variables and finally, run the application:\n$ export FLASK_APP=app.py\n$ flask run\nThe application will start and listen on localhost on port 5000:"],[86474,"Chapter 10 322\nDelete the Load Balancers and Target Group:\nFrom the left-hand menu, click Load Balancers in the Load Balancing section.\nIn the right-hand pane, select idx_ce147b54 the bb-alb load balancer, and then select the\nDelete load balancer option from the Actions drop-down list.\nIn the dialog box that appears, type in confirm to confirm the deletion and click\nthe Delete button\nNext, from the left-hand menu, click Target Groups in the Load Balancing\nsection. In the right-hand pane, select bb-target-group. Next, select Delete\nfrom the Actions drop-down list. Click the Delete button to confirm deletion.\nDelete the Secret from AWS Secrets Manager and disable the rotation policy\nFrom the search bar at the top of the AWS Management Console, search for and\nnavigate to CloudFormation. AWS uses CloudFormation to create and deploy a\nLambda function that is responsible for rotating the secret in Secrets Manager.\nIn AWS CloudFormation, you will note that there are two Secret Manager stacks.\nSelect the second stack (NOT the Nested one) in the right-hand pane. Click the\nDelete stack button and confirm the deletion by following the instructions in the\ndialog box that appears. This process will take a few minutes to complete. You\ncan click on the refresh button to confirm that the stacks have been deleted. The\nnested stack will also get automatically deleted.\nFrom the search bar at the top of the AWS Management Console, search for and\nnavigate to AWS Secrets Manager\nFrom the left-hand menu, click on Secrets\nIn the right-hand pane, click on the bb-db-secret details page. Select Delete\nsecret from the Actions drop-down list.\nIn the dialog box that appears, set the Waiting period to 7 days and click the\nSchedule deletion button\nDelete the Amazon RDS database\nFrom the search bar at the idx_09f0a6f0 top of the AWS Management Console, search for and\nnavigate to Amazon RDS\nFrom the left-hand menu, click Databases\nIn the right-hand pane, select Belly Brew Co. Limited's database (bellybrewdb)\nSelect Delete from the Actions drop-down list\nDe-select the option to Create final snapshot\nDe-select the option to Retain automatic backups"],[39334,"Chapter 10 322\ndigest\":\"sha256:cda838d6ef3ddf951c1c7f1086d1aa1ed895159a4e794b8629da66b35b94c83f\"}\n,\"type\":\"cosign container image signature\"},\n... omitted output\nAs shown before, the Rekor log provides an undeniable audit trail, preventing anyone from\ndenying their involvement in signing an image. Anyone can independently audit the Rekor log\nto verify the authenticity and integrity of image signatures. Rekor adds a critical layer of\nsecurity, making it significantly harder for attackers to compromise your image signing\nprocess.\nTo maximize efficiency and security, we can integrate Cosign and Rekor into our CI/CD\npipelines. This automation ensures that all images are signed and verified as part of your\ndevelopment workflow.\nFinally, by idx_551498c0 combining Cosign and Rekor, you can establish a robust idx_665151c1 and transparent system for\nmanaging container idx_64f53a2b image signatures, significantly strengthening idx_86df5871 your software supply chain\nagainst potential threats.\nIn the next section, we'll shift focus and learn how to execute the container's runtime by\ncustomizing Linux kernel capabilities.\nCustomizing Linux kernel capabilities\nCapabilities are features idx_a8162b48 that were introduced in Linux kernel 2.2 with the purpose of splitting\nelevated privileges into single units that can be arbitrarily assigned to a process or thread.\nInstead of running a process as a fully privileged instance with effective UID 0, we can assign a\nlimited subset of specific capabilities to an unprivileged process. By providing more granular\ncontrol over the security idx_f2565091 context of the process's execution, this approach helps mitigate\npotential attack tactics.\nBefore we discuss the capabilities of containers, let's recap how they work in a Linux system so\nthat we understand their inner logic.\nUnderstanding Linux Kernel capabilities\nCapabilities are idx_c52fde46 associated with the file executables using extended attributes (see man xattr)\nand are granted to the resulting process upon an execve() system call, subject to the kernel's\ncapability transition rules.\nThe list of available capabilities is quite large and still growing; it includes very specific actions\nthat can be performed by a thread. Some basic examples are as follows:\nCAP_CHOWN: This capability allows a thread to modify a file's UID and GID. •\n323 Securing Containers\nCAP_KILL: This capability allows you to bypass the permission checks to send a signal\nto a process.\nCAP_MKNOD: This capability allows you to create a special file with the mknod() syscall.\nCAP_NET_ADMIN: This capability allows you to operate various privileged actions on the\nsystem's network configuration, including changing the interface configuration,\nenabling/disabling promiscuous mode for an interface, editing routing tables, and"],[26928,"Chapter 10 323\nAs we can see, there is a regular expression that matches the files in the www folder inside the /srv/\nwww/ path, applying to all files and setting httpd_sys_rw_content_t, which allows read-write\naccess. There are folders here that can be used by the popular blog software WordPress. So, the\npolicy is already prepared for covering some of the most popular service folders’ requirements\nwithout requiring system administrators to write them ad hoc.\nWhen invoking semanage, it will output that it has some subcommands we can use, as follows:\n• import: Allows importing local modifications\n• export: Allows exporting the local changes\n• login: Allows managing the login and SELinux user associations\n• user: Manages SELinux users with roles and levels\n• port: Manages port definitions and types\n• ibpkey: Manages InfiniBand definitions\n• ibendport: Manages end port InfiniBand definitions\n• interface: Defines network interface definitions\n• module: Manages policy modules for SELinux\n• node: Manages the definition of network nodes\n• fcontext: Manages file context definitions\n• boolean: Manages Booleans for tweaking policies\n• permissive: Manages the enforcing mode\n• dontaudit: Manages the dontaudit rules in the policy\nFor each one of the aforementioned subcommands, we can use the -h argument to list help and\nto learn about the extra arguments that can be used for each one.\nFor the day-to-day use case, most of the time, we’ll be using port and fcontext, as these will\ncover extending or tuning the available services that come with SUSE Linux Enterprise Server,\nlike the example we have showcased with ssh listening on an additional port.\nKeeping the System Hardened with SELinux 324\nLet’s see how to manually set the context for files and how to restore the defaults in the next section.\nRestoring changed file contexts to the default policy\nIn the previous section, we mentioned that semanage enables us to perform changes to the policy,\nwhich is the recommended way to perform changes and to persist them for future files and folders,\nbut that is not the only way we can perform operations.\nFrom the command line, we can use the chcon utility to change the context for a file. It will al -\nlow us to define the user, the role, and the type for the file we want to alter, and similar to other\nfilesystem utilities such as chmod or chown, we can also affect files recursively, so it’s easy to set a\nfull folder hierarchy to the desired context.\nOne feature that we always found very interesting is the ability to use --reference to copy the full\ncontext of an existing file, so that the same context as the referenced file is applied to the target one.\nWhen we were introducing the example of Apache httpd earlier in this chapter, we did a test with\ntwo files, index1.html and index2.html, which were copied and moved to the /srv/www/htdocs"],[86485,"Chapter 10 324\nAs a Cloud Practitioner, understanding the importance of trade-offs, which services to\nconsume, and how to build and support your workloads is crucial to building a successful\ncareer in cloud computing.\nSummary\nWhen building solutions to host in cloud environments such as AWS, you must design your\napplication to be highly available, resilient to failures, and scalable. Furthermore, to ensure\ncost optimization, you should provision your resources on an as-needed basis, fulfilling\ndemand as it arises.\nOn AWS, you can configure Elastic Load Balancers to distribute incoming traffic across\nmultiple EC2 instances, ECS containers, and Lambda functions to ensure high availability. If a\nparticular EC2 instance fails, the load balancer will stop sending requests to that instance and\ninstead route traffic only to healthy instances. This ensures that end users do not experience\noutages.\nAWS offers various types of load balancers:\nApplication Load Balancers (ALB): Designed to handle HTTP/HTTPS traffic\nNetwork Load Balancers (NLB): Designed to handle TCP/UDP traffic and are capable\nof processing millions of requests per second\nGateway Load Balancers (GLB): Designed to manage traffic for virtual appliances,\ntypically sourced from the AWS Marketplace\nIn addition to achieving high availability, it is equally important to ensure that your\napplication is scalable. The AWS EC2 Auto Scaling service allows you to scale out or scale in\nbased on demand. You can configure scheduled scaling, dynamic scaling, and predictive\nscaling as needed.\nAWS offers several scaling policies to suit your application's needs, including target tracking\nand step scaling. These policies can be defined using metrics such as CPU utilization, request\ncount per target from the load balancer, and many others. Building your solutions using these\ntools and services to ensure high availability and scalability is critical for any business or\norganization.\nIn the next chapter, you will discover some advanced networking concepts designed to help\nyou build solutions for global presence and make your application accessible across multiple\nregions.\n•\n•\n•\n325 High Availability, Fault Tolerance, and Elasticity on AWS\nExam Readiness Drill – Chapter Review Questions\nApart from mastering key concepts, strong test-taking skills under time pressure are essential\nfor acing your certification exam. That's why developing these abilities early in your learning\njourney is critical.\nExam readiness drills, using the free online practice resources provided with this book, help\nyou progressively improve your time management and test-taking skills while reinforcing the\nkey concepts you've learned."],[25693,"Chapter 10 324\nSave your changes and assert that it works by pinging acme.com:\n$ ping acme.com\nPING acme.com (127.0.0.1): 56 data bytes\n64 bytes from 127.0.0.1: icmp_seq=0 ttl=55 time<1 ms\n64 bytes from 127.0.0.1: icmp_seq=1 ttl=55 time<1 ms\n64 bytes from 127.0.0.1: icmp_seq=2 ttl=55 time<1 ms\n...\nAfter all i d x _ d b 4 3 1 0 b b this, it is time to containerize the application. The only change we need to make to the\napplication is ensuring that we have the application web server listening on 0.0.0.0 instead of\nlocalhost.\nWe can do this easily by asserting that the application is running at host='0.0.0.0'. Please verify that\nthe start logic at the end of app.py looks like this:\nif __name__ == '__main__':\napp = create_app()\napp.run(host='0.0.0.0', port=5000)\nThen, we can start the application as follows:\n$ flask run\nNow, add a Dockerfile to the solution folder with the following content:\n9.\nhosts file not editable\nIf you don't have admin rights on your machine, you won't be able to edit the hosts file. Worry\nnot, we have a workaround. If we want to reach the health endpoint of our app at http://\nacme.com instead of localhost:5000/health, we can use the --resolve parameter of curl, as\nfollows:\n$ curl --resolve acme.com:5000:127.0.0.1 \\\nhttp://acme.com:5000/health\nNote"],[39336,"Chapter 10 324\nSee man getcap and man setcap for more details about these utilities.\nWe can inspect the active capabilities of a running process by looking at the /proc/<PID>/\nstatus file. In the following code, we are launching a ping command after setting the\nCAP_NET_ADMIN and CAP_NET_RAW capabilities. We want to launch the process in the\nbackground and check its current capabilities:\n$ ping example.com > /dev/null 2>&1 &\n$ grep 'Cap.*' /proc/$(pgrep ping)/status\nCapInh: 0000000000000000\nCapPrm: 0000000000003000\nCapEff: 0000000000000000\nCapBnd: 000000ffffffffff\nCapAmb: 0000000000000000\nHere, we are idx_7c1a0338 interested in evaluating the bitmap in the CapPrm field, which represents the\npermitted capabilities. To get a user-friendly value, we can use the capsh command to decode\nthe bitmap hex value:\n$ capsh --decode=0000000000003000\n0x0000000000003000=cap_net_admin,cap_net_raw\nThe result is similar to the output of the getcap command in the /usr/bin/ping file,\ndemonstrating that executing the command propagated the file's permitted capabilities to its\nprocess instance.\nFor a full list of the constants that were used to set the bitmaps, as well as their capabilities, see\nthe following kernel header file: https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/\ninclude/uapi/linux/capability.h.\n325 Securing Containers\nNow that we've provided a high-level description of the Linux kernel's capabilities, let's learn\nhow they are applied to containers.\nUsing capabilities in containers\nCapabilities idx_57eb1492 can be applied inside containers to allow targeted actions to take place. By default,\nPodman runs containers using a set of Linux kernel capabilities idx_9b2f76c3 that are defined in the /usr/\nshare/containers/containers.conf file. At the time of writing, the following capabilities are\nenabled and declared inside this file (with comments):\n#default_capabilities = ["],[26930,"Chapter 10 325\nFigure 10.8 – Demonstrating chcon usage\nAs we can see, both index1.html and index3.html now have the proper context—in the first case,\nusing the reference, and in the second, defining the type to use.\nOf course, this is not the only method. As we indicated earlier, the recommended way for setting\ncontext for applications is to define the path’s regular expressions via semanage, or better, use\nthe ones already defined by default. This empowers us to use the restorecon command to apply\nthe right context to the files, according to the configuration. Let’s check how it operates in the\nnext figure:\nFigure 10.9 – Using restorecon to restore context\nAs we can see, we used restorecon -vR /srv/www/htdocs/ and it automatically changed the\nindex2.html file into the httpd_sys_content_t that is defined for that folder, as we saw when\nwe were testing semanage to list the contexts.\nThe arguments used, v and R, make the utility report the changes (verbose) and work recursively\non the paths provided.\nKeeping the System Hardened with SELinux 326\nLet’s say we have messed up the system by running chcon over the root filesystem. What would\nbe the way to fix it? In this case, as we mentioned earlier, we should follow these steps:\n1. Set the operation mode to permissive to not block further accesses via setenforce 0.\n2. Put the marker to have the filesystem relabeled via touch /etc/selinux/.autorelabel.\n3. Modify the /etc/selinux/config file to set the boot mode to permissive.\n4. Reboot the system to let the relabeling happen.\n5. Once the system reboots, edit the /etc/selinux/config file again to define the operation\nmode as enforcing.\nBy operating in this way, instead of just running restorecon -R /, we are making sure that the\nsystem is operational and will continue to operate after reboot, and that a full relabel is applied\nto the filesystem, so it is left ready to re-enable the enforcing mode safely.\nLet’s see the next section on how to tune the policy within itself, using the Booleans to tune how\nit works.\nUsing SELinux Boolean settings to enable services\nMany services have a wide range of configuration options for many common cases, but not al -\nways the same ones. For example, an HTTP server should not access user files, but at the same\ntime, it’s a common way of operation to enable personal websites out of the www or public_html\nfolder in each user’s home directory.\nTo overcome that use case and, at the same time, provide enhanced security, the SELinux policy\nmakes use of Booleans.\nA Boolean is a tunable that can be set by the administrator and can enable or disable conditionals\nin the policy code. For example, let’s get a list of available Booleans for httpd by executing sudo\ngetsebool -a|grep ^http (list reduced):\nuser@geeko:~> sudo getsebool -a | grep ^http"],[39347,"Chapter 10 326"],[25696,"Chapter 10 326\nFigure 10.28 – Testing the monolith while running in a container\nEvidently, the monolith still works exactly the same way as before, even when using the correct URL, that is,\nhttp://acme.com. Great! Now, let's break out part of the monolith's functionality into a Node.js microservice,\nwhich will be deployed separately.\nExtracting the first microservice\nThe team, after some i d x _ 4 f 6 8 c c 6 5 brainstorming, has decided that the city tours catalog is a good candidate for the first piece of\nfunctionality that is cohesive yet self-contained enough to be extracted from the monolith. They decide to\nimplement the product catalog as a microservice implemented in Node.js.\nYou can find the code they came up with and the Dockerfile in the catalog subfolder of the project folder, that is, e-\nshop. It is a simple Express.js application that replicates the functionality that was previously available in the\nmonolith. Let's get started:\nCopy the catalog folder from the solutions folder:\n$ cp -r solutions/catalog .\nIn your Terminal window, from within the catalog folder, build the Docker image for this new\nmicroservice:\n$ docker image build -t acme/catalog:1.0 .\nThen, run a i d x _ d d 8 c 1 b 0 a container from the new image you just built:\n$ docker container run --rm -it \\\n--name catalog \\\n-p 3000:3000 \\\nacme/catalog:1.0\nFrom a different terminal window, try to access the microservice and validate that it returns the same\ndata as the monolith:\n$ curl http://acme.com:3000/catalog/tours?city=paris\nPlease notice the differences in the URL compared to when accessing the same functionality in the monolith. Here,\nwe are accessing the microservice on port 3000 (instead of 5000)."],[86487,"Chapter 10 326\nLink to this Chapter's Quiz\nYou need to unlock the online practice resources to access the link below. Check the Free\nBenefits with Your Book section in the Preface for detailed instructions on how to do this. You\nonly need to unlock the resources once.\nOpen Quiz https://packt.link/clf_c02_ch10 or scan the QR code\n327 High Availability, Fault Tolerance, and Elasticity on AWS\n11\nAWS Networking, Part 2 –\nRoute 53 and CloudFront\nWhen you type a URL into a web browser and are instantly presented with a website, what\nhappens behind the scenes is quite remarkable. The servers hosting these websites on the\nInternet are accessible via a public IP address, which was discussed in Chapter 7. However,\nwhat you enter into the browser's address bar is a domain name in Uniform Resource\nLocator (URL) format—such as https://iaasacademy.com, a website I own and use to deliver\nCloud Computing courses.\nThe \"magic\" happens thanks to a service called the Domain Name System (DNS), which\ntranslates URL-style domain names into their associated IP addresses. This translation allows\nyour browser to locate and connect to the correct server hosting the website you wish to\naccess. You can think of DNS as the Internet's phone book—designed to resolve human-\nreadable domain names into machine-readable IP addresses, enabling devices like your\ndesktop, laptop, or even smartphone to connect to the services provided by web servers\nhosting a wide variety of websites and applications.\nBut DNS does more than map domain names to IP addresses. In this chapter, you will learn\nhow to scale your network architecture by integrating resources across multiple AWS regions\n—a crucial strategy for organizations aiming to enhance global availability, performance, and\nresilience. You will learn how Amazon Route 53, AWS's DNS service, can be used to implement\nintelligent routing strategies across your globally distributed infrastructure. These routing\npolicies enable you to direct user traffic based on latency, geographic location, and health\nchecks, making them ideal for supporting multi-region deployments. Such configurations are\nkey to delivering low-latency access and establishing robust disaster recovery solutions.\nIn addition, this chapter introduces Amazon CloudFront, a globally distributed Content\nDelivery Network (CDN) service. CloudFront accelerates the delivery of your content, such as\nstatic assets, video streams, and APIs, by caching it at edge locations within the AWS Global\nInfrastructure. This reduces the physical distance between your application and your users,\nleading to lower latency and faster load times. By the end of the chapter, you will understand\nhow to architect applications that offer high availability and optimized performance to a\nglobal user base.\nThe following topics are discussed in this chapter:"],[26932,"Chapter 10 327\nThis list is a reduced subset of the available Booleans, but it gives\nus an idea of what can be accomplished. For example, by default,\nhttp cannot use the network to connect to other hosts, or send\nemail (usually done in PHP scripts), and it can’t even access the\nhome folder for users.\nIf we want to enable users in a system to publish their personal\nweb pages from the www folder in their home directory (that is, /\nhome/user/www/), we will have to enable the httpd_enable_\nhomedirs Boolean by running the following:\nuser@geeko:~> sudo setsebool -P httpd_enable_\nhomedirs=1\nThis will tweak the policy to enable http to access the user’s home\ndirectory to serve the pages there. If, additionally, the servers will\nbe stored on an NFS or CIFS mount, additional Booleans will be\nrequired. We’re still using the same targeted policy, but we’ve\nenabled the internal conditionals to permit access so that it is not\nblocked by SELinux.\nThe -P parameter after\nsetseebol is required to\nmake the change perma-\nnent. That means writing\nthe change so that it per-\nsists even after reboots;\nwithout it, the change will\nbe lost once we restart our\nserver.\nAs we’ve seen, getsebool and setsebool, respectively, allow us\nto query and set the values for the Booleans that tune the policy;\nsemanage boolean can also help here, as we can see in the next\nfigure:\nFigure 10.10 – Using semanage to manage Booleans\nIn Figure 10.10, we can see the Boolean we edited using setsebool.\nOne of the benefits is that semanage, as we introduced, allows\nus to export and import the local changes to the policy, so any\ncustomization made can be exported and imported on another\nsystem to ease the setup of similar server profiles.\nKeeping the System Hardened with SELinux 328\nAll the possible Booleans in the policy can be checked with sudo semanage boolean -l, as we\ndid to list the binding ports for applications in our HTTP example.\nWe have learned about using Booleans to tune how the policy adapts to some specific but pretty\ncommon cases. Next, we will explore what is probably the most used part for administrators—\ntroubleshooting, but focusing on SELinux.\nSELinux troubleshooting and common fixes\nOne of the main problems getting used to SELinux is that many people who are not familiar with\nit blame it for things not working. However, this argument is getting a bit outdated: SELinux was\nintroduced back in 2005.\nMost of the time, issues with SELinux and our system are related to changed file contexts, changing\nports for services, and, less frequently, issues with the policy itself.\nFirst of all, there are several places where we can check for errors, such as the audit log or the\nsystem messages. The first ones, for example, are the /var/log/audit/audit.log file we intro -\nduced earlier in this chapter."],[39350,"Chapter 10 328\nIt is possible to pass the --cap-add and --cap-drop options multiple times:\n$ podman run -d --name cap_test3 \\\n--cap-drop=KILL \\\n--cap-drop=DAC_OVERRIDE \\\n--cap-add=NET_RAW \\\n--cap-add=NET_ADMIN \\\ndocker.io/library/nginx\nWhen we're dealing idx_cd719e41 with capabilities, we must be careful when dropping a default capability.\nThe following code shows an error in the nginx container when dropping the CAP_CHOWN\ncapability:\n$ podman run --name cap_test4 \\\n--cap-drop=CHOWN \\\ndocker.io/library/nginx\n/docker-entrypoint.sh: /docker-entrypoint.d/ is not empty, will attempt to perform\nconfiguration\n/docker-entrypoint.sh: Looking for shell scripts in /docker-entrypoint.d/\n/docker-entrypoint.sh: Launching /docker-entrypoint.d/10-listen-on-ipv6-by-\ndefault.sh\n10-listen-on-ipv6-by-default.sh: info: Getting the checksum of /etc/nginx/conf.d/\ndefault.conf\n10-listen-on-ipv6-by-default.sh: info: Enabled listen on IPv6 in /etc/nginx/\nconf.d/default.conf\n/docker-entrypoint.sh: Launching /docker-entrypoint.d/20-envsubst-on-templates.sh\n/docker-entrypoint.sh: Launching /docker-entrypoint.d/30-tune-worker-processes.sh\n/docker-entrypoint.sh: Configuration complete; ready for start up\n2022/01/06 23:19:39 [emerg] 1#1: chown(\"/var/cache/nginx/client_temp\", 101) failed\n(1: Operation not permitted)\nnginx: [emerg] chown(\"/var/cache/nginx/client_temp\", 101) failed (1: Operation not\npermitted)\nHere, the container fails. From the output, we can see that the nginx process was unable to\nshow the /var/cache/nginx/client_temp directory. This is a direct consequence of the\nCAP_CHOWN capability being removed.\nCapabilities can be applied to a rootless container, but they are namespaced capabilities, which\nare more limited than regular ones and are unable to perform actions that would alter the host\n329 Securing Containers\nsystem. For idx_281c4246 example, if we try to apply the CAP_MKNOD capability to a rootless container, any\nattempt to create a special file inside a rootless container won't be allowed by the kernel:\n$ podman run -it --cap-add=MKNOD \\\ndocker.io/library/busybox /bin/sh\n/ # mkdir -p /test/dev\n/ # mknod -m 666 /test/dev/urandom c 1 8\nmknod: /test/dev/urandom: Operation not permitted\nInstead, if we run the container with elevated root privileges, the capability can be assigned\nsuccessfully:\n# podman run -it --cap-add=MKNOD \\\ndocker.io/library/busybox /bin/sh\n/ # mkdir -p /test/dev\n/ # mknod -m 666 /test/dev/urandom c 1 8\n/ # stat /test/dev/urandom\nFile: /test/dev/urandom\nSize: 0 Blocks: 0 IO Block: 4096 character special file\nDevice: 31h/49d Inode: 530019 Links: 1 Device type: 1,8\nAccess: (0666/crw-rw-rw-) Uid: ( 0/ root) Gid: ( 0/ root)\nAccess: 2022-01-06 23:50:06.056650747 +0000\nModify: 2022-01-06 23:50:06.056650747 +0000\nChange: 2022-01-06 23:50:06.056650747 +0000"],[25699,"Chapter 10 328\ntraefik.priority=10: This gives this route high priority. We will see why in a second.\ntraefik.http.routers.catalog.rule=\"Host(\\\"acme.com\\\") && PathPrefix(\\\"/catalog\\\")\":\nThe route must include the hostname, acme.com, and the path must start with /catalog in order\nto be rerouted to this service. As an example, acme.com/catalog?type=bicycles would qualify\nfor this rule.\nPlease note the special form of the fourth label. Its general form is\ntraefik.http.routers.<service name>.rule.\nNow, let's look at how we can run the city-tours container:\n$ docker container run --rm -d \\\n--name city-tours \\\n--label traefik.enable=true \\\n--label traefik.port=5000 \\\n--label traefik.priority=1 \\\n--label traefik.http.routers.city-tours.rule=\"Host(\\\"acme.com\\\")\" \\\nacme/city-tours:1.0\nAgain, in the preceding command, the line with --label traefik.http.routers.city-tours.rules must\nbe on one physical line, including its value. Again, if you have problems with the command, you'll find the\ncorrect version in /chapter-10/solutions/scripts.sh.\nHere, we forward any matching calls to port 5000, which corresponds to the port where the city-tours\napplication is listening. Pay attention to the priority, which is set to 1 (low). This, in combination with the\nhigh priority of the catalog service, allows us to filter out all URLs starting with /catalog and redirect\nthem to the catalog service, while all other i d x _ e b 7 3 0 a c 1 URLs will go to the city-tours service.\nNow, we can finally run Traefik as the edge router that will serve as a reverse proxy in front of our\napplication. This is how we start it:\n$ docker container run -d \\\n--name traefik \\\n-p 8080:8080 \\\n-p 80:80 \\\n-v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock \\\ntraefik:v2.0 \\\n--api.insecure=true \\\n--providers.docker=true \\\n--entrypoints.web.address=:80 \\\n--log.level=DEBUG \\\n--providers.docker.exposedbydefault=false\nNote how we mount the Docker socket into the container using the -v (or --volume) parameter so that\nTraefik can interact with the Docker engine. We will be able to send web traffic to port 80 of Traefik, from\n◦\n◦\n◦"],[26934,"Chapter 10 329\n• Think about what was done before the error happened, if this was an existing program:\n• Files moved instead of copied or created on the destination\n• Changed ports or folders for the software\nHaving arrived at this point, we should check audit.log for relevant messages. For the example\nwe mentioned about wrong context with the files in /srv/www/htdocs/, an example audit entry\ncould be the following:\ntype=AVC msg=audit(1753017038.500:148): avc: denied { read } for\npid=1149 comm=\"httpd-prefork\" name=\"index2.html\" dev=\"vda2\" ino=263\nscontext=system_u:system_r:httpd_t:s0 tcontext=unconfined_u:object_r:user_\nhome_t:s0 tclass=file permissive=0\nIt looks strange, but if we check the parameters, we see the path of the affected file, the PID, the\nsource context (scontext), and the target context (tcontext). So, in brief, we can see that httpd_t\ntried to access (get attributes) for a target context (user_home_t) and that was denied.\nWe can use the ausearch and audit2allow tools to generate a custom policy and enable this\nbehavior using two commands:\nuser@geeko:~> sudo ausearch -c 'httpd' --raw | audit2allow -M my-httpd\n******************** IMPORTANT *********************** To make this policy\npackage active, execute:\nsemodule -i my-httpd.pp\nuser@geeko:~> sudo semodule -X 300 -i my-httpd.pp\nHowever, this kind of recommendation should be taken with knowledge of what is being done.\nThat means that the preceding commands will fix httpd_t getting access to the user_admin_t\nfile. We can learn about what would be done by only running the first command, together with\nthe audit2allow pipe.\nRunning ausearch -c 'httpd' --raw | audit2allow -M my-httpd creates several files named\nmy-httpd in the current folder, one named my-httpd.te and another named my-httpd.pp. The\nsecond command, semodule, which we will not use in production servers, installs the modified\npolicy. But please, don’t ever do that until you understand what’s going on, as we will see in the\nfollowing lines.\nKeeping the System Hardened with SELinux 330\nThe interesting file for us is now the my-httpd.te file (te: type\nenforcement):\nmodule my-httpd 1.0;\nrequire {\ntype httpd_t;\ntype unconfined_service_t;\ntype user_home_t;\nclass process signull;\nclass file { open read };\n}\n#============= httpd_t ============== allow\nhttpd_t unconfined_service_t:process signull;\n#!!!! This avc can be allowed using the boolean\n'httpd_read_user_content' allow httpd_t user_\nhome_t:file { open read };\nFrom there, we can see that it uses a requirements session for the\ntypes involved, and later, the rule itself, which allows httpd_t ac-\ncess to user_home_t files for using the getattr function—nothing\nmore, nothing less.\nWill this fix our file access issue? It will effectively make httpd_t\nget access to the index2.html file, so there will no longer be errors,"],[39352,"Chapter 10 330\nUnderstanding SELinux interaction with containers\nIn this section, we will discuss SELinux idx_60b2409d policies and introduce Udica, a tool that's used to\ngenerate SELinux profiles for containers.\nSELinux works directly in kernel space and manages object isolation while following a least-\nprivilege model that contains a series of policies that idx_0b3cc4fb can handle enforcing or exceptions. To\ndefine these objects, SELinux uses labels that idx_0e2a5287 define types. By default, SELinux works in\nEnforcing mode, denying idx_cc33ad43 access to resources with a series of exceptions defined by policies. To\ndisable Enforcing mode, SELinux can be put in Permissive mode, where idx_95c0d167 violations are only\naudited, without them being blocked.\nBy default, SELinux uses idx_19433bd5 a targeted policy type, which tries to target and confine idx_9d4d6977 specific\nobject types (processes, files, devices, and so on) using idx_1905e269 a set of predefined policies.\nSELinux allows different kinds of access control. They can be summarized as follows:\nType Enforcement (TE): This controls access to resources according to process and file\ntypes. This is the idx_df1f2ef5 main use case of SELinux access control.\nRole-Based Access Control (RBAC): This controls access to resources using SELinux\nidx_60e9fc60 users (which can be mapped to real system users) and their associated SELinux roles.\nMulti-Level Security (MLS): This grants all processes with the same sensitivity level\nread/write access to idx_b935c1be the resources.\nMulti-Category Security (MCS): This idx_8803eced controls access using categories, which are\nplain text labels that are applied to resources. Categories idx_27f2ab24 are used to create\ncompartments of objects, along with the other SELinux labels. Only processes that\nbelong to the same category can access a given resource. In Chapter 5, Implementing\nStorage for the Container's Data, we discussed MCS and how we can map categories to\nresources that have been accessed by containers.\nWith type idx_f0c6c3d1 enforcement, the system files receive labels idx_79f3d891 called types, while processes receive\nlabels idx_5a503819 called domains. A process that belongs to a domain can be allowed to access a file that\nbelongs to a given type, and this access can be audited by SELinux.\nSecurity alert\nAs we mentioned previously, switching SELinux to Permissive mode or completely\ndisabling it is not good practice, as it opens you up to potential security threats. Instead\nof doing that, users should create custom policies to manage the necessary exceptions.\nNote\n•\n•\n•\n•\n331 Securing Containers\nFor example, according idx_9ad34420 to SELinux, the Apache httpd process, which is labeled with the\nhttpd_t domain, can access files or directories with httpd_sys_content_t labels."],[25702,"Chapter 10 330\nWe then saw how SDNs provide built-in firewalling between containers, and how to strengthen isolation by\nplacing services on separate bridge networks. We created and inspected custom bridge networks, enabled dual-\nstack IPv4/IPv6 support, and learned how to tune options such as subnets and MTU values.\nNext, we examined the host and null networks, where containers either share the host's stack or are completely\ndisconnected. We also saw how to run a container inside the network namespace of another container, a technique\nthat underpins Kubernetes Pods and is invaluable for sidecar debugging.\nWith that foundation, we moved on to port management. We practiced publishing container ports to the host\nusing different strategies, binding to specific interfaces, working with TCP and UDP, and understanding the\ndifferences across Linux and Docker Desktop.\nFinally, we climbed up one level of abstraction and introduced HTTP-level routing with Traefik. Here, we\ndemonstrated how a reverse proxy gives us a single, stable entry point, while enabling sophisticated routing, TLS\ntermination, and service-by-service extraction. This showed how what seems impossible—gradually decomposing\na monolith without breaking clients—becomes not only possible but straightforward.\nArmed with these techniques, you now have a deep understanding of how to connect, isolate, and expose\ncontainers on a single host, and how to introduce a reverse proxy to prepare your applications for modularization\nand future scaling.\nIn the next chapter, we're going to introduce Docker Compose. We will learn about creating an application that\nconsists of multiple services, each running in a container, and how Docker Compose allows us to easily build, run,\nand scale such an application using a declarative approach.\nFurther reading\nHere are some articles that describe the topics that were presented in this chapter in more detail:\nDocker networking overview: http://dockr.ly/2sXGzQn\nContainer networking: http://dockr.ly/2HJfQKn\nWhat is a bridge?: https://bit.ly/2HyC3Od\nUsing bridge networks: http://dockr.ly/2BNxjRr\nUsing Macvlan networks: http://dockr.ly/2ETjy2x\nNetworking using the host network: http://dockr.ly/2F4aI59\nQuestions\nTo assess the skills that you have gained from this chapter, please try to answer the following questions:\nName the three core elements of the Container Network Model (CNM).\nHow do you create a custom bridge network called, for example, frontend?\nHow do you run two nginx:alpine containers attached to the frontend network?\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•"],[26936,"Chapter 10 331\nGoing back to reviewing system logs, it might happen that we realize that something fails long\nafter it really started failing. So, using ausearch or passing the full logs to audit2allow should\nbe used carefully.\nIn the case of new software being deployed that has no SELinux support, we can do the checks\nthe other way around, in a test system:\n• Set SELinux to permissive mode\n• Deploy the software\n• Analyze all the alerts received to see whether there are expected things\n• Contact vendors for the software and open a support case with SUSE to work on a policy\nIf we’re getting locked out of our system because SELinux is in enforcing mode and we have\nreally messed up the labels very badly, such as by running a bad chcon command recursively\nagainst our root folder (for example, scripting a context change depending on a variable and\nthat variable being empty), we still have ways to get out of trouble:\n• Use setenforce 0 to put SELinux in permissive mode\n• Run touch /etc/selinux/.autorelabel\n• Reboot the host so that, at the next boot, SELinux restores the appropriate labels\nIf we are in a really bad situation, and we cannot use setenforce 0 or the system cannot even\nboot or perform the relabeling correctly, there’s still hope, but with some extra steps.\nWhen a system is rebooting, we can see the list of installed kernels at the grub prompt and use\nit to edit the kernel boot parameters.\nUsing the selinux=0 parameter, we completely disable SELinux, and it’s something we don’t want,\nbut we can use enforcing=0 to accomplish having SELinux enabled but in permissive mode.\nOnce we have our system booting into permissive mode, we can repeat the previous procedure\nto get back to the previous behavior and continue debugging the situation within the system\nitself with the prior indications given (checking system logs, etc.).\nKeeping the System Hardened with SELinux 332\nSummary\nThis chapter introduced SELinux, including how it works, how to check the processes, files, and\nports, and how to tune them either by adding new options or using Booleans, and some initial\ntroubleshooting skills that we should explore further to gain extra knowledge and experience.\nIn the next chapter, we will learn about a new feature included in SLES 16, Agentic AI with mcphost,\nthat allows users to query AI to understand and manage their server state.\nGet This Book’s PDF Version and\nExclusive Extras\nScan the QR code (or go to packtpub.com/unlock). Search for this\nbook by name, confirm the edition, and then follow the steps on\nthe page.\nNote: Keep your invoice handy. Purchases made directly from Packt\ndon’t require an invoice.\nPart 3\nImplementing Agentic AI\nwith mcphost\nModel Context Protocol (MCP) is a standard to connect different services and/or systems to\nlarge language models (LLMs) that power artificial intelligence (AI). SLES 16 comes with an"],[39355,"Chapter 10 332\nBy looking at the repository's content, we will find the three most important policy source files\nfor developing any module:\ncontainer.fc: This file defines the files and directories that are bound to the types\ndefined in the module.\ncontainer.te: This file defines the policy rules, attributes, and aliases.\ncontainer.if: This file defines the module interface. It contains a set of public macro\nfunctions that are exposed by the module.\nA process that's running inside a container is labeled with the container_t domain. It has\nread/write access to resources labeled with the container_file_t type context and read/\nexecute access to resources labeled with the container_share_t type context.\nWhen a container is executed, the podman process, as well as the container runtime and the\nconmon process, run with the container_runtime_t domain type and are allowed to execute\nprocesses that idx_2b4cf7f4 transition only to specific types. Those types are grouped idx_7fe2566d in the\ncontainer_domain attribute and can be inspected with the seinfo utility (installed with the\nsetools-console package on Fedora), as shown in the following code:\n$ seinfo -a container_domain -x\nType Attributes: 1\nattribute container_domain;\ncontainer_device_plugin_init_t\ncontainer_device_plugin_t\ncontainer_device_t\ncontainer_engine_t\ncontainer_init_t\ncontainer_kvm_t\ncontainer_logreader_t\ncontainer_logwriter_t\ncontainer_t\ncontainer_userns_t\nThe container_domain attribute is declared in the container.te source file in the container-\npolicy repository using the attribute keyword:\nattribute container_domain;\nattribute container_user_domain;\nattribute container_net_domain;\n•\n•\n•\n333 Securing Containers\nThe preceding attributes are mapped to the container_t type using a typeattribute\ndeclaration:\ntypeattribute container_t container_domain, container_net_domain,\ncontainer_user_domain;\nUsing this approach, SELinux guarantees process isolation across containers and between a\ncontainer and its host. In this way, a process escaping the container (maybe exploiting a\nvulnerability) cannot access resources on the host or inside other containers.\nWhen a container is idx_f2660d37 created, the image's read-only layers, which form idx_0f7abb49 the OverlayFS set of\nLowerDirs, are labeled with the container_ro_file_t type, which prevents the container\nfrom writing inside those directories. At the same time, MergedDir, which is the sum of\nLowerDirs and UpperDir, is writable and labeled as container_file_t.\nTo prove this, let's run a rootful container idx_894bbad6 with the c1 and c2 MCS categories:\n# podman run -d --name selinux_test1 --security-opt label=level:s0:c1,c2 nginx\nNow, we can find all the files labeled as container_file_t:s0:c1,c2 under the host\nfilesystem:\n#\n# find /var/lib/containers/storage/overlay -type f -context"],[25706,"Chapter 10 332\nHere is the solution:\nTo get the IPs of all the containers attached to the frontend Docker network, you can use the\ndocker network inspect command, followed by the network name. Here's an example\ncommand:\n$ docker network inspect frontend --format='{{range .Containers}}\n{{.IPv4Address}} {{end}}'\nThis will output the IPv4 addresses of all the containers attached to the frontend network,\nseparated by spaces.\nTo get the subnet associated with the frontend network, you can again use the docker network\ninspect command followed by the network name. Here's an example command:\n$ docker network inspect frontend --format='{{json .IPAM.Config}}' | jq -r\n'.[].Subnet'\nThis will output the subnet associated with the frontend network in CIDR notation (e.g.,\n172.25.0.0/16). The jq command is used here to parse the output of the docker network\ninspect command and extract the subnet.\nThe Docker host network is a networking mode that allows a Docker container to use the host's\nnetworking stack instead of creating a separate network namespace. In other words, containers running\nin host network mode can directly access the network interfaces and ports of the Docker host machine.\nThe purpose of using the host network mode is to improve network performance since it avoids the\noverhead of containerization and network virtualization. This mode is often used for applications that\nrequire low-latency network communication or need to listen on a large number of ports.\nHowever, using the host network mode can also present security risks since it exposes the container's\nservices directly on the Docker host's network interfaces, potentially making them accessible to other\ncontainers or hosts on the same network.\nThe Docker host network mode is appropriate for scenarios where network performance is critical and\nwhere network isolation is not a requirement. The following are examples:\nIn cases where the containerized application needs to communicate with other services running\non the host machine, such as a database or cache service, the use of the host network mode can\nimprove performance by eliminating the need for Network Address Translation (NAT) and\nrouting between containers.\nIn scenarios where the containerized application needs to listen on a large number of ports, the\nhost network mode can simplify network configuration and management by allowing the\ncontainer to use the same network interfaces and IP addresses as the host machine, without the\nneed to manage port mapping between the container and host network namespaces.\n4.\na.\nb.\nc.\nd."],[25710,"Chapter 10 334\nJoin us on Discord\nFor discussions around the book and to connect with your peers, join us on Discord at packt.link/discordcloud\nor scan the QR code below:\n335 Using Single-Host Networking\n11\nManaging Containers with Docker\nCompose\nIn the previous chapter, we learned a lot about how container networking works on a single Docker host. We\nintroduced i d x _ e a 9 e 6 b 4 1 the Container Network Model (CNM), which forms the basis of all networking between Docker\ncontainers, and then we dove deep into different implementations of the CNM, specifically the bridge network.\nFinally, we introduced Traefik, a reverse proxy to enable sophisticated HTTP application-level routing between\ncontainers.\nThis chapter introduces the concept of an application consisting of multiple services, each running in a container,\nand how Docker Compose allows us to easily build, run, and scale such an application using a declarative\napproach.\nThis chapter covers the following topics:\nDemystifying declarative versus imperative orchestration of containers\nRunning a multi-service application\nBuilding images with Docker Compose\nRunning an application with Docker Compose\nScaling a service\nBuilding and pushing an application\nUsing Docker Compose overrides\nModularizing applications with include\nWhen to use Docker Compose versus a full orchestration system\nAfter completing this chapter, you will be able to do the following:\nClearly explain the difference between an imperative and a declarative approach when defining and\nrunning applications\nDistinguish between a single container and a Docker Compose service and understand how Compose\nbuilds applications from multiple services\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\n•\nWrite your own Docker Compose file in YAML to define and run a multi-service application\nBuild, push, run, scale, and tear down an application using Compose commands\nAdapt configurations for different environments using overrides and modularize large stacks with the\nnew include feature\nConfidently decide when Docker Compose is the right tool, and when a full orchestration system is\nrequired\nTechnical requirements\nThe code accompanying this chapter can be found at https://github.com/PacktPublishing/The-Ultimate-\nDocker-Container-Book-Fourth-Edition/tree/main/chapter-11/solutions.\nBefore we start, let's make sure we have a folder ready for the code you are going to implement in this chapter:\nNavigate to the folder in which you cloned the previously listed code repository accompanying this book.\nNormally, this is the The-Ultimate-Docker-Container-Book-Fourth-Edition folder in your home folder:\n$ cd ~/The-Ultimate-Docker-Container-Book-Fourth-Edition\nCreate a subfolder called ch11 and navigate to it:\n$ mkdir chapter-11 && cd chapter-11\nIn the past, you needed to have a separate docker-compose tool installed on your system. This is not the case"],[39357,"Chapter 10 334\noverlay/4b147975bb5c336b10e71d21c49fe88ddb00d0569b77ddab1d7737f80056677b/merged/\nlib/terminfo/r/rxvt-unicode\n[…output omitted...]\nAs expected, the container_file_t label, which is associated with the c1 and c2 categories, is\napplied to all idx_724921fb the files under the MergedDir container.\nAt the same time, we idx_e27f8a07 can demonstrate that the container's LowerDirs are labeled as\ncontainer_ro_file_t. First, we need to extract the container's LowerDirs list:\n# podman inspect selinux_test1 \\\n--format '{{.GraphDriver.Data.LowerDir}}'\n/var/lib/containers/storage/overlay/\n9566cbcf1773eac59951c14c52156a6164db1b0d8026d015e193774029db18a5/diff:/var/lib/\ncontainers/storage/overlay/\n24de59cced7931bbcc0c4a34d4369c15119a0b8b180f98a0434fa76a6dfcd490/diff:/var/lib/\ncontainers/storage/overlay/\n1bb84245b98b7e861c91ed4319972ed3287bdd2ef02a8657c696a76621854f3b/diff:/var/lib/\ncontainers/storage/overlay/\n97f26271fef21bda129ac431b5f0faa03ae0b2b50bda6af969315308fc16735b/diff:/var/lib/\ncontainers/storage/overlay/\n768ef71c8c91e4df0aa1caf96764ceec999d7eb0aa584e241246815c1fa85435/diff:/var/lib/\ncontainers/storage/overlay/\n2edcec3590a4ec7f40cf0743c15d78fb39d8326bc029073b41ef9727da6c851f/diff\nThe rightmost directory represents the container's lowest layer and is usually the base\nfilesystem tree of the image. Let's inspect the type context of this directory:\n# ls -alZ /var/lib/containers/storage/overlay/\n2edcec3590a4ec7f40cf0743c15d78fb39d8326bc029073b41ef9727da6c851f/diff\ntotal 84\ndr-xr-xr-x. 21 root root unconfined_u:object_r:container_ro_file_t:s0 4096 Jan 5\n23:16 .\ndrwx------. 6 root root unconfined_u:object_r:container_ro_file_t:s0 4096 Jan 5\n23:16 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x. 2 root root unconfined_u:object_r:container_ro_file_t:s0 4096 Dec 20\n00:00 bin\ndrwxr-xr-x. 2 root root unconfined_u:object_r:container_ro_file_t:s0 4096 Dec 11\n17:25 boot\ndrwxr-xr-x. 2 root root unconfined_u:object_r:container_ro_file_t:s0 4096 Dec 20\n00:00 dev\n335 Securing Containers\ndrwxr-xr-x. 30 root root unconfined_u:object_r:container_ro_file_t:s0 4096 Dec 20\n00:00 etc\ndrwxr-xr-x. 2 root root unconfined_u:object_r:container_ro_file_t:s0 4096 Dec 11\n17:25 home\ndrwxr-xr-x. 8 root root unconfined_u:object_r:container_ro_file_t:s0 4096 Dec 20\n00:00 lib\n[...omitted output...]\nThe preceding output also idx_afb7d5a1 shows another interesting aspect: since the LowerDir layers idx_e41318cd are\nshared across multiple containers that use the same image, we won't find any MCS categories\nthat have been applied here.\nContainers do not have read/write access to files or directories that are not labeled as\ncontainer_file_t. Previously, we saw that it is possible to relabel those files by applying\nthe :z suffix to mounted volumes or by manually relabeling them in advance before running\nthe containers.\nHowever, relabeling idx_426d60ac crucial directories such as /home or /var/logs is a idx_fe9ae000 very bad idea since"],[39359,"Chapter 10 336\nOn other distributions, Udica can be installed from its source by running the following\ncommands:\n$ sudo dnf install -y setools-console git container-selinux\n$ git clone\n$ cd udica && sudo python3 ./setup.py install\nTo demonstrate how Udica works, we are going to create a container that writes to the /var/\nlog directory of the host, which is bind-mounted when the container is created. By default, the\nprocess with the container_t domain would not be able to write a directory labeled with the\nvar_log_t type.\nThe following script, which has been executed inside the container, is an endless loop that\nwrites a log line composed of the current date and a counter:\nChapter10/custom_logger/logger.sh\n#!/bin/bash\nset -euo pipefail\ntrap \"echo Exited; exit;\" SIGINT SIGTERM\n# Run an endless loop writing a simple log entry with date\ncount=1\nwhile true; do\necho \"$(date +%y/%m/%d_%H:%M:%S) - Line #$count\" | tee -a /var/log/custom.log\ncount=$((count+1))\nsleep 2\ndone\nThe preceding script uses the set -euo pipefail option to exit immediately in case an error\noccurs, and the tee utility to write the command's output both to standard output and the /\nvar/log/custom.log file in append mode. The count variable increments on each loop cycle.\nThe Dockerfile for this idx_d0932e8e container is kept minimal – it just copies the logger script and executes\nit at container startup:\nChapter10/custom_logger/Dockerfile\nFROM docker.io/library/fedora\n# Copy the logger.sh script\nCOPY --chmod=755 logger.sh /\n337 Securing Containers\n# Exec the logger.sh script\nCMD [\"/logger.sh\"]\nThe container image is built with the name custom_logger:\n# cd /Chapter10/custom_logger\n# buildah build -t custom_logger .\nNow, it's time to test the container and see how it behaves. The /var/log directory is bind-\nmounted with rw permissions to the container path /var/log, without this altering its type\ncontext. We should keep the execution in the foreground to see the immediate output:\n# podman run -v /var/log:/var/log:rw \\\n--name custom_logger1 custom_logger\ntee: /var/log/custom.log: Permission denied\n22/01/08_09:09:33 - Custom log event #1\nAs expected, the script failed to write to the target file. We could fix this by changing the\ndirectory type context to container_file_t, but, as we learned previously, this is a poor idea\nsince it would prevent other processes from writing their logs.\nInstead, we can use idx_c0d3cd2d Udica to generate a custom SELinux security profile for the container. In\nthe following code, the container specs are exported to a container.json file and then parsed\nby Udica to generate a custom profile called custom_logger:\n# podman inspect custom_logger1 > container.json\n# udica -j container.json custom_logger\nPolicy custom_logger created!\nPlease load these modules using:\n# semodule -i custom_logger.cil /usr/share/udica/templates/"],[39361,"Chapter 10 338\nRestart the container with: \"--security-opt label=type:custom_logger.process\"\nparameter\nOnce the profile has been generated, Udica outputs the instructions to configure the container.\nFirst, we need to load the new custom policy using the semodule utility. The generated idx_9abeb5ea file is in\nCommon Intermediate Language (CIL) format, an intermediate policy language for SELinux.\nAlong with the generated CIL file, the example loads some Udica templates, /usr/share/\nudica/templates/base_container.cil and /usr/share/udica/templates/\nlog_container.cil, whose rules are inherited in the custom container policy file.\nLet's load the modules using the suggested command:\n# semodule -i custom_logger.cil /usr/share/udica/templates/\n{base_container.cil,log_container.cil}\nAfter loading the modules idx_5ef194a7 in SELinux, we are ready to run the container with the custom\ncustom_logger.process label, which is passed as an argument to Podman's --security-opt\noption.\nAll the other container options were kept identical, except for its name, which has been\nupdated to custom_logger2 to differentiate it from the previous instance:\n# podman run -v /var/log:/var/log:rw \\\n--name custom_logger2 \\\n--security-opt label=type:custom_logger.process \\\ncustom_logger\n22/01/08_09:05:19 - Line #1\n22/01/08_09:05:21 - Line #2\n22/01/08_09:05:23 - Line #3\n22/01/08_09:05:25 - Line #5\n[...Omitted output...]\nThis time, the script successfully wrote to the /var/log/custom.log file thanks to the custom\nprofile that was generated with Udica.\nNote that the container processes are not running with the container_t domain, but with the\nnew custom_logger.process superset, which includes additional rules on top of the default.\nWe can confirm this by running the following command on the host:\n# ps auxZ | grep 'custom_logger.process'\nunconfined_u:system_r:container_runtime_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023 root 26546 0.1 0.6\n339 Securing Containers\n1365088 53768 pts/0 Sl+ 09:16 0:00 podman run -v /var/log:/var/log:rw --\nsecurity-opt label=type:custom_logger.process custom_logger\nsystem_u:system_r:custom_logger.process:s0:c159,c258 root 26633 0.0 0.0 4180\n3136 ? Ss 09:16 0:00 /bin/bash /logger.sh\nsystem_u:system_r:custom_logger.process:s0:c159,c258 root 26881 0.0 0.0 2640\n1104 ? S 09:18 0:00 sleep 2\nUdica creates idx_6c429514 the custom policy by parsing the JSON spec file and looking for the container\nmount points, ports, and capabilities. Let's look at the content of the generated\ncustom_logger.cil file from our example:\n(block custom_logger\n(blockinherit container)\n(allow process process ( capability ( chown dac_override fowner fsetid kill\nnet_bind_service setfcap setgid setpcap setuid sys_chroot )))\n(blockinherit log_rw_container)\nThe CIL language syntax is beyond the scope of this book, but we can still notice some"],[39363,"Chapter 10 340\nThe available templates are implemented for common scenarios, such as accessing log\ndirectories or user homes, or even opening network ports. Among them, the\nbase_container.cil template is always included by all the Udica-generated policies as the\nbase building block that's used to generate the custom policies.\nAccording to the behavior of the container that's derived from the spec file, other templates are\nincluded. For example, the policy inherits the log_rw_container block from the\nlog_container.cil template to let the custom logger container access the /var/log directory.\nUdica is a idx_ce3668a4 great tool for addressing container isolation issues and helps administrators address\nSELinux confinement use cases by overcoming the complexity of writing rules manually.\nGenerated security profiles can also be versioned inside a GitHub repository and reused for\nsimilar containers on different hosts.\nSummary\nIn this chapter, we learned how to develop and apply techniques to improve the overall\nsecurity of our container-based service architecture. We learned how leveraging rootless\ncontainers and avoiding UID 0 can reduce the attack surface of our services. Then, we learned\nhow to sign and trust container images to avoid MITM attacks. Finally, we went under the\nhood of containers' tools and looked at the Linux kernel's capabilities and the SELinux\nsubsystem, which can help us fine-tune various security aspects for our running containers.\nNow that we've done a deep dive into security, we are ready to move on to the next chapter,\nwhere we will take an advanced look at networking for containers.\nFurther reading\nFor more information about the topics that were covered in this chapter, take a look at the\nfollowing resources:\nMITRE ATT&CK Container Matrix: https://attack.mitre.org/matrices/\nenterprise/containers/\nSigstore project home page: https://docs.sigstore.dev/\nPodman image signing tutorial: https://github.com/containers/podman/blob/\nmain/docs/tutorials/image_signing.md\nLukas Vrabec's blog: https://lukas-vrabec.com/\n•\n•\n•\n•\n341 Securing Containers\nCIL introduction and design principles: https://github.com/SELinuxProject/cl/\nwiki\nUdica introduction on Red Hat's blog: https://www.redhat.com/en/blog/generate-\nselinux-policies-containers-with-udica\nJoin us on Discord\nFor discussions around the book and to connect with your peers, join us on Discord at\npackt.link/discordcloud or scan the QR code below:\n•\n•"],[52788,"Chapter 10 342\nidentities can help prevent identity fraud by enabling real-time verification of credentials and\nreducing the reliance on easily stolen or forged information.\nDecentralized identities also promote inclusivity and accessibility. Individuals who lack\ntraditional identity documents or live in regions with limited infrastructure can create digital\nidentities that are recognized globally. This enables access to services such as banking,\nhealthcare, and education, regardless of geographic location or socioeconomic status. The\nportability of decentralized identities means users can carry their credentials across borders\nand platforms, fostering seamless digital interactions in an increasingly connected world.\nDecentralized identities idx_2ca675b2 offer a transformative approach to digital identity management. By\nshifting control from centralized authorities to individuals, DIDs enhance privacy, security,\nand user autonomy. They reduce the risks associated with data breaches and identity theft,\nstreamline the verification process, and open up opportunities for people who are underserved\nby traditional systems. As technology and standards continue to evolve, decentralized\nidentities are poised to become a cornerstone of secure, user-centric digital ecosystems.\nA digital identity represents who a person is in a digital space. Verifiable credentials help prove\nspecific claims about that person when needed.\nIntroduction to verifiable credentials\nVerifiable credentials are a foundational concept in the emerging landscape of decentralized\nidentity systems. They idx_33bf09b4 provide a secure, privacy-preserving, and interoperable way to prove\nattributes, qualifications, or claims about an individual, organization, or device in the digital\nworld. Unlike traditional credentials, such as plastic ID cards or paper certificates, verifiable\ncredentials are digitally signed and can be presented, validated, and revoked with\ncryptographic assurance. This approach not only streamlines the process of sharing and\nverifying information but also gives individuals greater control over their personal data,\nreducing the risk of identity theft, fraud, and data misuse.\nAt their core, verifiable credentials are digital statements made by a trusted issuer about a\nperson. These statements, or claims, can represent a wide range of information—from\ngovernment-issued identification and academic qualifications to professional licenses and\nmemberships. Each credential is composed of three main components: the issuer (who creates\nand signs the credential), the subject (the person to whom the credential pertains), and the\nverifier (who checks the authenticity of the credential). The credentials themselves are typically\nidx_74fcfc41 encoded in a standard data model, such as the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C)"],[39364,"Chapter 10 342\nPart 3\nManaging and Integrating\nContainers Securely\nIn this part of the book, you will learn how to troubleshoot, monitor, and create advanced\nconfigurations for the running containers on the operating system host.\nThis part of the book includes the following chapters:"],[52791,"Chapter 10 344\nGroup are driving the development and adoption idx_46cee8fe of these standards, ensuring that verifiable\ncredentials can be used globally across a variety of platforms and industries.\nDespite their promise, verifiable credentials face several challenges. Achieving widespread\nadoption requires collaboration among issuers, wallet providers, verifiers, and regulators to\nestablish trusted ecosystems and governance frameworks. Ensuring usability and accessibility\nfor all users, including those with limited technical skills or resources, is essential for inclusion.\nThere are also ongoing discussions about privacy, data protection, and interoperability, as well\nas the need for robust mechanisms to handle credential revocation and recovery in case of\ndevice loss or compromise.\nVerifiable credentials idx_e9d2e28e represent a transformative step forward in digital identity management.\nThey empower individuals to control their personal information, reduce reliance on\ncentralized authorities, and create new possibilities for secure, privacy-preserving interactions\nonline. As standards mature and adoption grows, verifiable credentials are poised to become a\ncornerstone of decentralized, user-centric digital ecosystems, supporting a wide range of\nservices and enabling trusted digital interactions around the world.\nNow that we understand the core concepts, let's look at how digital identity and verifiable\ncredentials are implemented in Microsoft Entra.\nUnderstanding Microsoft Entra Verified ID\nMicrosoft Entra Verified ID is a cutting-edge solution designed to enable secure, privacy-\nrespecting digital idx_c0fe6041 identity verification for individuals and organizations. Built on open\nstandards such as W3C Verifiable Credentials and Decentralized Identifiers (DIDs), Entra\nVerified ID empowers users to obtain, manage, and present digital credentials in a trustworthy\nand interoperable manner. With Entra Verified ID, organizations can issue digital credentials—\nsuch as proof of employment, educational qualifications, or professional certifications—that\nusers store securely in their own digital wallets. When users need to verify their identity or\nclaims, they can selectively disclose only the necessary information to verifiers, maintaining\ncontrol over their personal data and enhancing privacy.\nMicrosoft Entra Verified ID integrates seamlessly with existing identity systems and platforms,\nmaking it easier for businesses to adopt decentralized identity solutions without overhauling\ntheir infrastructure. The platform supports robust security measures, including cryptographic\nsignatures, and real-time revocation, to ensure that credentials are authentic and up to date.\nBy removing the need for centralized authorities and enabling user-centric identity\nmanagement, Entra Verified ID reduces the risk of fraud, streamlines onboarding, and"],[52794,"Chapter 10 346\nLet's understand idx_4030c6c7 this with the help of an example. Bob is an employee of Litware Inc. Due to\nhis employment with Litware Inc., he gets discounts from his gym. To get the discount he must\nprove that he is an employee of Litware Inc. Litware Inc. issues verifiable credentials to all its\nemployees that Bob stores in his Microsoft Authenticator App. He can now present this\ncredential on the gym website to prove his employment and avail the discount. This\ntransaction is logged to help Bob track who he has presented his credential to.\nWhen organizations setup Microsoft Entra Verified ID, their DIDs are registered with\nMicrosoft Entra Verified ID Network. This is a discovery and trust framework that makes it\neasy for verifiers to find issuers' DIDs and credential types. This enables streamlined credential\nverification across organizations.\nMicrosoft also partners with third-party providers of identity services such as AU10TIX,\nIDEMIA and LexisNexis. These are government ID verification partners that are available in the\nMicrosoft Security Store. Organizations idx_51fc9de6 can choose to require employees and business guests\nto provide a government identification proof and perform a face check against that as a part of\nthe verification process. This is particularly useful in cases of remote onboarding and total loss\nrecovery – when employees have lost all access to their account and need to recover.\nLicensing\nOrganizations idx_585c423f can issue, verify or revoke up to 50,000 transactions per month with the free\nversion of Microsoft Entra. After this, Microsoft Entra P1 or P2 is required to continue using\nVerified ID.\nNow that we understand the basic concepts, let's dive deeper into Microsoft Entra Verified ID.\nGet started with Microsoft Entra Verified ID\nNow that idx_aa2dc681 you have understood the basic concepts of decentralized identities and verifiable\ncredentials, let's get started with setting up Microsoft Entra Verified ID. Microsoft Entra\nVerified ID can be set up using a quick setup or a custom setup.\nUsing quick setup\nIf you have a custom domain name configured in your Microsoft Entra tenant, you can use\nquick idx_93c07698 setup to automate all configuration steps and start issuing verified credentials within\nThe did:web method lets organizations publish their DID documents directly on their\nweb domain using HTTPS and DNS. This approach leverages existing web\ninfrastructure for trust and discovery rather than blockchain anchoring.\nNote\n347 Microsoft Entra Verified ID\nminutes. Setting up a custom domain is explained in Chapter 3, Implementing Microsoft Entra\nID. Quick setup creates your signing keys, registers DIDs, verifies domain ownership and\ncreates a Verified Employee credential, all with the click of a button. The shared signing key is"],[52266,"Chapter 10 347\nFigure 10.1: Pain points of requirements analysis\nThese pain points usually arise because traditional requirements analysis depends heavily on\nmanual coordination, subjective interpretation, and limited sources of information:\n• Traditional requirements analysis is time-consuming and inefficient. Organizing and\nconducting physical meetings often requires coordinating the schedules of numerous\nparticipants, which is a complex and time-consuming task in itself. Additionally, the\ninformation collected from meetings may need further organization and analysis before\nuseful conclusions can be drawn. Moreover, user interviews and questionnaire surveys\ntypically require significant manpower to carry out—from designing questionnaires to\ndistributing, collecting, and analyzing the data—each step consumes a great deal of time,\nand various unforeseen challenges may arise throughout the process.\n• Limited by participants’ experience and cognitive biases. Requirements analysis often\nrelies on the subjective judgment of professionals, inevitably introducing their cognitive\nbiases. For example, project managers and analysts may interpret user needs based on\ntheir own experience and preferences, neglecting or misunderstanding the user’s real\nintentions and needs. In addition, due to differences in cultural background, language\ncommunication, or personal preferences, users may not express their needs precisely,\nwhich can easily lead to misinterpretations or omissions of information.\nApplying LLMs in Project Management 348\n• There is a quality balance issue in requirements analysis. In traditional requirements\nanalysis, obtaining high-quality information often means having less data because in-\ndepth interviews and thorough meeting discussions require a lot of time and attention,\nmaking it impossible to cover a wide user base within a limited time. Conversely, to pursue\nquantity, the depth and accuracy of data may be sacrificed, and balancing these two\naspects is always difficult.\n• Additionally, it is difficult to completely avoid the influence of environmental factors\nduring meeting discussions and user interviews. Many practitioners may be constrained\nby social expectations when expressing themselves in public, unwilling to express their\ntrue thoughts candidly, or may change their opinions due to conformity, all of which\nimpact the authenticity and accuracy of requirements analysis.\nHowever, with rapid advancements in information technology, especially in the field of artificial\nintelligence, the rise of LLMs has opened up brand-new possibilities for requirements analysis.\nInnovative technologies represented by ChatGPT have been applied, with ChatGPT’s core\nadvantage lying in its deep understanding and processing capabilities of natural language, giving"],[52797,"Chapter 10 348\nFigure 10.3: Verified credential\n349 Microsoft Entra Verified ID\nBy default, employees can get their Verified ID from the My Account portal. If organizations\ndesire, they can create a custom application for issuing Verified ID. This is explained later in\nthis chapter under the section Custom credentials.\nAdministrators can also specify groups of users who can use My Account to download Verified\nID. These options are available by clicking on View details under the Verified Employee\ncredential as shown in Figure 10.3 and then selecting Issue a credential. Refer to the following\nscreenshot:\nFigure 10.4: Verified credentials settings\nNotice that idx_b108eb81 you can control which users receive a credential and whether you want employees\nto be able to use the My Account portal to download their Verified IDs."],[52268,"Chapter 10 349\nWhen applied to requirements analysis, LLMs help address limitations in several practical ways:\n• Rapid Processing of Large Amounts of Data: LLMs can efficiently process massive text\ndatasets, such as lengthy project files, intensive email correspondence, and detailed\nmeeting minutes. Analyzing this content manually could require significant time and\neffort, while LLMs can accomplish the same tasks quickly, saving vast resources.\n• Deep Learning and Understanding: Leveraging advanced machine learning algorithms\nand extensive training datasets, LLMs can not only identify keywords and phrases in text\nbut also understand their context and underlying meaning, providing more accurate\ninformation extraction and content analysis.\n• Uncovering Implicit Requirements: In traditional requirements analysis, some latent\nbut unexpressed needs can easily be overlooked. LLMs can use contextual reasoning and\npattern recognition to discover these implicit requirements, helping project teams grasp\na more comprehensive picture of needs.\n• Adaptation to Multiple Text Formats: Whether it’s structured reports, unstructured social\nmedia posts, or ad hoc meeting notes, LLMs can comprehend and process different text\nformats and extract valid information from these diverse data sources.\n• Cross-Domain Knowledge Integration: Cross-domain requirements analysis is often\nlimited by the analyst’s knowledge scope. Since LLMs are trained on texts covering a wide\nrange of topics, they possess cross-industry and cross-domain knowledge bases, enabling\nthem to take richer background knowledge into account and provide comprehensive\ninsights during analysis.\n• Continuous Learning and Improvement: With ongoing input of new data, LLMs can\ncontinue learning and adapting to new linguistic environments and requirement changes,\nkeeping their analytical abilities up to date.\n• Unbiased Objective Analysis: Unlike human analysts who may have cognitive biases,\nLLMs can perform unbiased data analysis, objectively distilling information to ensure\nthe accuracy and fairness of requirements analysis.\n• Decision Support: By providing accurate requirements analysis, LLMs can assist managers\nand decision-makers in better understanding market and user needs, optimizing the\ndecision-making process, and thereby improving the success rate and efficiency of projects.\nTaken together, these capabilities show that the value of LLMs is not limited to faster text\nprocessing. Their larger contribution lies in helping teams form a clearer, more complete, and\nmore adaptable understanding of project requirements.\nApplying LLMs in Project Management 350\nThe value proposition of LLMs for requirements analysis\nThe capabilities of LLMs are reflected not only in rapid processing and in-depth analysis of text\ndata, but also in their ability to reason over massive amounts of information through continuous"],[52798,"Chapter 10 350\nEnd user experience\nTo download idx_1ee3488d verifiable credentials from My Account, follow the steps below:\nSign in to https://myaccount.microsoft.com.\nFigure 10.5: Get Verified ID from My Access page\nOn the Home page, click on Get your verified ID. A QR code will be displayed. If you\ndon't have the Microsoft idx_2e74f81c Authenticator app, you will be prompted to download it. If\nyou have it, you can scan the QR code using the app and save the Verified ID in the app\nas shown in the screenshot below."],[52270,"Chapter 10 351\nOnce requirements are understood and agreed upon, the next step is to convert them into tasks,\ntimelines, priorities, and resource decisions that the project team can act on.\nTask planning\nTask planning is also an indispensable aspect of project management, involving key areas such\nas task breakdown, prioritization, time scheduling, and resource allocation. Accurate and\nefficient task planning ensures smooth project workflows and the achievement of objectives. In\ntoday’s era of big data and artificial intelligence, the application of LLMs provides project teams\nwith unprecedented support and assistance, greatly enhancing the intelligence of task planning.\nBy leveraging natural language processing and machine learning technologies, LLMs are able to\ndeeply analyze massive amounts of historical project data to identify best practices and patterns\nof potential failure. Based on the experience of past successful cases, these models can offer\nrecommendations for current projects, such as which tasks should be prioritized, the expected\ntimeframe, and how to rationally allocate resources. Additionally, the models can provide\npersonalized task allocation plans by taking into account the professional expertise and historical\nperformance of individual team members.\nLLMs also possess predictive capabilities for challenges and risks that may be encountered.\nBy analyzing the incidence rate of issues at similar stages in past projects, combined with the\nspecific conditions of the current project, the models can foresee and provide early warnings for\npotential difficulties and bottlenecks, enabling project managers to proactively take measures\nto avoid or mitigate risks.\nAs agile project management methods become more widely adopted, quick iteration and\nadaptability have become increasingly important. As shown in Figure 10.3, the combination of\nLLMs with agile methodologies can further promote team collaboration and improve response\nspeed.\nFigure 10.3: The combination of LLMs and agile methods\nApplying LLMs in Project Management 352\nThe model can analyze the team’s work output and feedback in real time, dynamically adjusting\ntask plans to ensure tasks are always aligned with project objectives and meet the latest user needs.\nIt can optimize workflows in real time based on ongoing task performance, increase transparency,\nand enhance communication and cooperation efficiency among team members.\nLLMs can also provide real-time monitoring of task execution, collect execution data, and analyze\nefficiency bottlenecks to uncover potential systemic issues. Through these insights, the model\nnot only provides guidance to the team during the execution phase but also offers strategic\nimprovement suggestions for future project planning.\nOverall, the application of LLMs in task planning is not just about providing a simple tool, but"],[43599,"Chapter 10 352\nobservability vendor, along with details about the IDP and platform team. They idx_fee422ef pitched a\nplatform that enabled a new and scalable concept called self-service, a new utility idx_3418b6ce for the\nobservability tooling, including the new concept of business process observability, and plans\nfor cost management.\nAfter a difficult series of conversations with senior leadership and several years of work to\nimplement the proposal, the original goals of the project were met and even exceeded. New\nbusiness intelligence, such as feature interaction metrics, created the framework for data-\ndriven product decisions. Company processes and procedures were reshaped in ways that the\nworking group hadn't foreseen, but ACME Financial Services came out of it much more\nefficient and seeing lower overall costs.\nEvolving trust in the observability platform\nIt took ACME Financial Services many years of effort to update the company's observability\npractices and reshape the company around a new way of operating. Putting all the pieces in\nplace was just idx_f26a59f5 the beginning as that is what allowed the company to evolve within the new\nparadigm. It was one thing to enable the use of a new tool and to provide data that was\npreviously unavailable, but quite another for the tools and data to become part of workflows.\nOnce workflows incorporated the new methods, it was yet another task for teams to start\ntrusting the feedback that the observability platform provided. It was not a quick process and\none that could not be rushed. ACME Financial Services cares deeply about its reputation with\ncustomers, and trying to force the company to trust the observability platform was a difficult\ntask. Before any goals or mandates could be established, there would need to be a critical mass\nof teams within the company that had modified their practices and showed tangible benefits.\nThese teams would be examples for others as the whole company transitioned their\nworkflows. Only once those examples and success stories existed could more stringent plans\nbe created.\nThis is not to say that senior leadership and the observability working group took a step back\nonce everything was in place. They aggressively pushed for consumption of the data and use of\nthe observability platform, while also stressing that teams would need to evolve their\npractices. Senior leadership also pushed for OKRs to track company adoption of the\nobservability platform, with the goal of having 100% of the teams across the whole company\nusing it. They also created a KPI to track the accuracy of the suggestions made via the\nplatform's AI agent, and set an initial goal that the suggestions provided by the platform would\nbe correct 80% of the time. Their definition of accuracy was a suggestion from the"],[52800,"Chapter 10 352\nUsing advanced setup\nIf you do not have a custom domain verified in your tenant or if you want more control over\nconfiguring idx_810c18de your DIDs, you can use advanced setup. This gives the administrator full control\nover the configuration process. Using advanced setup, organizations can issue custom\ncredentials that they design from scratch. At a high-level, advanced setup includes the\nfollowing steps:\nConfiguring Azure Key Vault to store the keys for signing and verifying credentials.\nCreating and registering decentralized identifiers.\nLinking your domain to the DID to provide a trusted source for credentials.\nLet's go through them in detail.\nCreating a key vault\nAzure Key Vault is an Azure service to securely store secrets and keys. It also enables access\nmanagement idx_ebe6aefb of secrets and keys. The Verified ID service leverages Azure Key Vault to store\npublic and private keys used to sign and verify credentials.\nKeep the following considerations in mind while creating the key vault:\nYou must have the contributor role on the Azure subscription or resource group where\nthe key vault will be deployed.\nThe permission model on the key vault must be set to Key Vault Access Policy instead\nof Azure role-based access control.\nBy default, the account that created the key vault is the only one that can access it. The Verified\nID service needs to access the key vault. Authenticate the key vault to allow the account used to\nconfigure Verified ID to create and delete keys. Also ensure that the account has permissions to\nsign to enable it to create the domain binding for Verified ID."],[52272,"Chapter 10 353\nFigure 10.4: The scope of communication and collaboration\nIn this context, LLMs, as a powerful artificial intelligence technology, can understand, generate,\nand optimize textual information through natural language processing techniques, thereby\nsignificantly improving the efficiency of information transmission and communication.\nThe importance of communication and collaboration\nCommunication and collaboration are key to successful project management. Effective\ncommunication helps ensure that every team member, stakeholder, and participant fully\nunderstands the project goals, strategies, and current status. This not only promotes the\nachievement of common objectives but also helps build an atmosphere of mutual trust and respect,\nwhich is crucial for team morale and motivation.\nA project team may consist of members with different cultural backgrounds, professional expertise,\nand skill levels. The effectiveness of communication and collaboration directly impacts the internal\nworkflow, decision-making process, and innovative capacity of the team, thereby affecting the\nspeed and quality of the entire project. Clear communication can reduce misunderstandings\nand mistakes, saving time and resources needed to correct errors, while also enhancing synergy\namong team members, enabling them to quickly adapt to changes and solve problems together.\nApplying LLMs in Project Management 354\nHowever, in practice, project management often faces numerous challenges in communication\nand collaboration. First, there may be barriers to information transmission; for example, complex\ntechnical language or industry jargon may make it difficult for non-experts to understand. Second,\nvirtual team members may lack face-to-face interactions, which could lead to information\ndistortion or a lack of emotional expression. Additionally, as the project scale increases and the\nnumber of people involved grows, communication between different levels and departments\nbecomes more complex. Departments or small groups may form information silos, disagreements\nor lack of consensus may arise among team members, and the needs of stakeholders may change\nfrequently—any of these can hinder the communication process and impact the smooth progress\nof a project. As Belbin pointed out in the 2010 research paper Team Roles at Work (Butterworth-\nHeinemann), effective internal communication and collaboration within the team is one of the\nkey factors in ensuring project success.\nLLMs as a solution to communication and collaboration\nchallenges\nFacing such challenges, LLMs provide new solutions. Through natural language processing\ntechnology, LLMs can interpret the complexities of human language, transform technical or\nspecialized information into more understandable forms, and even automatically generate"],[52803,"Chapter 10 354\nClick on Select keys to select the Azure Key Vault created earlier and click Save as\nshown in the following screenshot.\nFigure 10.8: Verified ID advanced setup\nBehind the scenes, the Verified ID service provisions a service principal called Verifiable\nCredentials Service Request. This service principal represents the Verified ID service in your\ntenant and is used to handle issuance and verification requests for verifiable credentials. You\ncan navigate to Entra ID | Enterprise Applications to ensure that the service principal has\nbeen created.\nRegistering an application\nWhile using idx_f744e7f0 advanced setup, the administrator will need to register an app to act as the issuer\nidentity (DID owner). The actual distribution of credentials is performed by a separate issuing\napplication that you build and host elsewhere. Follow the steps below to register an app in\nEntra ID.\nSign in to https://entra.microsoft.com as Application Administrator.\nNavigate to Entra ID | App registrations | New registration.\nEnter a display name for the app.\nUnder Supported account types, select Accounts in this organizational directory\nonly (Default directory only - Single tenant). Click on Register to create the\napplication.\nThis domain should not be a redirect or the DID domain cannot be linked.\nEnsure that you use HTTPS for the domain. For example, https://\ndid.contoso.com.\nTip"],[43602,"Chapter 10 354\nother day, to every week, and so on. Eventually, the reviews became so infrequent as to almost\nnot happen at all.\nThe developers' path to trust\nUnlike the SREs, the application developers were much slower to adopt the new observability\nplatform. Engineers had a reputation for adopting new tools rather quickly when it made idx_8668add9 their\nlives better, but in this instance, they didn't have a strong foundation in observability data,\nwhich meant they had a difficult time understanding how it would benefit them. They were\nalso being asked to incorporate a new paradigm, observability-driven development, into their\ndesign process, and they needed to understand what that meant in order to make the\nrequested changes.\nSimilar to the SREs, the application developers intended to start by heavily scrutinizing the\nsuggestions that came from the observability platform. This ended up being a little chaotic at\nfirst, as some developers scrutinized the changes while others blindly accepted them without\nunderstanding anything that was being done. Once an application team addressed that\ninconsistency and got everyone to scrutinize the suggestions, they started to make progress.\nThe application developers reviewed suggestions from the observability platform for\ncorrectness, as well as to understand the root cause that triggered the suggestion. The\napplication developers knew that they were going to need to incorporate observability-driven\ndevelopment into their workflow, so they used the tool suggestions as a place to start learning\nabout what kind of data is needed and what kind of problems can be identified.\nOnce a suggestion from the observability platform was accepted, it was heavily tested in\ndevelopment and preproduction environments before being deployed into production. This\nmeant creating testing scenarios that could create the issue that was being fixed and observing\nthat the change did in fact fix the problem. As the suggestions from the observability platform\ncontinued to help alleviate production issues, the application developers started to trust them\nmore and also started to gain an understanding of how to design their applications to be\nobserved. This knowledge was critical for the application developers to successfully implement\nobservability-driven development.\nWith a basic understanding of the kinds of data that can prove useful to the observability\nplatform, the application developers began to change their design process to make it easier to\nobserve and understand the inner workings of their applications. At first, this was a slow\nprocess, which is usual when implementing a new concept. As their experience grew and they\nincorporated more observable designs, the quality and type of suggestions received by the\nobservability platform also improved. This increased the application developers' confidence in"],[52274,"Chapter 10 355\nIn daily workflows, the acquisition, and processing of information are often crucial to a project’s\nsuccess, and LLMs play an essential role in this regard. Through intelligent question-and-\nanswer systems, team members no longer need to spend large amounts of time filtering and\nverifying information, as they can directly ask the LLM questions and quickly receive precise and\nauthoritative answers. This instant Q&A functionality greatly alleviates the pressure of knowledge\nretrieval, allowing team members to focus more on decision-making and innovation. Whether\nit’s explaining technical terms, analyzing industry trends, or solving complex problems, LLMs\ncan provide answers, effectively reducing misunderstandings, and communication barriers, and\naccelerating decision-making processes.\nLLMs also excel at summarization and extraction. They possess the ability to synthesize scattered\ninformation into summaries and reports, which is a boon in fast-paced work environments. Team\nmembers can input research findings, market data, or project updates into the model, which will\norganize this information and produce clear, easily understandable summaries that help the\nteam better grasp the overall picture and ensure all members are on the same informational page.\nRecommendation functionality is also an indispensable part of LLMs. Based on provided data and\nprerequisites, they can forecast possible outcomes and offer advice and solutions, pointing the\nteam in the right direction. This kind of data-driven intelligent recommendation not only enhances\nthe team’s ability to tackle complex issues but also increases work flexibility and creativity.\nLLMs act as information integrators and knowledge sharers, making knowledge exchange within\nthe team more convenient. Each member’s experiences, insights, and research findings can be\naggregated and compared by the LLM, fostering knowledge accumulation and the collision of\ninnovative ideas. On the level of team collaboration, this means a broader shared vision and a\nmore solid foundation for decision-making.\nLLMs deepen the team’s communication methods, optimize information processing flows, and\nenhance decision quality. In this era of information explosion, making use of these advantages\nof LLMs can undoubtedly enable more efficient team collaboration, faster growth, and more\ncreative outcomes.\nIntegrating and managing project knowledge\nWith their advanced natural language processing capabilities, LLMs have become powerful\nplatforms for knowledge integration and management. Team members can input ideas,\nprofessional insights, research data, and practical experience into the system. The language model\nthen uses complex algorithms to analyze, compare, and synthesize these various inputs, thereby\ngenerating conclusions or recommendations that thoroughly consider all available information."],[52810,"Chapter 10 356\nHost the did ‑ configuration.json file on your web server under https://\nwww.domain.com/.well-known/did-configuration.json.\nOnce the file is publicly available, you can verify it by clicking on Refresh verification\nstatus. The portal verifies that the did ‑ configuration.json file is reachable over the\ninternet and valid.\nVerifying DID domain ownership is an important step as it establishes the relationship\nbetween a DID and a domain. This solves the trust problem by allowing an entity like a mobile\nwallet or the Microsoft Authenticator app to validate the relationship between a DID and a\ndomain.\nMicrosoft Entra Verified ID uses the well-known DID configuration specification to create a\nlink between DIDs and domains. The verifiable credentials service links the DID and domains.\nIt includes the domain information that was provided in the DID to generate the well-known\nconfiguration file.\nAdvanced setup supports building real, custom, enterprise-grade verifiable credentials tailored\nto your organization's identity, governance, and automation needs.\nCustom credentials\nMicrosoft Entra Verified ID can issue any verifiable credential you define, from employment\nand education idx_7d986026 credentials to KYC, age, membership, idx_75a6693c and entitlement credentials. Plus, it\nsupports consuming pre-built credentials from the Verified ID Network.\nLet's understand how you can issue and verify custom credentials.\nIssuing custom credentials\nOnce the idx_d738826c Verified ID setup is complete, you will be able to create custom credentials using the\nfollowing steps:\nSign in to https://entra.microsoft.com as Authentication Policy Administrator.\nNavigate to Verified ID | Credentials. In the right pane click on Create credential.\nOn the Create credential page, select Custom credential and click Next.\nEnter a name in the Credential name box. This name is used to identify the credential.\nEnter the Display definition and Rules definition in JSON format and click Create to\ncreate the credential."],[43605,"Chapter 10 356\nOnce the team trusts the output from the AI, they should take on the task of automating the\napplication idx_c662dd08 of the suggestions. By the end of this phase, no one should be involved in reviewing\nor approving suggestions from the AI for the defined task.\nThe verify phase\nIn this phase, human involvement for the specified task should start decreasing. Suggestions\nfrom the idx_0da78d17 AI are being accepted and applied without human intervention, so human actions\nrevolve around monitoring and verifying the actions performed by the AI.\nA key difference between the trust phase and the verify phase is that in the trust phase, each\nsuggestion is evaluated as it comes in, whereas in the verify phase, suggestions are evaluated\nafter they have been applied, as part of a regularly scheduled review. Observability tools take\nover the task of monitoring whether the AI behaves as expected.\nThe frequency of the scheduled reviews would depend on the number of suggestions over a\nperiod and the criticality of the task being performed. The team should pick a cadence that\nthey find acceptable and start there. As trust in the suggestions is reinforced through the\nreview process agreeing with the implemented suggestions from the AI, the time between\nreviews can increase.\nBy the end of this phase, there should be little to no verification review processes required for\nchanges related to the specific task. The team is then ready to choose a new task and perform\nthe process again from the beginning.\nHow trust changed the company\nIt was one thing to tell teams in ACME Financial Services to do something, but quite another\nfor them to embrace it. In order to achieve the maximum benefit from ACME Observability, the\nobservability working group knew that teams would need to trust the suggestions provided\nand eventually turn over responsibility for some things to the tool. Once the teams allowed the\ntooling to be responsible for tasks in its domain, they would have more time to work on more\nproductive work for the company.\nHow SREs were impacted by trusting the tool\nOnce the idx_3d6aee6c SREs reached a point where they trusted the suggestions provided by ACME\nObservability and allowed them to be implemented without review, their work started to\nchange rather drastically. All cluster and application scaling was handled by the observability\nplatform. Incidents from usage spikes all but completely disappeared. Incidents caused by an\napplication deployment were automatically addressed by rolling back to a previous version.\n357 ACME Financial Services: How Will the AI Future Shape Our Company?\nIncidents as a idx_df5801a9 whole dropped drastically. This benefited the SREs in two key ways:\nThe SREs didn't idx_3366e0ba have to spend as much time performing Root Cause Analyses (RCAs)."],[52276,"Chapter 10 357\nFigure 10.5: The role of screen recording software in information sharing\nScreen recording offers multiple advantages:\n• It allows team members who are unable to attend real-time meetings to review the content\nat their convenience, ensuring seamless information transfer\n• The video records every detail of the discussion, including whiteboard sketches, slideshows,\nor the process of making important decisions, making the logic behind complex ideas\nand decisions much clearer\n• By watching and reviewing these recordings, team members can quickly grasp the main\ncontext and key problems addressed in the meetings, fostering deeper learning and\nknowledge accumulation\nLLMs provide value-added services for screen recordings. AI technology can automatically\nanalyze speech, text, and visual information in screen recordings and extract the core points and\naction items from meetings. This automatic summarization not only improves the efficiency of\ninformation recording but also enhances the team’s understanding and absorption of meeting\ncontent. Members do not need to manually take notes or worry about missing key information;\nAI has already prepared concise and clear summaries for them.\nApplying LLMs in Project Management 358\nAI’s automatic summarization technology can also be adjusted and optimized based on team\nfeedback and needs, to ensure that the information provided is both accurate and targeted. By\ncontinuously learning and adapting to the team’s communication patterns, AI can better capture\nand highlight the topics that team members care about most.\nIn the end, this combination of screen recording and AI automatic summarization not only greatly\nimproves the team’s ability to synchronize information internally and make quick decisions, but\nalso helps build and maintain a knowledge asset base. By organizing and indexing the key points\nand decision logic of meeting content, the team can effectively avoid redundant work and quickly\nretrieve historical decisions and discussion results when needed. The entire process significantly\nenhances the organization and utilization efficiency of knowledge assets, bringing long-term\nstrategic value to the team.\nConnecting communication platforms and project tools\nBy using LLMs as communication and collaboration platforms, team members can achieve rapid\nintegration and sharing of information in a highly interactive and intelligent environment. Such\nplatforms, by providing instant communication tools and file-sharing functions, allow team\nmembers to easily communicate and collaborate without being limited by geographic locations.\nOn this unified communication and collaboration platform, team members can upload and\ndownload important project files, jointly edit documents, and update project progress in real\ntime. In addition, LLMs can understand and process natural language queries, making searching"],[43608,"Chapter 10 358\nhad been found in dependent packages. Updating dependencies to mitigate vulnerabilities was\nalways a time-consuming task that the application developers completely offloaded to the\nobservability platform.\nWith fewer idx_86eea1e1 bugs and performance issues, the application developers were able to concentrate\non creating value for the company. They were able to implement new features much faster\nbecause they didn't have to spend significant portions of their time diagnosing and\ndetermining fixes for production issues or working through dependency updates. They were\nalso able to work on long-desired application redesigns to bring their applications in line with\nthe company's various standards, such as authentication and trust, visual design, and log\nformatting. This resulted in a more uniform look and feel and consistent function for the\ncompany's customers.\nBusiness process observability bears fruit\nImplementing business process observability provided insight into the workings of the\ncompany's idx_ddef1c42 applications and their interactions that the company never had before. They found\nmany failures in application interactions that led to less than expected resiliency, redundant\nactions, and bottlenecks. Some insights that they gained after implementing business process\nobservability included the following:\nOpening a new account involved multiple applications across several business areas.\nEach performed its own authorization verification, and if any part of the process failed\nor determined that authorization was not granted, then an error bubbled up and left\nthe process in an unknown state. Reattempting the creation wasn't guaranteed to\nsucceed.\nApplications didn't share a trust model. Each application would verify authentication\nand authorization independently, which created a bottleneck and resiliency concern in\ntheir authentication system. This undermined the applications' design to ensure\nresiliency through scaling their Pods.\nThey found many examples of a request ping-ponging across applications, for example,\nA->B->C->A->B->D, because an application later in the request pipeline needed data\nfrom an application earlier in the pipeline in order to make a decision.\nWith these kinds of insights, ACME Financial Services was able to improve their process flow\nacross most of their business operations. They ensured that no point in a process was a\nbottleneck or limited the resiliency of their applications.\nIncident idx_672c8964 response became much more efficient. Instead of bringing everyone into a call to\ndiagnose a problem, the incident response teams now knows exactly which applications are\n•\n•\n•\n359 ACME Financial Services: How Will the AI Future Shape Our Company?\ninvolved in a process. This allows efficient troubleshooting, as well as avoiding burnout among"],[52813,"Chapter 10 358\nOnce the idx_3cefb609 credential is created, the following pieces of information are required to configure the\napplication that is used to verify the credential:\nAuthority: This is the decentralized identifier.\nManifest URL: This is the URL that the Authenticator app evaluates before showing\nthe verifiable credentials issuance requirements to the user.\nTenant ID: This idx_c0454bfc is the Globally Unique Identifier (GUID) in the manifest URL as\nshown in the box below.\nThis information is available by clicking on Issue a credential on the custom credential page\nas shown in the following screenshot.\nFigure 10.10: Capture information\n•\n•\n•\n359 Microsoft Entra Verified ID\nOnce you idx_08a8cb5f have this information, add a client secret to the verifiable credentials app created in\nthe Registering an application section. The following are the steps:\nNavigate to Entra ID | App registrations | All applications and select the application\ncreated earlier. Copy the Application (client) ID and save it.\nClick on Certificates & secrets | New client secret.\nEnter a description and expiry and click Add. Record the secret's value. This is the only\ntime the secret's value is displayed. Make sure you copy it before navigating away from\nthis screen.\nAt this point you must have the authority URL, manifest URL, tenant id, application (client) ID\nand client secret to configure the application that issues credentials.\nCreating the issuing app\nThe application idx_7ad585d5 that issues verifiable credentials is not the app registration created during\nadvanced setup. It is your own backend application, running on Azure, on-prem, AWS, or GCP.\nFollowing are the high-level steps to configure this application:\nCreate an app registration in Microsoft Entra for the issuing application and assign a\nclient secret. This application must have the VerifiableCredential.Create.All API\npermission. Grant admin consent to the app.\nThe application must obtain an access token from Entra ID using the OAuth 2.0 client\ncredentials flow. Use the following information to configure the app:\nToken endpoint: https://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenantId}/oauth2/\nv2.0/token\nScope: https://verifiedid.did.msidentity.com/.default\nThis token is required to call the Issuance API.\nCall the Issuance API to create an issuance request. Your app must send the following\nPOST request to the Issuance API:\nPOST https://verifiedid.did.msidentity.com/v1.0/verifiableCredentials/\ncreateIssuanceRequest"],[52278,"Chapter 10 359\nLLMs improve communication efficiency and resource\nintegration capability\nBy integrating LLMs into team communication mechanisms, we can create an efficient and highly\ninteractive work environment. This advancement allows information and screen recordings to\nbe transmitted and shared in real time, greatly enhancing team members’ communication and\ncomprehension abilities. On a unified and intelligent platform, team members can easily engage in\nface-to-face direct communication while also seamlessly exchanging and integrating files and data.\nA LLM-based platform serves not only as a communication tool but also acts as a collaboration\nassistant. The analytical and suggestion features of LLMs help team members filter out the most\nrelevant content from massive amounts of information, provide context-based operational\nrecommendations, and identify potential communication barriers. Over time, the model\ncontinuously learns from the team’s communication patterns and preferences, enabling it to\noffer more precise personalized support and optimize workflows.\nThe platform’s intelligent integration ability enables seamless connection with a variety of tools\nand applications, realizing unified management of project management software, document\neditors, instant messaging services, and other resources, thereby simplifying workflows. Team\nmembers can create and customize automated tasks as needed, unleashing creativity and allowing\nthem to focus on more valuable work.\nA communication platform integrated with LLMs will transform traditional ways of working.\nThrough its efficient information processing capabilities and interactive communication features,\nit effectively improves the quality of team collaboration and work efficiency. As the team becomes\nincreasingly reliant on this system, workflows become more streamlined, project delivery speed\naccelerates, and the organization ultimately gains a clear competitive advantage.\nImproving communication efficiency between different roles\nThe application of LLMs is not limited to providing information query services; it can also facilitate\nmore efficient communication and team collaboration in various work environments. For example,\nwhen integrated into a company’s internal communication platform or workflows, LLMs can\ninstantly answer professional questions raised by team members, whether related to specific\nproject details, market analysis, technical challenges, or administrative management queries.\nAdditionally, the model can understand and process natural language, recognizing industry\nterminology and abbreviations used by staff in communication. Therefore, when team members\nare not familiar with other departments’ work, LLMs can serve as a knowledge bridge, helping to\nexplain and translate technical terms and ensuring smooth cross-departmental communication."]]